      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR BlEttß) SA I'PREY</head>
        <p>McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR BlEttß) SA I'PREY</p>
        <p>PERMANENTLY IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Permanent is derived from two Latin words, per through manerc, to continue, and when applied to, say, work or a cure, means, to put it simply, that it "goes on," and is not merely temporary. A cure such as this of Mrs. Daley, which has lasted over eighteen months is an example of what is meant by a permanent cure. It was over eighteen months after her cure that Mrs.H. Daley, 1, Booth-street, Sankey, Warrington, said :—" was thoroughly cured by Doan's pills, and I've felt grand ever since. They cured me after I had been suffering for five years. I was swollen all over my body, also under my eyes, which used to water; I had head- aches and dizziness, and always felt tired ; backache used to be terrible. I always kept about, though it was a hard matter for me to do so; I used three boxes of Doan's pills for my cure." The following are the particulars Mrs. Daley gave us just after she was cured :—" don't know what I should have done if I hadn't tried Doan's backache kidney pills for they relieved me wonderfully after I had been suffer- ing for years. lam so pleased with the amount of good the pills have done me that I have no objection to the facts of my case being published, so that others may hear of the medicine. "On and off for years I was a martyr to kidney trouble ; I had dull, gnawing pains round my loins, and sometimes the pains were so bad and I was so weak that it seemed as if my back was coming in two. It was a hard matter indeed for me to get straight if I did any bending. "After trying all kinds of medicines without success, I was advised to use Doan's pills. I have taken nearly two boxes of these, and they have done me the world of good ; the terrible pains have gone now. I have spoken to many people about Doan's pills. (Signed) H. Daley." If you neglect the Kidneys, you will suffer from Dropsy, or Stone, or Gravel, or Urinary Troubles, or Rheumatism, or one of the many other terrible results of kidney disease. Doan's pills act directly and effec- tively on the kidneys, curing them when diseased and strengthening them when weak. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Do not forget to use the name, DOAN'S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King at Marienbad.—The annual "cure " at Aix Homburg, or Marienbad, is looked upon as one of the necessities of fashionable life. The King always takes his " cure " seriously, but his Majesty's visits have done much to popularise Marienbad, and to attract a great many visitors who go there simply to meet their friends and display their new frocks and millinery. It is this class of people who pay so little respect to the King's desire for privacy, during his stay at Marienbad. As a rule, foreign nationalities allow their royalties to come and go with more freedom than is the British custom. It is certain that neither our King or Queen could appear on the esplanade of any of our watering places, without being mobbed or snap-shotted, and it is the British and American visitors who are the worst offenders in spying on the King at Marienbad. The Souvenir Craze.—" Souvenir hunting " is a disease more prevalent in America than in this country, but the managers of many of the most fashionable hotels in London are complaining of the depredations of visitors who "annex" little articles or ornaments of perhaps no great value, and carry them off as souvenirs. At some establishments silver spoons, sugar tongues, and cruet fittings have disappeared to such extent that the managers have gone to the length of providing special" souvenir "articles, but these of course are not wanted. In most cases the articles pre not taken for their value, as the worst offenders are 'said to be those to whom money is absolutely no object. They simply have the bump of acquisitiveness very large, and appear to be unable to resist the opportunity to secretly " attach " some addition to the "souvenir "collection. The Farmhouse Holiday.—The holiday season has not been over and above summery. Those who have indulged in the rural holiday have perhaps been better off than those who chose the seaside, and the develop- ment of the farmhouse holiday is one of the features of recent years. Nearly all the railways issue books con- taining particulars of farmhouse accommodation in the districts they traverse. This has done much to popularise the rural holiday and nothing is more suitable than farmhouse fare for those with a taste for the "simple life." A common recreation of such holidays is to join in operations on the farm,whether haymaking, harvesting, or hop-picking, as the case may he, but it is easy to find farms where boating, golfing, fishing, shooting, are almost obtainable. One can adopt one's wardrobe to all kinds of weather in the country, more easily than at the seaside, but on every hand we hear lamentations over our lost summer. Perhaps it is owing to the comet. Whenever the various comets appeared in the past, it was the custom to attribute all misfortunes, great and small, to their mysterious influ- ence. This year, however, the more we grumbled at the weather, the worse it got, and the least it can do is to make amends by giving us a fine September. The Deceased Wife's Sister.—For close upon seventy- five years, marriage with the deceased wife's sister has been the subject of heated controversy, and it would be too much to hope that the strife it has occasioned will altogether cease, now that the Bill legalising such marriages has been passed. It is a social reform which may affect any family at one time, and it would be strange if a matter of such general interest were free from controversial views, but two broad facts stand out, viz., that a great majority of the clergy are opposed to such marriages, whilst there is little doubt that public opinion on the whole is in favour of their legalisation, as they have long been legal in the Colonies. Friction is therefore pretty sure to arise, although under the Bill no clergy of the Church of England is compelled to solemnize such a marriage and may refuse to lend his church for this purpose. The Altered Law.—The new statute relating to marriage with a deceased wife's sister, legalises all such marriages past and future, unless in the past one or the other of the parties has subsequently married another person legally. Inheritance to a title or property, is not affected by such a marriage in the past, being now legalised. Complications which it was thought the legalising of such past marriages would involve, are thus avoided. The Bill does not remove the prohibition against a widow marrying her brother-in-law. The con- tention that the majority of women are against the law, has little evidence to justify it. No doubt to many a woman the idea that her husband may one day marry her sister, is distasteful, and the Bishop of Hereford has said that" The Bill might almost be described as a measure for slamming the door in the face of the maiden aunt." It may have such possibilities in some cases, but these will be few and far between. 'he " maiden aunt " is not as a rule likely to be less welcomed in the home of her relatives, whilst the great argument in favour of the altered law, especially as it affects the working classes, is, that she is likely to make a better step-mother than the ordinary run of women in such positions. Laundresses and the Weather.—The passing summer has not proved such a very disastrous one to the laundress as might be imagined, for whereas in fine dry seasons we are able with care to don washing frocks, And underskirts, several times, once wearing this year has—generally speaking—reduced them to sashed and draggled-looking rags. So the laundresses have not done so very -badly after all, as despite the lack of suitable weather,vast numbers of girls and women have chosen to shiver in cool linens, and cottons, and airy- fairy muslin robes. Those among us who prefer to bask rather than shiver will welcome the coming of autumn, when we can wear our warm tweeds and friezes without an irritating sense of being defrauded out of our rights in the matter of a summer, New Colours.—Very bright and very startling shades of tan and brown, which can only prove most unbecom- ing to the average complexion' are appearing. This is all the more to be regretted as there are so many exquisite shades of these two colours available in all materials. Green, too, is promised a great vogue in the near future, and divers tones from the palest blue-green to dark emerall are to be found in shopland. It is doubtless quite safe te say that some shade of green suits every women, still, the task of selecting it is not always an easy one, and settling upon the wrong tone of green is indeed a tragedy. Shades intended for day wear should be chosen in strong daylight, and those for evening wear by artificial light, and the women who has to hecontent with very few frocks should endeavour to fix upon tones—though they are very few and far between—that are wearable alike by night and day. Spotless Neckwear.—Dainty collars, collar bands, nd neckwear generally, are of the utmost importance o the gentlewoman, and the dye that frequently rubs off the linings of dark fur collars, and dark coat collars is often responsible for much aggravation of spirit. An excellent plan is to tack a neat pleating of lace in every coat collar, and piece of fur intended for the neck, just where it is likely to rub against the blouse, keeping the pleating well out of sight. When the lace is soiled it can be removed, washed and replaced, a procedure that will be found to greatly help us in preserving the daintiness of our neckwear. Nuts and Fruit.—With our love for things out of season in the millinery world, it is a little surprising to note the lavish use the best shops are making of nuts, bunches of nuts of every description, iVith appropriate foliage, being seen upon so many of the latest dressy afternoon models. Fruit too, is coming to the fore again, grapes leading. A year ago we grew weary of this particularly heavy-looking fruit, the huge bunches giving an unbecoming, top-heavy appearance to the bats in which they were placed. Apples, pears and plums, Invariably possess the same fault, indeed with the .exception of cherries, and the smaller berries, fruit almost defies artistic handling.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>KIDNEY is a deceptive disease—thousands TROUBLE , have it and don't know it. If you ant quick results you can make no mistake bYttsing Dr.Kilmer's Swamp-Root, the great kidney remedy. At chemists in is. 11d. and 2s. 3d. sizes Sample bottle by post free, also pamphlet telling you . how to find out if you have kidney trouble Dr. Kilmer Co„ Dept, 329 TeinpleCAMbers, London. E .C. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>AUTUMN MILLINERY</p>
        <p>Among the new millinery. models, says the Tribune, are several large shapes which recall the picture hats worn by the Empress Eugenie in the days when Winterhalter took delight in depicting her rare beauty. These hats droop in front and at the back, and are worn thrown off the head and quite flat. Under the brim there is a quaint quilling of lace, and round the crown clouds of gossamer or of pleated tulle, with a careless cluster of choice flowers at one side. This drooping.shape will probably take the place now occupied by the cloche when winter again sets in, and it would look lovely in fine felt or silk beaver. Just at the present moment some of the models which are appearing in this pic- turesque shape are trimmed lavishly with flowers, after the manner of the cloches above described, or with immensely large wings which jut out beyond the brim at either side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PRETTY DRESS IN ANY LIGHT MATERIAL.</head>
        <p>PRETTY DRESS IN ANY LIGHT MATERIAL.</p>
        <p>The becoming dress . shewn in our sketch is suitable alike for linen, cotton, or fine woollen material. The original was made of pale biscuit-coloured linen, and looked very charm- ing and simple trimmed with pale blue linen. The skirt was a plain, well-fitting shape, with inverted pleats behind, and was trimmed with a band of pale blue linen above the hem. The blouse-bodice had a deep rounded yoke of the blue linen, bordered with a narrow strap of the</p>
        <p>biscuit-coloured linen. It opened rather low at the neck to shew the little vest of tucked lawn tnd lace. The fulness of the blouse was ar- ranged in deep tucks, and fastened with in- visible hooks and eyes down the centre front. The large flat buttons that adorn the front of the blouse were covered with the blue linen, and laced across with a fine blue silk cord. BUSH LIFE IN AVSTEALIA.</p>
        <p>An article in the Queen ,on "Bush Life in Australia from a Woman's Point of View" pre- ;ents a most unattractive picture of incessant toil tnd drudgery, with no intellectual alleviations. Ilhe writer, an Australian woman, born and Dred in the bush, having noticed in English and L'olonial newspapers tentative efforts to induce .ducated Englishwomen to go to Australia in warch of employment, writes of her own experi- trice with the object of enlightening those who, ititer reading tales of life on a flourishing sheep ›r cattle station, may imagine them to be ypical of bush life. The few rich squatters .vho have homes of comfort and luxury ire, she says, no more typical of bush ife than Arundel Castle is of English 2ountry life. The Australian bush is a. DISCO of which even Australian women fight shy. There is a surplus of women in the towns, and it is only in the bush that they are needed, and for this purpose only a strong farm-labouring kipe. that could follow the plough, is suitable.</p>
