      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>YELLOW DIAMONDS.</head>
        <p>YELLOW DIAMONDS.</p>
        <p>There seems lately to be a. perfect furore for :he yellow or so-called "Cape" diamond, says the Onlooker, and those which Mr. Sigismund Neumann has brought over from one of his re- :cnt trips from South Africa as a present to his wife are said by experts to rival in size and acauty any stone yet found in the famous mines pf Golconda. and to be correspondingly valu- able. The largest of these stones has been set or Mrs. Neumann in a tiara, and is removable st will to wear as a brooch or pendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SMART AUTUMN COAT.</head>
        <p>SMART AUTUMN COAT.</p>
        <p>The subject of our sketch is a simple but smart little coat suitable for every-day wear iuring the coming autumn. This little coat may be mpst successfully realised in either cloth )r covert-coating In the former case biscuit, pale grey, and black are the most suitable shades For the purpose; while in the latter the paler ihades have a better effect than the darker. The coat is cut to define the figure everywhere except ,just in front, where it is semi-itting. At</p>
        <p>the back the centre seam is opened almost to the waist and finished with a narrow stitched flap; the side and front seams are similarly treated, the openings, however, being shorter, and without the stitched flap. The sleeves aro of the plain coat shape, and are of full length, being finished at the wrist with a line of machining and a couple of buttons. A smart collar of velvet, strapped at the edge with cloth, adds the finishing-touch to this useful and be- coming garment.</p>
