      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BRILLIANT IRISHWOMAN.</head>
        <p>A BRILLIANT IRISHWOMAN.</p>
        <p>Lady Gregory, who has just discovered in an old pocket-book and published two most inte- resting letters about Napoleon, is a wonderfully clever and picturesque old lady, says M.A.P. A daughter of the great Galway family of Persse, she married as his second wife Sir William Gregory, who was a notable figure in O'Con- nell's day and lived to be Governor of Ceylon. Some years ago his widow published " Mr. Gregory's Letter-Box," a book of absorbing in- terest, containing the cream of her husband's correspondence when he was Under-Secretary for Ireland. On her own account, Lady Gregory is a playwright, and has borne a notable part in the remarkable revival of the Irish national drama. She has one son, Mr. W. R. Gregory, who is an artist.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE. STUFFED STEAL—Take lilb. of well-hung steak, cut in one piece, and three-quarters of an inch thick. Lay it on a table, and spread upon it a thin layer of good veal stuffing. Ball it round, tie it with twine to keep it in shape, and fasten the ends securely. Dissolve in a stewpan a lump of butter, put in the steak, and turn it about until it is finely browned all over, then pour over it nearly a pint of beef stock, which may be made with beef extract, cover the same- :um closet.. and let the steak stew wend, until teader, then lay the meat on a hot dish ano re- move the twine. Thicken the gravy, pour it over the beer, and serve very hot. Great care masa be taken that the meat is stewed very slowly, lec it will be tough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>are amusing, my man,:' tie said;. "but you've only four minutes left." Oatway glared at him. "Oh, ain't I? Then I've got to be quick, 'aren't I? You're Lord Lynborough's nervy, ain't you ? " Talbot nodded, his eyes still on the watch. "Quite right; I am." "And you're his heir, and the net noble Earl, cos he 'aren't married, and ain't got no son ? " said Oatway. "Quite right again," assented Talbot. "Your time is growing shorter--" "Then you're mistook," snarled Oatway, leaning forward, his hands on his knees, his cigarette in the, corner of his thick lips. "The noble Earl was married, and he has got a son! Talbot rose with a cold sneer. "I suppose you have been on the stage my man 2" he said, contemptuously. "If so, 7I can understand your failure. Here is a sovereign for you—for yo'- hand." He glanced at the bandage. 'Better the neighbourhood as quickly as possible--" Oatway caught the coin and thrust it into his pocket. "The first o' many," he said, con- fidently. "You think I'm lying, pitchin' a tale ? Right ho! But I can prove it, prove it to you and everybody else's satisfaction. I knew the Earl's wife. I know his son—his lawful son and heir."</p>
        <p>"Then I'll advise you to produce him," retorted Talbot, with a sneer. "I've listened long enough to your tipsy ravings--" "Produce him ? " echoed Oatway, then he fell silent and seemed to be listening. The next moment he sprang up and dragged the exquisite Mr. Talbot behind a tree. - "Produce him, you says!" he whispered hoarsely in his ears. "It's easy enough to do that. Look there!"</p>
        <p>Some workmen were coming along the road, and in their midst was Ralph, his coat on his arm, his handsome, tanned face cheerful and smiling. "Look there ! " hissed oa,tway. "By Heaven, that's him I"</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Helpless Mother.</head>
        <p>A Helpless Mother.</p>
        <p>Her Life a Bitter Struggle against Persistent Weakness Restored to Sound Health by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>One of the most lamentable instances of impoverished blood is presented in the case of Mrs. M. A. Suckling, of 12, VVitherfield-road, Haverhill, Suffolk, whose message is important to her sex generally, but especi- ally to those on whom rest the responsibilities of motherhood. To a reporter of the" Suffolk and Essex Free Press " Mrs. Suckling said : "For a long time after the birth of -- youngest child, I remained exceedingly -*whited. I was racked with cruel</p>
