      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ENGLISH V. IRISH.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH V. IRISH.</p>
        <p>\ --- Sunday Night Scene at Stretton.</p>
        <p>The sequel to a disgraceful Sunday night scene at Stretton was heard at the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, before Messrs. H. Nield and F. A. Frost, when three Irishmen. named Martin Kann, Martin Cos grave, and Patrick Cosgrave, and two Englishmen, named Thomas Smith and Herbert Smith, were charged with having been 6,milty of disorderly behaviour on Sunday night. All pleaded guilty except Thomas Smith, who pleaded guilty in self-defence. Acting Sergeant Kennerley, stationed at Stretton, said that at 9.30 on Sunday night he was on duty in Stretton village, when he saw about twenty Irishmen formed up in a row between the Cat and Lion Inn and the Vicarage, shouting and yelling. In a few minutes about fifteen Englishmen came up, and a regular row began. Witness and Constable Dutton went amongst them, and the men ran in all directions. The officers followed them who went towards Appleton Church. When they got the other side of Birk Crag they faced round on the officers, who made a dash and caught the two Cosgraves. They were handcuffed together, and Dutton took charge of them, whilst witness caught Kenn in Mr. Winstanley's yard with a belt (produced) wrapped round his hand. The Englishmen had knocked all the windows out of the shanty in Winstanley's yard. There sticks, fire-irons, and stones thrown, and the men themselves had been badly knocked about. It was a disgraceful scene, and there was a lot of blood lying about the road.</p>
        <p>Constable Dutton corroborated, and said Thomas Smith took a leading part in the affair. The Irishmen made "a great howling noise," and showers of stones were flying about. Defendants were bound over in their own recogni- zances to keep the peace for six months. Thomas Smith and the Cosgraves were then charged with having been drunk and disorderly at the same time and place. Acting-Sergeant Kennerley said all three were drunk. When the gang came to a standstill, the Cosgraves said "Come on, the constabulary, we'll let them have it." Smith was also acting like a madman. Constable Dutton corroborated, and defendants were fined 2s. 6d. and costs each.</p>
        <p>A tramp named Charles Carr was lying on Wednesday on a slag hill connected with Calder blast furnaces, Coatbridge, shen a train of hot slag boulder was tipped over him, and burned him to death.</p>
      </div>
