      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HOUSE ON THE HILL.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE ON THE HILL.</p>
        <p>BY KATHARINE S. MACQUOID, author of "His Heart's Desire," "A Ward of the King," "Miss Eyon of Eyneourt," "His Last Card," "A Little Vagabond," Sze.</p>
        <p>'Outside the air was full of raw mist, though in it was that strange mildness so frequent in November; this year, however, St. Martin's nummer had come without the sunshine we have learnt to expect with it.</p>
        <p>Michael Moss, tall, sunburnt, and healthy- koking, stood with his back to the hearth on which logs blazed on the old-fashioned andirons. The long room, always dark at this end from its low ceiling crossed by heavy oak beams, and its oak-panelled walls, would have been gloomy this morning but for the fire which seemed brighter by contrast, Michael's face, too, was, from the same cause, ruddier than usual, as the fire lit up its pleasant features and the chestnut-brown, 'curly hair. "No use, no use, grannie, I'm a confirmed bachelor, and your friend, Miss Molly, would laugh at me, as she laughs at everything; she always has laughed at every man since I can remember her. Bless you! she's happy enough by herself; she don't want a man to disturb her life and rob her of her freedom. I hear she says that when she's talked to about marrying." Michael looked severe, and he went out abruptly into the raw mist, without a backward glance, He was glad to end the subject. Mrs. Moss had come to spend the winter with her grandson at the Garth. The picturesque old place had come unexpectedly to him while he was away in South Africa; on his return, he had asked her to come and help him in his new position. The bent, fragile old woman sighed as her eyes wandered round the room. "A cosy home for anyone," she thought; "for her above all, used as she is to gentle ways and cultivated tastes. Michael throws away his chances."</p>
        <p>She looked at the 'dark walls. Several of the panels were in linen-fold carving; on her left a broad recess was filled with books; beside the chimney breast on the right was a much deeper nook, lighted by a small painted window, with a shelf round it; this held some rare German glass, and some bits of blue and white china. These and other like tokens reminded the gazer that the Garth had been a manor-house till it .came to its last owner, Mr. Standen. A tap at the door, then in came a rather tall, slender woman, brown and supple in movement as a gipsy ; dark eyes and red, curving lips smiled' cheerfully, as she nodded her greeting to Mrs.. Moss.</p>
        <p>"I made sure you were alone, and you said I might announce myself," she laughed cheerily. "I believe you have stayed in all day. Too bad of you, when we're so proud of our air; you'll not go back any better for it, sitting all alone while you knit those stoekings ! I know -what'll do you good; come out for a walk with me. I'll bring you back with a colour like a rose in spite of the mist. Does not "—she hesitated before the name—" Mr. Michael Moss take you out walking ? " There was a twinkle in grannie's eyes, set deeply in their dark hollows. "My grandson's a busy man, Miss Molly; maybe my pace would not match with his."</p>
        <p>Miss Molly did not see the twinkle; she was taking down from a peg in the garden porch grannie's cloak and hood. "Lor' love ye Mrs. Moss "—she did not look fully.at the old woman—"he's a man, and that means he has only room in his thoughts for self. Self tills a man's view—it leaves no space for a thought of others." The old woman sighed as We fastened her hood. "'Tis but our third meeting," she said, timidly, as if the shortness of acquaintance did not warrant her outspokenness; "yet I feel as if I'd known you for years. I think you're far too blithe and bonny to use bitter words. Now and again I've thought you had a special grudge against some man, but you are hard on all of them."</p>
        <p>Miss Marsh seemed vexed, her colour deepened, and her lip had a scornful curl. " ' If Youth only knew, if Age only could '—a wise old saw enough; but I'm neither young nor old, so it doesn't touch me. As to the men you set value on, I wish other women were as free as lam."</p>
        <p>"As you think you are." A sudden light in her visitor's dark eyes warned grannie. Miss Marsh opened the outer door, and they set forth together. The older woman stumbled as she stepped from the garden into the road. Molly's strong arm caught her and saved her from a fall. Perhaps the longing to protect and help, innate in some women, towards feebler creatures, made laughing, mocking Molly feel tenderly for her companion. "Why did you not come here before ? " Mrs. Moss stared; a deeper meaning than they conveyed sounded in the girl's words. The sunken eyes and thin lips smiled very sweetly as Mrs. Moss answered: "I had no one to come to. Till a few months ago Michael was soldiering in South Africa." "Is he your only grandson ? " "Yes; and I did not know him when he was younger. My son and his wife lived far away." "But Mr. Michael came here years ago." Her defiant tone caught Mrs. Moss's attention. "Were you here at the time ? " A short silence; then the answer came hurriedly: "Oh, yes; he was here before I came home from school."</p>
        <p>Miss Molly became suddenly voluble as to the price of land per acre, the bad potato crop, and the terribly long time the fowls had been moulting. At last she paused. "Michael was not farming his own land in those days," Mrs. Moss said; "he worked for his god- father, Mr. Standen. I don't know how it came about, but I heard he had taken a dislike to Brigden ; perhaps "—with a keen, inquiring look —"he met with a disappointment there. My next news of Michael came in a letter from the front. Some time after that someone said that he had quarrelled with his godfather, so we were sur- prised when Mr. Stanton died to learn that he'd willed the place and everything else to Michael."</p>
        <p>Molly was silent; grannie felt impatient. "He'll have to marry," she said, gravely. "I have home ties and must leave him before Christmas. Michael can't live alone; he wants a wife a bright companion who will amuse him." "Then there's no help for the poor man." Molly laughed merrily. "Plenty of girls are always ready to be made slaves of. The silly creatures think they catch prizes; they can't see they are giving up all that's worth living for—l mean freedom."</p>
        <p>The Garth stood far back in its big garden, just beyond the village. The yellow road went down' and as the trees behind the hedgerow grew larger, till they almost met overhead and spread more widely, it gained in beauty. A mild wind 'had risen; as it drove the mist before it the atmosphere grew comparatively clear. Ever since Molly's last words, Mrs. Moss had been calling up her courage. She had the feeling that she was about to discover a secret. "You are bitter," she said; "and one can't _ _</p>
        <p>reconcile it with your happy, laughing way. I wonder if you have real cause for your dislike to mankind ? Miss Molly pulled her cape more closely 'together and shivered. "The wind is scattering the mist," she said, "and we shall soon have rain; it will be better for you to turn back, Mrs. Moss. NO "—this in answer to grannie's pleading face—"l really have no more story to tell than the 'imife-grinder ' had."</p>
        <p>Grannie's summons came much sooner than she had looked for it. Being a woman who, not caring to talk of herself, spent most of her time in helping others, she had merely said to Molly Marsh "I have home ties." She had given her</p>
        <p>widowed life to cheer a sister who, older—than she was and less well provided for, was suffering from this short separation, though she had per- suaded her dear Ruth to go and help Michael in his changed position. Only a few days after Molly's visit a letter came urging Mrs. Moss to return home. ' She handed the gammons to Michael; he shrugged his shoulders with annoyance. "I must say izood-bve to Molly Marsh before I go, Michael. I am sorry to leave you, my dear boy, when you have been so kind." Then, after Page.): "I fancy you don't wish her to come Gem wet I MIKA climb the hill to her hem."</p>
        <p>Looking extra severe, he said stiffly: "Mist :darsh's comings and goings are perfectly in- lifferent to me, so long as we do not meet. She .s perfcetly welcome to come here. You like !ler, I wish to keep out of her way; she comes, therefore, as your visitor." _ _</p>
        <p>His manner was forced. Mrs. Moss felt more titan ever sure than there had been something between these two. She said to herself: "I will know the truth before I quit Brigden ; mysteries do not make for happiness." Since her father died, Molly had not at first been absolute mistress of the old house on the</p>
        <p>Her guardians, a strong-willed couple, decided that she could not guide herself, and that she was better away from Brigden. At last Molly rebelled, and they gave her freedom, but by that time she was thirty. They had tried to marry her to men of their own choice. She said she hated all men, and would live and die an old maid. Against her will she had spent most part of each year in another county; she had few neighbours near the old house on the hill. Yet Molly told herself (when she was at last, her guardians said, free to make mistakes (she was going to begin real life. The guardians had lived entirely in the country, surrounded by admiring and less wealthy relatives, so that their ideas were provincial and archaic from lack of contradiction.</p>
        <p>Molly read her new friend's note and threw it down on the table with a petulant movement. She presently read it again, her eyes darkening with tears.</p>
        <p>"It's my luck ! " she said, passionately. That morning Molly had told herself Grannie Moss was the only woman she had met in whom she felt tempted to confide. "Perhaps, her thoughts went on, "it is safer that she should go. I may have mistaken her real nature, and she might give me away." She went down at once to the Garth.</p>
        <p>Chatting beside the blazing fire her heart, so long kept from any show of feeling, grew warmer and warmer. She felt as though she could not say good-bye to her new friend. At last she rose, and, bending over the fragile old woman, tenderly kissed her. "You'll come to 13rigden again, won't you? And next time you'll come to me; the hill to my place is too steep for you, and—and—l feel on sufferance at the Garth. Charming house as it is, I know I am not welcome ? "</p>
        <p>There was a question in her voice. "Then it has been doubly kind of you to come; it must be unpleasant even to enter a man's house against whom you are so greatly prejudiced." Molly walked across to the window and looked out. There was nothing to be seen but dank vegetation. The mists had been frequent, and the poor drenched chrysanthemum blossoms in the garden hung down their heads as if ashamed of their lack of beauty. The gentle voice from the ingle-nook called her back to Mrs. Moss.</p>
        <p>"I thank you very much for so kindly asking me to your house, but, my dear—if I may call you so—it is hardly likely we shall meet again. I gather from our doctor's letter that my dear sister is very ill. At her age I cannot hope for prompt recovery; even if it comes, I shall not leave her again. "You could not say that, if you cared for me," Molly exclaimed, moved out of her usual self- restraint. "Oh, you don't know how much I care for you!" The old woman had risen; she took both Molly's slender hands. "Thank you, dear. Will you think me a con. ceited old woman, always to want to help those who are good enough to care for me? I do believe I could help you, if you would but trust me. Tell me, won't you, why you so greatly dis- like my grandson ? " Molly turned from the pathetic, pleading eyes. Still she did not draw her hands away. Presently: _ _</p>
        <p>"I did not want to set you against him, but he has made a friend of mine unhappy." "Are you quite sure? I entirely believe in you, Molly, but you may mistake about another person's feelings." "I'll tell you; then you'll see I make no mistaken."</p>
        <p>"Sit down, dear." Mrs. Moss drew a chair forward, but Molly placed herself on a low stool so that her face was hidden.</p>
        <p>"It happened several years ago. My friend was at school, and was told that her father was very ill and that she was to go home next morning. She was strong-willed, and also eccentric; she resolved to go and see her father without delay. She had money enough, and without a word to anyone she managed to get safely to the railway. The home station was at some distance from her father's house.</p>
        <p>"She set out to walk home, so as to avoid the village. Before she was half-way snow began to fall • it very soon became thick and blinding. She could scarcely see her way, the hill was steep, and the climb soon became slippery; she made a false step, and fell. "A voice cried out from below:</p>
        <p>"'Stay where you are.' "In a minute she was raised up. She was unable to stand, her ankle gave her such sharp pain; and she said so. "The person who had come to help her—a tall, strong-looking man—bent down and looked in her face.</p>
        <p>have seen you before,' he said. You want to go to-' He named her home. You cannot walk. I must carry you, unless you prefer I should ask your people to send a carriage ? ' ."My friend said No,' to this. The man took her up in his arms as though shP were a baby. He set her down when they reached the house door within the courtyard. She was stupefied by her fall, and also very much ashamed of her plight. She scarcely thanked her rescuer for his kindness. She did not ask his name, or whether he would come in. Her ankle was soon well, and her father recovered. She worried about her seeming ingratitude and want of courtesy to the stranger, but she did not speak of her adventure to her father.</p>
        <p>"The first time they went to church she saw her rescuer in a seat near hers. When they came out she as7.ed her father who that gentleman was.</p>
        <p>"Her father seemed surprised. gentleman,' he said. "I said she was strong-willed, and also eccen- tric. She at once related her adventure, and added: ' He behaved just like a gentleman.' _ _ _</p>
        <p>' That is not a</p>
        <p>"Her father laughed, said she was a silly child, and must go back to school. She was only fifteen. He said she must not come home again till he went himself to fetch her.</p>
        <p>"She did a foolish thing after that, though when she told me of it I thought she was right. She wrote to Mr. Martin, the man who carried her, and warmly thanked him for his kindness. called next day at the school and asked to see her, but she was not told of his visit till afterwards. She then discovered that others had heard of it. She was teased and laughed at, and therefore felt angry at his indiscretion in calling at the school.</p>
        <p>"She thought she hated him; but she was always thinking of him, and wondering when she should see him.</p>
        <p>"She left school when her father died, and the day after the funeral she received a letter full of sympathy from Mr. Martin. "He asked at the end of his letter how soon he might call on her. "My friend was now eighteen. It seemed to her wiser to shew the letter to her guardians, and tell them she meant to see this visitor when he called. They both laughed at her; she felt mortified. They said that as her fortune would be larger than anyone had expected, she would for a time receive many such proposals; it was well, they added, that according to her father's will she was to be a minor till she was twenty- six, otherwise she would be snapped up by some needy fortune-hunter. One of her schoolfellows had already pointed out this danger ; she was also softened by her father's death, and anxious to obey the guardians he had chosen for her. They took her away to their own home, and the matter ended." _</p>
        <p>Molly paused, as if she had more to tell. "Did the girl forget all about him ? " Mrs. Moss inquired, with assumed carelessness. Molly's eyes sparkled and her cheeks flushed. "No, the girl did not forget. I believe she cared more for him than she knew, or she would not have been so keenly mortified by that which came after. On her return to her home she met Mr. Martin. She bowed, and stopped to greet him, from impulse, I fancy. Will you believe it? lie looked at her with contempt, he did not even en.ige his hat. and nassed on. Sin.ce that meeting life has been dull for her. She avoids all men, and, for her sake, so do L That is her story." A little silence, then Mrs. Moss said: "If I send your friend a message will you repeat it to her?"</p>
        <p>Molly looked hard at her, then a sad smile softened her face. "I may forget it. you know.*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 31, 1907.</p>
        <p>"You'll not forget ; you are not so hard as zou wish me to believe. Now listen. Please tell '?our friend that, so far as he knew, her father was right. Mr. Martin was not then in a suitable position to win her regard; besides, she was too young for such an acquaintance, though she rightly divined he was her equal. Bid her be honest with her own heart, and if she can find a way to be open and honest with the other. For, Molly, he loves.her dearly. He is proud, and he thinks he has been despised as an inferior; can she not see this ? She will, dear girl—indeed she will see it, if you tell her I will answer for the result."</p>
        <p>Molly's bent head had dropped lower and lower; a deeply-flushed face was now uplifted, but it shunned grannie's kindly eyes. "Yes, I'll tell her what you say. But, as you know, one person cannot say how another will judge the same subject." Molly spoke coldly, as if the matter bored her; yet she lingered, unwilling to say good-bye. "I am to start directly after dinner," grannie said. "I cannot thank you enough for your friendliness to me. Good-bye, my dear child, and Heaven bless you." Molly's arms were round her as the two women kissed. Grannie thought she heard a stifled sob. She followed her visitor to the door and watched her alert, graceful movements till she was hidden from sight. "'Tis best to let her go; she is not frivolous, and she will think over our talk. Her own pride should teach her to make allowance for his; if they would both pray one for the other, all would surely come right." While Mrs. Moss put her few possessions together—for though she always looked dainty she spent little on her dress—she went on think- ing of Molly and of the warm affection she herself felt for the woman so disliked by her grandson. "I ought in justice to hear his story," she thought. "He may have stronger reason for his rudeness than mere pique."</p>
        <p>Michael Moss put his grandmother into an empty carriage and stood talking to her. "You must take a wife—you must indeed, dear boy." He gave a whimsical laugh for answer. "The inconsistency of women," he said. "You too, and I thought you were free from their weaknesses; you seem to have forgotten that I; am a confirmed bachelor. Your kind visit has. shewn me I am managing my house all right, so I shall persevere in carrying out the kind advice you have given me." "And now I give you the best of all—marry! Listen: You are still young; you tell me you an enjoy a game of cricket, and so on. How will it be a few years hence? You have few acquaintances; your life as you grow older will be very lonely. Put your ear closer, dear Michael. You know a woman who loves you as you deserve to be loved, but she would rather die than betray it." So far in an incisive whisper; then aloud. "Good-bye, and thank you." He could not answer, for the train was moving; he waved his hand, and smiled as long as he could see her; then he fell into thought, frowned, and went home. _</p>
        <p>"Grannie would not tell a lie," he thought, "but what she says can't be possible. Molly could never forgive the way I have treated her, the avoidance I have shewn. Besides, she detests men, and would not submit to one among them. She has to my knowledge refused two good offers." He went home and sat before his desk, ostensibly to write business letters. But his thoughts flew off to Molly Marsh. How he had loved her once—how he had watched her develop- ment as years went by. Her father's pride—for Mr. Marsh had always refused to return his greeting---had not checked the growth of his passion. Then she had at last been free, and he had resolved to put her to the test; he had claimed his right as a man to see her face to face. His heart beat in great throbs as he recalled his own feelings when he wrote that request to visit at the house on the hill. The old longing to see her, if it might be to hold her in his arms, was again strong in him; then the detestable reality tore away all self-deceit, and taught him with bitter incisiveness that all had been a dream.</p>
        <p>She had never answered his letter, and two days later she had left home. Before her return he gave up his post as manager to Mr. Standen and took a London clerkship. He was well paid, but he hated the confined life; still, he had more distraction and also better means of cultivation than he could get in the country. He toiled on till the troubles began in South Africa. When he returned as owner of the Garth, his acquaintances —they were only a few—told him he must marry. He laughed, and said he was a confirmed old bachelor; and, by way of silencing gossip on this subject, he had invited Mrs. Moss to pay him a long visit. "I meant her to stay on," he thought, dis- contentedly; "sooner than lose the wise, pleasant old dear, I'd have asked Aunt Selina to bear her company."</p>
        <p>Next day, and the day after, he could not shake off the thought of Grannie's words. Then he remembered a fact which entirely contradicted them. He had noted Sunday after Sunday that Molly Marsh went home from church by a circuitous path certainly ten minutes further than the direct way which he had to take to the Garth.</p>
        <p>"She does it to avoid me," he thought. "Poor dear Grannie speaks as she wishes." He thought that if ever he brought his mind to marriage the wife he chose must be meek and loving, not a self-willed man. hater.</p>
        <p>Then came Sunday. The morning was clear and cold, but the air felt healthier than it had been in those milder days of mist. Molly Marsh was at church, and Michael, against his will, stood looking after her, as she took the longer road. Bow well she walked; how different she was from any other woman; how superior to those smart girls he had last week met at the Hall.</p>
        <p>Since Michael had come into money he had been asked to dine at the best houses in the neighbourhood, whose inhabitants had till now ignored his existence. He rarely accepted these invitations; he shrank from the chance of meeting Molly Marsh. While he still looked after her she turned her head. Their eyes met, and instinctively he raised his bat.</p>
        <p>Without waiting to see whether she returned his greeting he hastened along the way she had taken and soon came up with her. She smiled and held out her hand. He thought she looked more delightful than ever. "Why do you come out of your way,, Mr. Moss ? You will find it quite a round. You will have to go across the waste to the Garth." He fancied that her voice trembled, and this encouraged him. "You think I shall be over- tired? I wonder whether you will be kind enough to ask me in to rest at the top of the hill 9" Their eyes met; Molly looked . provokingly saucy. Michael laughed. "Dear my" Molly's little nose had an upward tilt on occasions. "Come in by all means. You —you might sooner have come if you would have so far condescended."</p>
        <p>It was her turn to laugh now, and she did so heartily, for he looked puzzled. He followed her into a cosy library, evidently her special den, for beside the writing-table, which bore traces of much correspondence, was a covered basket with ends of knitting wool and silk. "That is the best chair for resting in." "I'll stand, please, till I've asked your pardon and confessed my fzults." "Please don't," Molly said, nervously. She began to fear she had been overbold. "Well, then, may I say something else ? " Ho waited, but she did not speak. "May I come again, and again, till I have persuaded you that I love you ? " Grannie had not deceived her; but Molly thought he seemed over-confident. She felt rebellious.</p>
        <p>"Come as often as you like, but remember, I make no promise." She had seated herself. He went and stood over her as if he felt sure of her consent. "I was told that if I asked for your love you would give it." Molly v.,9s blush- ing, but at this she held her head very erect. "Will you give it ? " he said, very gravely, for he began to doubt. Molly leant back and laughed heartily. "Shall I fetch my father's stick that you may give me the beating your face threatens? Yeu are a gentle wooer indeed ! And LI was told von loved me dearly, so dearly that I had spoilt your life. Was I not right when I said men were hateful ? " She still laughed, but he saw tears in her bright eyes. "Dearest Molly, lam bent on proving you wrong, except when you say 'I love you ' ; and," clasping his arm round her, he whispered as he kissed her, "I say I will have you whether you late we or not."</p>
        <p>am: EN.)</p>
        <p>.1 CHARLES FORDE'S I</p>
        <p>the committee* to say wbo should bear it. He could not, however, take the view that there was a line or punishment. Mr. ROBERTS: I withdraw punishment and sub- stitute bardshi ps. (Laughter.) FATHER WILSON said the reason why he made the distinction was because the committee were not respon- sible for the deduction and other employment was open to the teachers while they were waiting for admission to college. If the committee agreed to pay the full amount they might be opening the door to very serious responsibility in the future. At the same time he admitted that £l3 did not seem very much. The voting was as follows : For the amendment: Revs. J. Davies and John Yonge ; Alderman Bennett; Councillors Wilkinson and Toole; Messrs. H. Roberts. R. F. Fairclough, E. Plinston, J. Upson, T. P. Bradshaw-10. Against: Rev. N. Wilson • Alderman Tinnion ; Councillors Sankey,Lord,Pembehon, Diskinson, Barton, Evans; Messrs. A. J. Jolley, H. Kingdon.-10. The CHAIRMAN gave his casting vote in favour of the committee's recommendation which was adopted.</p>
        <p>DEFECTIVE CHILDREN. At a meeting of the School Management Committee, a report of the School Attendance Committee with reference to the defective children at the Latchford R. C. school was considered, and it was resolved, that this committee is of opinion that there is now no neces- sity to increase the staff of that school. FATHER Wrr.soN explained that at one time it was thought there was a considerable number of more or less defective children, but on further medical examina- tion it was found that only two children required special treatment and they had been withdrawn from the school. KINDERGARTEN INSTRUCTION. • The School Management Committee resolved : (a) That a course of kindergarten lessens for teachers be arranged for the coming winter session; (b) that a class be held for two hours each week, and that teachers in the employ of the Committee to be admitted free; and (c) that Miss Florence Sharpley be appointed to teach the class at a fee of per evening. Father WILSON said Miss Sharpley was one of the most capable instructors in the subject, and the com- mittee earnestly hoped the teachers would take full advantage of the classes and attend regularly.</p>
        <p>PUNISHMENT BOOK TO BE KEPT. The School Management Committee considered the following regulations recently issued by the Board of Education, viz. :—(a) the code of regulations for public elementary schools; (b) the regulations for Secondary schools; and (c) the regulations for Technical schools, Schools of Art,and other schools and classes for further education. With reference to (a), it was decided to call the attention of the head teachers to the require- ments of the code in regard to the punishment book. In view of the difficulty of satisfactorily fulfilling the requirements in this respect, it was resolved, that the attention of the Association of Education Committees be called to the re-insertion of this regulation, and to an expression of opinion on the part of this Committee that the Board of Education should be approached with a view to its removal. With regard to (b), the Director was instructed to ask the Governors of the Grammar school what steps they propose to take in view of the amended requirements of the Board of Education. FATHER 'WILSON said the difficulties of keeping a punishment book would be very great. It was thought by some that it might even lead to surreptitious punish- ments. Consequently it had been decided to recom- mend that the attention of the Board of Education be called to the rule with a view to its removal.</p>
        <p>NEW EVENING SCHOOL. Alluding to the arrangements for the evening classes for the forthcoming winter sesson, Father Wilson said they were the same as last year with the exception that a new branch technical school had been established at St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street, to meet a very con- siderable demand in that quarter of the town. Over 120 scholars had pledged themselves to attend during the coming session. The committee might cordially congratulate them- selves upon the reports from the Board of Education upon the work of the Branch Technical Schools last session. Both the attendances and the results were better, the expenses proportionately less, and the grants aboul £2OO more. The report on the Central Technical listitute was quite satisfactory. The most important feature was the demand for more accommoda- tion, and an attempt was being made to meet it by the improvements which were to be carried out by the Management Committee in conjunction with the Gymnasium Committee, those bringing the Gymnasium at least partially into , use for Secondary School purposes. The whole of the School Management Committee's minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>Ell LE - EANSf°I4 ILIOUSNESS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE DEFICIENCY IN TRAINING COLLEGE ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>HARDSHIP ON 'PUPIL TEACHERS</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall, on Monday evening under the presidency of Councillor Fulton Smith. There were also present :—The Mayor (Alder- man Smethurst). Aldermen Bennett and Tinnion. Coun- cillors Evans, Barton, WilkinsOn, Sankey, Ellison, Dickinson, Toole, Lord, Pemberton, the Revs. J. Yonge, J. Davies and N. Wilson, Messrs. H. Roberts, A.J.Jolley, R. T. Fairclough, H. Kingdon, E. Plinston, T. P. Bradshaw, J. Upson, J. Moore Murray (director of education). PUPIL TEACHERS AND TRAINING COLLEGES. THE ACCOMMODATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee resolved that three pupil teachers who passed the recent Preliminary Examination for the certificate but failed to secure admission into training colleges be appointed as half-time teachers for the year commencing on the Ist August, 1907, in order that they may attend the pupil teachers' classes six half days each week and sit again for the Preliminary Examination for the certifi- cate ; the salary to be at the same rate as that paid during the last year of their engagements (R2O) less the amount of the Government grant for pupil teachers (.£7) which . will not be received. The Rev. FATHER WILSON, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said the pupil teachers mentioned were no longer recognized as such by the Board of Education and consequently the committee was not entitled to the grant. The Rev. J. DAVIES moved that the recommendation be not confirmed. He held that the teachers ought to receive the same salary as last year and that the com- mittee should hear the loss of the grant.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE said it seemed rather a heart- less thing that the teachers should be fined over again. They were fined because they did not get into college and if they were to continue in the service of the com- mittee they were to be fined in the loss of salary. On the face of the decision looked a little hard. _</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON, in seconding- the -amendment, said he understood the teachers had failed to secure admission to the colleges owing to lack of accommodation. There- fore the committee was going to punish them. The problem was a serious one, and,he hoped the Association of Education Committees would take into consideration the lack of accommodation for teachers who had passed the examination. _</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON said it was the fact that the teachers had passed the examination but could not gain admission owing to the lack of accommodation. The Committee's resolution pressed hardly upon them. They would be able to give the Committee a considerable amount of valuable service, having had two years' experience in the schools. They would, however, receive less than the first year pupil teacher who was just starting, and it seemed to him they would be worth £2O a year to the Committee. The lack of college accommodation was owing to some extent to the fact that the local authori- ties had not tackled the question as Warrington was prepared to tackle it. Two years ago a conference was held at which the Man- chester Committee proposed a scheme by which a train- ing college should be established for teachers in Lancashire and Cheshire, but Warrington was the only authority that WAS prepared to support the scheme. Everybody was afraid of spending a little money to bring about that very desirable object. Mr. KINGDON asked how the vacancies in the training colleges were filled. Did those who were rejected fail to obtain as high an educational standard as those who gained admission, or did they put all the names in a hat and draw for positions? (Laughter.) Mr. H. ROBERTS said it hardly seemed fair to these young people that, having complied with the require- ments necessary for admission to college and failed to get in because the colleges were not large enough or sufficiently numerous, they should have to return to their duties as pupil teachers and spend further time to prepare for another examination which ought not to be necessary if the present or past Governments had done their duty with regard to the provision of colleges. He supported the amendment because he thought the committee could a great deal better afford to bear the expense of what would otherwise be neither more nor less than a fine, without any offence having been com- mitted. In this case the punishment did not fit the crime at all—in fact there was no crime. Mr. R. T.FAIRCLOCGFI said such a course as the Com- mittee recommended was never adopted by the school managers before the Education Committee was formed. They always paid the money and never fined the teachers in that way. He suggested that Father Wilson should withdraw the minute _ -</p>
        <p>FATHER WILSON said he was not authorised to with- draw the minute and personally he did not feel inclined to agree to its withdrawal. He agreed with Nir.Roberts that no crime had been committed but he also asserted that there had been no punishment or fine. The com- mittee were not responsible for the new rules. The teachers could obtain employment elsewhere as uncerti- flcated teach eir4 and two of them at least bad agreed to the committee's terms.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERTS said these teachers were amongst the percentage who had been refused admission to colleges, not for any fault but simply because there was no room. He asked Mr. Murray whether they were now as half time teachers in any sense different to what they were before, and he was assured that they were not. They would perform the same duties and serve the same hours—in other words they had to waste another year of their time outside college through no fault of their own, and the committee deducted £7 from their salaries. If that was not a punishment he would call it by any other name they liked, but certainly that was the effect of it.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBHRTON said there ought to be new regulations to govern the examinations, as there might easily come a time when the committee would have more pupil teachers than they required. It ought to be announced before the examinations took place that there were only a certain number of vacancies tc be filled. Mr. ROBERVI : It is admitted on all hands that the college accommodation is not equal to the demands of the educational interests of the country. - Councillor PEMBERTON : If that is so, it is hard lines that additional expense should be thrown on the municipal authorities. Why should the ratepayers have to make up the loss? We are here to do what is right and just to the ratepayers. If the teachers are employed on the terms recommended they will have an oppor- tunity of getting in college next year, but I don't see much hope for them. The college accommodation is inadequate now, and the chances are that next year there will be a great many more candidates. Mr. UrsoN : Only 25 pupil teachers come out each year in Warrington. The Rev. JOHN YONGE suggested that Mr. Davies should alter his amendment to the effect that £2O each be paid to the teachers. The CHAIRMAN: That would be out of order. You must bear in mind that you are establishing a precedent in the matter and you may put some future authority in a very difficult position. The Rev. J. YONGE : If a thing is unjust we ought to put it right. The CHAIRMAN: It is not unjust when they have accepted the terms. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY suggested that instead of there being an injustice, a favour had been conferred upon the teachers. They were allowed the privilege of sitting for the examination again and obtaining a higher place in the lists. He believed the training college authori- ties considered thequalifloations of the applicants when selecting teachers for admission. It was not fair to cite what school managers had done under the old con- ditions as a necessary precedent for the action of the Committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. DAVIS said that according to the new Tules it was not those who were at the top of the lists but those who made first application that secured admission.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD said he supported the resolution at the meeting of the Committee. He was under the impression that the teachers were at the bottom of the list and if that was not so, he would like to be corrected. Was the selection a lottery, or was it badly arranged? Mr. UPSON: It is pass or fail now in the examination. Mr. JOLLEY : But there are such things as distinctions.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD said he was under the impression that by giving the teachers another year's tuition, the Committee was doing them a favour. The question was whether it was fair to the other students to have to compete with those who had had the benefit,of the extra instruction. FATHER Wn.sorr said the teachers were not bound to pass any other examination. They were in -a position of advantage as compared with the other pupil teachers, because they could send in their applications to the training colleges at once with a certain amount of assurance. Next year when the new regulations were in force they would have a better chance. The object of preparing them for another examination was that they in improve their positions by giving dis- tinctions n some subjects which would entitle them to a preference. There were some hardships and it was for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>"WOODMAN, SPARE TEI ALT TREE."</head>
        <p>"WOODMAN, SPARE TEI ALT TREE."</p>
        <p>• The Sites and Buildings Committee resolved that the extra strip of land on the western boundary of the site for the Bolton Council school be prepared for use as a nature study garden. It was decided that the boundary wall on the north side of the site for the Bolton Council school be diverted so as to enable a tree on that portion of the land to be retained. - - Alderman BENNETT complimented the committee upon the latter resolution which he said, showed admirable discretion on their past. He hoped other committees would follow their example and obey the aphorism "Woodman, spare that tree." (Laughter.) The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DOG SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DOG SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Creditable Display.</p>
        <p>The success which attended the fifth member's show in connection with the Warrington and District Canine Society, held in the Drill Hall, on Saturday, was proof of the vitality of the .organisation. Although the entries, totalling 175. showed a slight decrease on last year, the quality was better all round, and the show did the members a great credit; The Drill Hall is admir- ably adapted for the purpose of such an exhibition. It was somewhat surprising that the attendance of the public was hardly up to the usual standard. Mr. L. P. C. Astley, who acted as judge, discharged the duties expeditiously, and his decisions gave entire satisfaction. i Much praise s due to Mr. White the energetic and efficient hon. secretary. Appended are the principal awards in order of merit : Silver Challenge Cup, presented by the president (Colonel Edelsten) for best Dog in the Show: R. T. Baines. Silver Challenge Cup, presented by Mrs. J. A. Edelsten for best Bitch: W. Bunting. Silver Cup, presented by Captain Avery for best Dog or Bitch Puppy bred by exhibitor: W. Bunting. Challenge Bowl, presented by Mr. W. A. Leslie for best Airedale Puppy: W. Bunting. Special prize given by Mr. F. W. White for best in brace class: E. Plinston. Special prize, given by Mrs. Rylands for best Fox Terrier: H. Milling. Retriever, Setter or Pointer: R. T. Baines; W. Meech; F. W. White. Collie or Old English Sheep Dog or Bitch: R. T. Baines; Miss G. Rylands. Airedale Dog: F. Shaw; W. Collins. Aire- dale Bitch : W. Bunting; J. H. Harris T. B. Berry Irish or Welsh Terrier Dog or Bitch: F. 'G. Fairhurst: J. Bffiley ; W. B. Walker. Fox Terrier Dog, rough; H. Milling; F. W. Waring. Fox Terrier Bitch, rough; F. W. White; W. Gallop; E. Burrows. Fox Terrier Dog, smooth: F. Turner; J. Howard; H. J. Pearse, Fox Terrier Bitch, smooth; A. Dwerryhouse : Miss H. N. Richardson; D. Hewitt. Field Spaniel: J. Beswick (1 and 3) ; E. Sumner. Cocker Spaniel: H. W. Booth. Schipperke Dog: E. Plinston; W. C, Baines, Schipperke Bitoh : E. Plinston (1 and 3) • H. W. Booth. Bull Dog: Colonel J. A. Edelsten : Captain H. L. W. Norrington. Bull Bitch: Colonel Edelsten ; J. Leslie; W. Leslie. Bull Terrier: R. T. Baines; F. Turner. Black and Tan Terrier: Mrs. J. Randall Scotch or Skye: R. T. Baines; W. H. Blackly. Pomeranian: R. Merrill. Pug or Yorkshire: T. Townley; Miss G. Rylands (2 and 3). Any variety Dog ,over 251bs. weight: R. T. Baines: E. Sumner; H. Beswick. Any variety Bitch over 251b5.: J. Beswick; R. T. Baines • W. Leslie. Any variety Dog under 251b5.: R. T. Baines; H. Milling ;E. Plinston. Any variety Bitch uuder 251b5.: R. T. Baines ; E. Plinston; F. W. White. Any variety Dog or Bitch not previously mentioned: R. T. Baines: E. Plinston. Any variety Puppy except Fox Terrier: 1, W. Bunting; R. T. Baines; E. Plinston. Any variety Puppy bred by exhibitor: W. Bunting: E. Plinston; Colonel Edelsten. Novice Dog: W. Meech; W. C. Baines; E. Plinston. Novice Bitch: R. J. Baines; E. Plins- ton; F. W. White. Maiden Dog: W. Meech; W. C. Baines; E. Plinston Maiden Bitch R.T. Baine3; Colonel Edelston F. W.White. Brace class: E.Plinston. Litter: T. B. Berry; G. Fairhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Committee was held on Tuesday evening, Mr. G. Pilling presiding. There were also -present, Messrs. J. MoDermid, T. Westbrook, F. Warburton, J. W. Bonchier, F. Pearson, J. Mynekyme, J. Hallows, J. H. Clarke, G. F. Ashton (clerk to- the Rural District Council), and J. Swinton (surveyor). THE LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE. The Clerk said he had received a letter from the Warrington Corporation in reference to the lighting of the village and he was pleased to say the Gas Committee had approved of their plan. PASSAGE NEAR VICTORIA-SQUARE. Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright, of Warrington, attended before the committee with respect to the width of the passage from Victoria-square to Gaskell- street. After some discussion it was decided tirtt the passage be made 20ft. wide. THE RED LION INN. Mr. Clarke (chairman oflthe Building Sub-committee) stated that the committee had approved of certain addi- tions being made to the rear of the Red Lion Inn, London-road, for Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Com- pany. The additions had been recommended by the Surveyor. ' The alterations were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. \</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. \</p>
        <p>Student: "There must be some mistake in my examination marking. I don't think I deserve an absolute zero." Inspector: "Neither do I; but it is the lowest mark I am allowed to give."</p>
        <p>Doctor: "What? Troubled with sleepless- ness? Eat something before going to bed." Patient: "Why, doctor, you once told me never to eat anything before going to bed." Doctor (with dignity) : "Pooh, pooh! That was last January. Science has made enor- mous strides since then."</p>
        <p>"Here! You, sir," cried the irate old gentleman, "didn't I tell you never to enter this house again?" "No, sir," replied his daughter's persistent suitor. "You said• not to cross your threshold,' so I climbed in at the window."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dove: "Henry, I think you are posi- direly cruel. Here I've tried so hard to cook you a nice dinner, and you haven't had a word to say to me about it." Mr. Dove: "Darling, I love you too much for that. If I said what I thought you'd never speak to me again."</p>
        <p>Timpkins was coming home from his Christmas holiday. Traift crowded. Timp- kins takes his little girl On his knee. Fashion- able young lady comes up to carriage window to see if there is room; upon which little Mabel exclaims: "Oh, papa, I'll stand and give the lady my seat.". Passengers giggle. Timpkins blushes. Young lady does ditto, and rushes on.</p>
        <p>Constituent: "Now, Mr. Wunnout, I wish you'd do your best to get my boy a good -Government position." M.P. : what can your son do?" Constituent: "What Can he do? Great Scot, man, if he could do any- thing I wouldn't be bothering you!"</p>
        <p>The father had gone away, and left his only son in charge of the shop. 'Are you the head of the firm?" asked a m,an With a sample case, entering the establishment. ."No, sir," remarked The young man. "I'm only the _heir of the head."</p>
        <p>A labourer in the employ of a North- country. faglner had a cheese set down before him that he might help himself. His master said to him : —"Sandy, you take a long time to breakfast." "You're right, master." answered he. "A cheese o' this size iss nae sae soon eaten as ye may think."</p>
        <p>, Sportsman (wishing for fresh fields to conquer); "I should like to try my hand at big game." Ignorant Youth: "Yes; I sup- pose you find it very hard to hit these little birds!"</p>
        <p>"Maudie," said a father to his little daugh- ter, "your school report is not nearly so good this term as it was last." "Well, dad, last term you sent Mrs. Fizzleton a couple of clucks, and this term you didn't,,' said Maudie, in an aggrieved tone. •</p>
        <p>Business Man: "Now, look here; didn't I tell you not to show your face here again?" Persevering Commerical Traveller: "You did, my dear sir, but I can't leave my face at home, can I, when I come here?"</p>
        <p>"Jimmy's got a great scheme to get out o' school when the days are fit for skating." "How does she work it?" "He goes out an' washes his face, an' the teacher thinksk he's ill an' sends him home."</p>
        <p>"Are you and Miss Spinks engaged?" in- quired a young man of his chum. "Report says so." "Well, she once sent me a true lover's knot." "Ah ! That looked like busi- ness." "Yes, but she spelt it without a k.,</p>
        <p>The j..eading Lady: "I promised mother that I wouldn't become an actress." Her Rival: "Well, you kept your word all right."</p>
        <p>Charitable Lady: "If you do a little work in the garden for me, now, I'll give you a good dinner after you've finished if." Beggar: "You'll get off cheaper, lady, if ye gimme the dinner now an' leave out the work. Work always gives .me an awful ap- petite."</p>
        <p>`"I received a lot of rejected munuscripts to-day," said Titmarsh. "Did . you?" re- plied his friend. "I had no idea you had an mbition to shine as an author." "Not ex- actly that," said Titmarsh. "You see, my girl and I ,quarrelled, and she returned all my letters.'</p>
        <p>Do you not at times have soulful yearnings which you, long to express in words, but can- not?" asked the fair-haired youth, who had a leaning toward the sentimental. "Yes, I recollect something like that once," ad- mitted the .college imdergraduate. "I wanted to telegraph home for money, and hadn't enough to pay for a telegram."</p>
        <p>During the last elections several banners with various mottoes were displayed. A. doctor coming home, seeing a crowd jeering outside his door, wondered what it was all about. The truth dawned on him when lie saw a banner connecting his house and the undertaker's opposite with the motto: " United we stand; divided we fall."</p>
        <p>Teacher (to class in school): "How many seasons are there?" Class: Four." ," Quite right," said the teacher. "Now, can yov name them?" Class: "Spring, summer, autumn, and winter." Teacher: "Now, can anyone tell me what season this is?" After It pause a little urchin held out his hand. "Well, ,Tommy," said the teacher. "Please sir, the football season."</p>
        <p>Member of a City firm (to whom a testi- monial his been presented by the staff on re: tiring from business) to manager: "I am much obliged to .you all for your very hand- some present, which I highly appreciate." Manager: "Pray don!t mention it, sir. We are much obliged to you for the opportunity of making it.</p>
        <p>The young man had just gone out of the shop when the assistant who had attended to him called up the shop-walker. "Did you hear that young fellow he asked, nodding towards the door where the customer had gone out: "No; what was the matter . with. him?"- "He wanted a pair of knicker- bockers to play football in." "Didn't he get them?" "He did not." "Why not? We've got plenty." "Yes, but he wanted flesh-coloured ones." " Well, we've got them." "No, we haven't. I showed them to him, and he turned up his nose and said that wasn't his flesh-colour. He said he wanted them black and blue,"</p>
        <p>Mrs.. Fibbins has written to the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals to know if something can be done to prevent horses being scratched. .She is sure it - must be very painful, because her husband, a rac- ing man, is sometimes. quite upset, and sh i e hears him groan n his sleep about a horse being scratched.</p>
