        <head>12, BRAM:WNW:IE ST.. DIUNSGATR, MANCIIHSTHE.</head>
        <p>12, BRAM:WNW:IE ST.. DIUNSGATR, MANCIIHSTHE.</p>
        <p>frIGHFIRLD IDOLL EI SG, BISPRM. A—Eloarding School 1 for Girls. Facing sea; bracing air. Thorough educa- tion. Physical Training. Inclusive terms. Paincipals. the Misses IRELLND.</p>
        <p>A INSDALIC, near Southport.—Horne Fchool for CX Girls (Bostrelers). Mod.inclu.Refs.- THORNVALE.StRt ion rd.</p>
        <p>The long-drawn-out Parlia- THE END OF mentary session of 1907 THE SESSION, has at last come to a close. Its end has been marked by a demonstration less dramatic, perhaps, but not less significant than that of last year regarding the relations between the two Houses of the Legislature. The Lords have becn frankly undismayed by the threats of the Government. The resolution passed after long debate in the Lower House adumbrated the course which the Government hoped to be able to take to curtail the power of the Lords to thwart what they speak of as " the will of the people," but what is more correctly defined as the will of the majority of the House of Commons. With, that resolution before them, the Unionist Peers in the House of Lords have gone steadily on to examine and to amend the bills that have been sent to them from the Lower House. They have acted strictly in accordance with their constitu- tional rights.Whetner they have been wise enforc- ing upon the House of Commons their views regard- ' ing the proposals submitted to them,tirne and the electorate alone can tell. The programme which the Government has carried through was one calculated intimately to effect the interests of the hereditary Chamber. The principal point of attack has been the land. The important land bills and one lesser were sent from the House of Commons to the Upper Chamber. Two only have returned, and these in such a condition that the Government and the majority in the Commons have felt them to be mutilated and deprived of a large portion of their usefulness. The Evicted Tenants Bill was submitted to very close scrutiny by the Lords, and many of the alterations on which they insisted have been accepted by the Government, although in Mr. BIRRILL'S opinion they have destroyed the ' main value of the bill as a message of peace to Ireland. The Small Holdings Bill has been less roughly treated. But there, too, the Lords have not neglected the opportunity of enforcing where they could certain alterations of machinery. Scotland has fared worse than the sister kingdoms. Two bills for her benefit were presented by the Government, and both have been killed by the House of Lords. The Small Landowners Bill was not regarded with great enthusiasm by all the</p>
        <p>Scotch supporters of the Government, and its loss therefore, has been less keenly felt than will be that of the Land Values Bill which the House of Lords threw out upon the second reading. But with all the difficulties entailed by the squabbles with the House of Lords, the Govern- ment can still place a fair margin of work accom- plished to its credit. The achievements of the I session have not by any means been small. The two Land Bills stand in the forefront. The Territorial Forces Bill has enshrined one of the most ambitious schemes of army reform since the great re-organisation under Mr. CARDWELL. Mr. LLOYD-GEORGE has succeeded in carrying no less than five useful measures, including the Merchant Shipping Bill and the Patents and Designs Bill. By taking up the case of the deceased wife's sister long left in the hands of the private Member, the Government has at last put an end to a bitter and disturbing controversy, and in a sense effected a reform in the marriage law of the country. It would be useless to contend that upon this qnes- tion, opinion has followed, or could follow, party Hoes, and perhaps the triumph of the promoters of the bill may be said to be qualified as far as the Government is concerned. The record of the session is undoubtedly one which does credit to the party in power. Mistakes have been made. The Premier without question overloaded the pro- gramme at the beginning of the year, and much that was promised had to be abandoned. But even so, the number of measures carried, and the institution of a new section of Parliamentary machinery in the Grand Committees, represent a _ tale of accomplishment for which we should not be able to find a parnilel for many sessions.</p>
        <p>As to the future little need be said. The Government has made many promises. Some of them must of necessity be left unfulfilled. Edu- cation, the Licensing Question, the Scottish Land Question, and a host of other matters are down upon the list for attention in 1908. It iS impos- sible that they can be all dealt with, even if there were no prospect of other matters of emergency arising to curtail the time at the disposal of Ministers. Unfortunately in one quarter of the United Kin gdom,at any rate,there is the promise of 'trouble in the recess which may affect very closely the business of the Government when the new selsion begins. Mr. REDMOND, we imagine, did not make his threat of a great agrarian agitation in Ireland during the autumn without full know- ledge of the forces at the command of the United , Irish League, and the intentions of the leaders of political opinion in Ireland. English people can only hope that his threat will not be fulfilled by a return of those disturbances to which " twenty years of resolute rule " put an end. If, however, the trouble does assume proportions and a character which will demand firm handling, we are confident that Mr. BIRRELL will not shirk the unpleasant duty which other Chief Secretaries have had to discharge.</p>
        <p>London does not seem to TORIES IN LONDON. have rocked on its founds-</p>
        <p>tions owing to the invasion by Warrington Conservatives on Saturday, and even St. Paul's is reported to be safe though the whole Tory train-load st,lod on the steps at once. We believe our friends, the enemy, had a good time in the city,except after dinner when they were penned in a small and stuffy room to hear political pearls of great price fall from the lips of local leaders. Mr. W. P. Rylands was, as usual nowadays, chief speaker. Also, as usual, he dwelt upon the sins of the Government, prayed for an early election, and made an attack upon Sir H. Campbell Bannerman.lt is a testimony to the wise leadership of England's popular premier that his enemies, big and little, take every opportunity of misrepresenting hire. Mr. Rylands did so on Saturday, and he has done so before. Not long ago he distorted Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman's famous phrase about" method of barbarism" in order to make it a political weapon, and on Satur- day he was equally reckless. He told his hearers that "they had Sir Henry's admission that he was prepared to subordinate any real interest of the country to what he was pleased to call party tactics." Just imagine any political leader—even Mr. Balfour—making such a fatuous aJmission. Yet Mr. Rylands debits the Premier with having done so. Is it worth while, we wonder, asking him for chapter and verse? Out of curiosity, however, we will request Mr. Rylands to tell us when and where Sir Henry Campboll-Bannerman admitted being guilty of such a despicable intention as to wilfully " subordinate any real interest of the country to party tactics."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Education 1,1 PUPIL TEACHERS' Committee devoted most of GRIEVANCE, their meeting on Monday evening to the discussion of a subject of considerable importance to the pupil teachers in their employ. The SChool Manage- ment Sub-Committee submitted a recommendation that three pupil teachers who passed the recent preliminary examination for the certificate but failed to secure admission into training colleges be appointed as half-time teachers in order that they might attend the pupil teachers' classes and sit again for the examination, the salary to be at the same rate as last year (£2O) less £7, the amount of the Government grant which would not be received. Many members of the committee considered this a decided hardship. It was not the fault of the teachers that they did not get into college. They qualified by passing the examination, but there was no accom- modation for them. While waiting for another opportunity to gain admission they will render exactly the same service to the committee as last year, yet their salary is to be reduced by £7. This appears to be a harsh procedure but.the committee disclaimed responsibility and placed it on the Board of Education. Either the pupil teachers or the ratepayers would have to stand the loss, and although in this case the amount would be trivial to a large education authority, yet the committee hesitated to commit their successors to a precedent which might eventually involve a much heavier responsibility. The voting on the matter was equal but by the Chairman's casting vote the committee's recommendation was confirmed and the teachers will receive the reduced amount. They have undoubtedly a grievance but the diffi- culty on which the whole question turns is that of training college accommodation. It is high time that something was done to obviate the unpleasant necessary of having to reject so many candidates who have qualified for the college course, and the sooner the question is tackled the better will it be for our educational system.</p>
        <p>The dignitaries of the State THE POOR Church are working them- DECEASED WIFE'S selves into a fine frenzy SISTER, over the deceased wife's sister, and pursuing that unfortunate lady with a most un-Christian malevolence. The Primate has issued a letter for the guidance of the clergy in which he says: " We 3 hall in my judg- ment, serve best the interests of the Church and people by not solemnising such marriages, or furthering their solemnisation in church." This view is also expressed by the Bishops of London and Salisbury, and provokes the " Daily News " to remark; "How easily the Anglican dignitaries can set aside the law of the land when it suits their purpose is well shown by the pronounce- ments both of the Archbishop of Canterbury and of the Bishop of London. Yet, we imagine, no two people in the country were more genuinely pained at the cantankerous conduct of the Passive Resisters." It seems to us that Churchmen who are thus prepared to oppose the law of the land and the will of the</p>
        <p>people are wanting in commonsense, and we slaall be surprised if they are found to be in any con- siderable number. It puzzles the simple layman to understand why the Church should oppose the natural union of the deceased wife's sister with her brother-in-law, and yet sanction the wedding of blood-relations like first cousins. It also puzzles a lot of people to com- prehend the teaching which sometimes refuses burial in consecrated ground to the innocent babe which happens to be called to Heaven before being baptised. Surely its qualifications for the Kingdom above should be sufficient for the holiest soil here *below. But we are getting accustomed to such a spirit of intolerance though it never fails to arouse resentment. The sympathies of the great mass of the people of this country are not with the Anglican Church in these matters. The deceased wife's sister has long been considered an unkindly-used lady, and now that she has come into her legal rights the opposition of the Church to the common law will probably not only be resented but futile. Every- , thing—so far as weddings in the Anglican Church are concerned—depends on the spit it of the indi- rvidual incumbent, and we believe the great majority will ignore the recommendations of the Primate, and submit to the dictates of reason, backed as they are by the opinion of the King and by the law of the land.</p>
        <p>We do not know whether DRUNKENNESS IN the public of Warrington is WARRINGTON. taking advantage of the new Chief Constable or not, but there is a lot of drunkenness in the town. There was plenty before Mr. Nicholls was appointed, and it seems to be on the increase, though we are far from suggesting that he is in any way responsible for the condition of things. In fact it Is questionable whether he can do much to lessen the evil, for the class of drunkenness about which we have to complain is not the ‘. incapable " sort which naturally invites the attention of the constable. The " drunks who are the greatest nuisance are they who have just drnnk enouLth to render them unsteady in their walk, loose and loud in their language, glassy of eye and reeking of breath. You can find them in every street and nearly every tram car after about ten o'clock at night, and to the decent sober-going public they are an abomin- ation. We travelled home in a car the other evening—it was a Sunday evening, too— with a motley crowd, more than half of whom were in various stages of drunkenness. They brought an atmosphere with them that was almost luminous, and they reeled into their seats with the usual maudlin grin. The most tolerant individual could hardly help feeling that these were not the type of men who should be free to use cars containing a fair proportion of ladies. What the remedy is, we are somewhat at a loss to say, but we should cer- tainly like to see the police hale a few of these topers off to the cells if, only for the night. " Durance vile" is the only place they are fit for. It is a pity that the same fate does not avail them as has been meted out in the town of Bayard, lowa. Stirred by fiery addresses on temperance, the women of the place met on Saturday night with their faces heavily veiled, and set out on a round of the saloons. On their round they found seven drunken men, whom they seized and tarred and feathered. Five of the victims were inconspicuous citizens addicted to habitual inebriety, but two were rich and prominent men of the district, to whom such an ordeal was degrading in the extreme. The women have sworn to repeat the treatment on any future offenders against sobriety, and they have constituted themselves the guardians of the com- munity so far as indulgence in liquor is concerned Would that the Bayard ladies could come and visit Warrington! They would find plenty of patients for the tar and feather treatment.</p>
        <p>A communication made to "RIGHT ABOUT a London weekly paper, and FACE." said to come from a leading Conservative, makes the somewhat startling but by no means unreasonable suggestion that during the present Parliamentary recess Tariff Reform will be thrown overboard by the Conservative Party. The session now at an end, it is observed, is the last to witness the ascendency of the Protectionists in the party councils. Sir A. Acland-Hood is to be got rid of for the return to the House of Commons of the old Tory whip,Lord Arthur Hill,marks the commence rneut of the reversion to the status quo ante, when the Tariff agitations of 1906 were unknown. It was Sir A. Acland-Hood who was mainly instrumental, in enabling Mr. Chamberlain to capture the Conservative organisations for Tariff Reform, a piece of smartness which has turned out most disastrously, for the Conservatives in the House of Commons are a mere handfu;, and even they are not all Tariff Reformers. However, according to the authority quoted, the process is to be reversed, and we cannot affect to be surprised by such an intimation, for even if the cause of Protec- tion were not already discredited, it would be made uproariously ridiculous by the Tariff comedy which is just now being played in Australia. But what will Mr. Balfour say to any demand for the reversal of the engines ? Oh, Mr. Balfour, it appears, is himself to give the word of command. There is a beautifully—we had almost written brutally—frank cynicism in the manner in which the leading Conservative suggests that the whole Tory Party will turn right-about- face when Mr. Balfour utters the command. " The veteran Member for West Birmingham will not, of course, again be seen in the House." "Alfred Lyttelton, Bonar Law, and Henry Chaplin will do as Mr. Balfour wishes,grateful for his finding them safe seats after last year's smash up. No one else counts." That is a most cruel way of putting it; and yet it seems to ring true. Mr. Chaplin has given up Protection before, and might, of course, give it up again ; and if he is not sufficiently loyal to " the cause " to stand by it in fair weather and in foul how can we expect the others to do so ?</p>
      </div>
