        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>A crusade to which Mr. Seddon intends to devote Ins energies during the recess (says the London correspondent of the "Manchester Guardian ") sug- gests that the passion for record-breaking is about to invade the sphere of political propaganda. Starting on the 16th September, the member for Newton means to speak every day, including Sundays, till some date in March in advocacy of Sir Charles mike's Shops Bill. The campaign is being organised by the National Union of Shop Assistants, and it will include thirty demonstrations on a big scale and a consider- able number of others of a subsidiary character.</p>
        <p>G One Sunday Morning a sergeant was in charge of some recruits, and he gave the customary commands for the men to form sections according to their religious "persuasions." "Church of England in front; Wesleyans next; Roman Catholics in the rear." The manceuvre executed,one yokel was left standing alone. "Well," said the sergeant, "and what . may you be waiting ? " "Please, sir, you haven't called the name of my persuasion yet." Oh, haven't I, really? And what • may you be ? " "Please, sir, I'm a Unitarian." "A what." "A Unitarian, sir." "Are you, really? Well, I don't know nothing about such fancy things as Unitarians— fall in with the Roman 'Catholics ! ' * • The commercial gentleman who paid £3 and costs at the Manchester County Police Court, on Monday, must feel ineffably foolish at present. On Sunday afternoon he turned the handle of the fire alarm out- side the Stretford Town Hall, and then walked away. The result was that about twenty men, a tender, a steamer, and a horse escape turned out from London- road, and galloped the whole way to the Stretford Town Hall. The serious part of the matter is that the call was one of the many false ones which the brigade has suffered lately. If a fire were really to start somewhere while the Brigade was out the result would be disastrous. o There are now no fewer than eight local government bodies on which women have .the right to serve. They are :—Town councils, county councils, the London County Council, the London borough councils, urban district councils, poor law boards, rural district councils, and parish councils. Eligibility to serve on these bodies consists in some cases in the electoral qualification; and in others in either the electoral or a residential qualification. Who will be the first woman Mayor, we wonder? It might be a good way out of the difficulty of finding a Mayor in Warrington, if a member of the fair sex were offered the honour. Mrs. Caudle might keep order in the Warrington Town Council more effectively than any mere male.</p>
        <p>* * We understand that there is a project on foot to establish in the town a Parliamentary Debating Society. Some of our readers will recollect that an excellent Society of this kind flourished in the town prior to 1881, when some of the leading men of the borough were active members. The two leaders of the Society were, if we remember rightly, Dr. Huson Moore and the Rev. Richard Pilcher. Possibly some old member of the Society could furnish us with further interesting information. It is a rather remark- able thing that Warrington has been without a Parliamentary Debating Society for so long, and we welcome the idea of re-establishing such a valuable institution. There are any number of budding politicians in the town, and a Debating Society would give Liberal, Tory, Socialist and Labourite an oppor- tunity of exchanging views and criticisms which might be beneficial to all.</p>
        <p>• No one more suitable than Canon Gibson Smith could have been chosen to preach the memorial sermon to the late Rev. E. J. Willcocks. He was one of the late headmaster's closest friends, having been associ- ated with him years ago in the work of the Grammar School, and the intimacy which continued to the end of Mr. Willcocks' strenous life, gave him an insight into the character of the man whose loss Warrington so deeply deplores. Canon Gibson Smith's tribute at the Parish Church on Sunday was exceptionally fitting, for it dealt not with Mr. Willcocks' public life, but with his noble Christian character and his shining example of steadfast devotion -to duty. One point which he justly emphasised was that in spite of the conscientious care and accuracy with which Mr.Willcocks did his very responsible work -lie never became a living machine, and no one could -wish for a more warm-hearted and affectionate friend.</p>
        <p>_ It is good news to learn that there is some prospect of the ridiculous wooden swing bridge on tne main road at Sankey Bridges being replaced with a new and more up-to-date structure. There has been a conference at Preston of the various authorities interested in the matter, and the County authorities have drawn up a scheme for a new bridge, apportioning the cost thereof to Warrington at 5,750. Alderman Tinnion assured the Conference of Warrington's moral support -but declined to accept any financial responsibility. We hope further information about the scheme, and Warrington's attitude,will be forthcoming at Tuesday's Council meeting. The improvement will 'be a very valuable one, but it is questionable whether it is worth £750 to the ratepayers of Warrington. Much 'depends on the size of contributions from other interested authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever Epidemic. For some weeks there has been an epidemic of scarlet fever in Warrington and unfortunately it con- tinues to spread. Numerous fresh cases have been reported this week and last night the patients in the Infectious Diseases Hospital who are suffering from the fever numbered 80.</p>
        <p>BIEL BUTTER, is. and is. ld.per lb. ASHLEY'S.—Advt• Labour Bureau. During the month of August 118 fresh applicants have registered their names for employment as com- p.ared with 101 during August 1906. Forty-six situations were offered by employers, and the number of situations filled through the Bureau was 30, being 13 men, 5 youths, 7 women, and 5 girls. There are pow on the register out of employment 178 persons, in the following trades : —General labourers 14, wire trade workers 16, iron trade workers 11, carters and vanmen 9, engine drivers and firemen 13, fitters and miscellaneous occupations 32, youths 26, girls 16, women for charing and daily work 37. The demand for domestic servants and youths, age 11 to 15, was far in excess of the supply, and the 13.ureau could only fill a percentage of the situations offered Domestic servants leaving situa- -tions, and boys leaving school might avail them- 'selves of the assistance offered, which would be to their and the Labour Bureau's mutual advantage EmploymeAt for men showed a slight decline during the month. A few of the principal industries still seep reducing their staffs whilst others are remaining on short time. The number of fresh applicants registered does not represent the whole of the persons who lost their situations during the month. Several do not register their names thinking that in their particular cases the Bureau is unable to give much assistance. Thrifty persons of good character should not hesitate to have their names registered, as it is only by this means the committee can ascertain the extent of unemployment and have the required information to enable them to deal with the problem.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Rev. J. Manning. Sincere regret has been felt in Wesleyan circles at the departure of this much respected minister. Ris good works have been many and varitd, but his connection with the town will be ever remembered for his inauguration of the successful popular Saturday night concerts, which have been the means of pro- • viding wholesome recreation for many who would otherwise be indulging in aimless street parading, I and public-house visitation ThelConcert Committee • took the opportunity, of showing their appreciation of their leader, by making him a presentation, and met for this purpose on Tuesday night at the Winmarleigh Cafe. The Secretary (Mr. Edwin Plinston) presided. ' Most of the members expressed !heir deep regret at Mr. Manning's departure, and their high appreciation of his services and affectionate regard for him. The presentation consisted of a mahogany revolving bookcase and polished silver-mounted salad bowl. In asking the rev, gentleman's acceptance of the gifts, Mr. Plinston voiced the feelings of all present, when Ile expressed the hope that Mr. and Mrs. Manning might have many years of happiness ; and strength to toil on in the service of the God they so faithfully served, and the Church they so much loved. The rev. gentleman thanked the Committee on behalf of his 'Wife and himself, for their kindness. He should ever remember his happy connection with those present, and hoped they would all continue their efforts, and be -true followers of their Lord and Master. After the tresentation the meeting took the form of a social, glit refreshments being partaken of.</p>
        <p>The Borough Member. Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, M.P. (says "The World Travel and Continental Supplement.") has secured rooms at the Victoria, Interlaken, Switzerland. He is President of the Swiss Golf Association. The golf meeting is shortly to be held, when teams will contest against the champion and holder of the Crosfield shield, which carries the championship of Switzerland Success of Local Volunteers. At the Lancashire County Rifle Assoiation meeting held at Altcar on Friday and Saturdayc last the fol- lowing won prizes: -Sergeant Drummer J.W. Plinston Legh Cup 200 and 600 yards 31 andl32,,total 63, won £1; Lord Lieutenant's Ist stage: 200, 500 and 600 yards ; 33, 32, 30, total 95; 2nd stage, 15 shots at 600 yards, total 66, won £2; Nursery 500 yards, 33; won £1 ; Nursery aggregate, 126 ' points; £l. Sergeant Major Ingram, Lord Lieutenant's Ist stage, 28, 33, 28, total 89 ;.won £1; also Sergeant J. Massey in same competition, 28, 31, 25, total 84 ;won £l.</p>
        <p>The British Band Festival. Well-known bands from all parts of England, Scotland, and Wales, to the number of about 150, will compete at the British brass band festival to be held at the Crystal Palace on Saturday, Sepfember 28th,and the total prizes amount to £1,750. The contest for the 1,000 guinea challenge trophy promises to be exceptionally keen, ond among the famous bands that have signified their intention of competing are : Crosfield's Soap Works and Gossage s Soap Works. Besides the great championship competition, eight other contests have been arranged. One of the most interesting of these will be the boys' band contest, which is open to all boys' bands, whether brass or reed.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANca.snutz.—A.dvt.</p>
        <p>Piano Hiring Case. Ms Honour Judge Shiress Will, KU., at the War- rington County Court on Thursday, heard the adjourned case in which Messrs. Crane and Sons, Ltd., Scotland-road, Liverpool, the well-known piano- fcrte manufacturers, sued Mr. Maurice Ruben, of 78, Knutsford-road, Warrington, picture dealer, for the specific return of a Rosewood cottage .pianoforte; of the value of 28.9 a.—At the previous hearing Mr. Arthur Browne, for the plaintiffs, informed his Honour that the defendant did not appear, nor was he repre- sented.—Evidence waa now called showing that formal demand had been made for the specific return of the piano. Negotiations had been conducted with Noah Ruben, but Maurice, said Mr. Browne, had still not turned up from his holidays.—His Honour made an order for the specific return of the piano, with costs. Rescue from Drowning. A plucky rescue from drowning was witnessed at Bridge Foot on Saturday afternoon. P.O. Roberts was on duty at about four o'clock, when he saw a large number of persons in an excited state standing near he iron rails running alongside the river. fie ran to the spot and saw a boy struggling in the water. The danger of the lad, who had accidently fallen off the rails, had been seen by a youth named Thomas , Groarke, of 30, Oxford-street, and by two men named John Limy, 3, Bryn's-yard, Winwick-street, and John I Starkey, 58, John-street, all of whom jumped into the water. Groarke got to the boy .first, but his strength , failed him and .he had to return as best he could WI the side, being pulled out of the water by the con- stable. Lucy and Starkey brought the drowning boy I o the side, and he, too, was pulled out of the water, and accompanicid home, apparently little the worse for his immersion. 0-rent-credit is due not only to Groarke but to Lucy and Starkey for their promptitude. SAUSAGE SEASONnOW commenced. ASFILEY'S—Advt. Prize Distribution at Daresbury. The annual distribation of prizes provided from the School Endowed Fund took place the other afternoon the awards being handed to the successful scholars by Mrs. Wynne Jones and Mr. John Cooper. As usual, the prizes were given in money; books and presents awarded for regular, attendance, gopd conduct and general efficiency. The following 39 children attended every time the school was open and consequently secured a substantial money prize : —Harry Littler, Harry Potts, Granville Furnival, Doris Webb, Richard Hastings, Edward Astbury, Herbert Green, Lewis Boaler, Ethel Kitchingman, Lucy Fogg, Leonard Jones, Jessie Fogg, Mary Faulkner, Frank Houghton,'i Evelyn Hastings, Rose Wilkinson Annie Taylor,Aroos Henshall, Thomas Houghton, Elsie Prosser, Reginald Forster, Thomas O. Jones, Winifred Done, Sydney Davies, Alfred Green, Mary G. Potts, William Witter, Frank Virilklnson;Norah Tarran, Norah Oulton, Gladys Prince, Catherine Prince, Annie Faulkner, Ellen Jamesoe, Thomaa Jones, Ethel-- Done,-- Constance Taylor, William Boaler. Clara Hall.. At the conclu- sion of the distribution three cheers were given for Mrs. Wynne Jones and Mr. Cooper and the scholars dispersed.. WOOD MANTELs,Kitchen Ranges and Fire Grates, largest stock in the district.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Boy's Sudden Death. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the Polio° Station on Wednesday on William Burke (15), son of John Burke, iron-worker, 3, Chester-street.— Deceased's father said the boy was employed as a moulder's helper at Messrs. Richmond's works. At six o'clock on Friday evening he arrived home from work and complained of ear-ache. A drop of whiskey was poured into his ear and seemed to give relief. Deceased went to bed but at five o'clock on Saturday morning he awoke and again complained of the pain. He was persuaded to stay in bed until noon when he hull some milk, brandy and soda. He lay on the sofa intil five o'clock when he commenced to vomit. lAt ten o'clock he went to bed and witness sat up with him. At two o'clock on Sunday morning deceased became restless, his face twitched,his fingers clenched, he struggled for a time and became quiet. Witness sent for a doctor but was unable to get one and deceased died at 5.30.—Dr. Smith said he had made a post-mortem examination and found an abcess inside the ear. The inflammation from it had spread inwards and resulted in meningitis which was the cause of death.—The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence. GARDEN TENTS.—Tennis, Croquet, and other out- door sports.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Beautiful Warrington Society. • The second annual exhibition of flowers and plants will be held, as will be seen from our advertisement columns, this afternoon at the Drill Hall. The exhi- bition will be opened by Mrs. Lyon at 2.30. At the opening ceremony it is anticipated thsre will be present, the Mayor and Mayoress, Mrs. Ireland Black- borne (the wife of the President, Colonel R. Ireland 'Blackburn°, C. 8.) and a large gathering of ladies and gentlemen of the town and surrounding districts who are interssted in this worthy movement. Splendid entries have been received and the judges will have a difficult task as there is keen competition in the various classes. The school children's classes for plants and wild flowers will again be a feature of the exhibition. For lovers of music a great attraction is provided in the ground orchestra which will be under the conductorship of Mr. Tom Boothroy. The pro- gramme is one of the finest we have seen for a long time and includes the famous Peer Gynt Suite, some of the gems. of the'grand operas and selections from "The Gondoliers" and "Yeomen of the Guard." Teas and refreshments will be provided by Mrs. Powys. Altogether an hour or two will be well spent at the Exhibition and it is to be hoped there will be a record number of visitors. FOR PURE PORK SAusA.GE,try ASHLEY'S.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Charge Against a Warrington Labourer. William Lawrence, tramping labourer, was remanded at Aberystwyth on Monday on a charge of unlawfully wounding Hugh Hughes, farmer, Cwmbwsa, Penrhyncoch. For the past two weeks the prisoner had been working for Hughes, and drew some money on Friday, with which he proceeded to have a spree. On the following day he returned to the farm, and attacked his employer with a stick about the head. Hughes had three nasty cuts on the head, and was unable to appear in Court. A further charge of breaking thirty-six panes of glass at the farm will also be proceeded with at the next hearing. Lawrence was again brought up on Thursday—lt .appeared from the evidence of Mr. Hughes, who came into court with his head heavily bandaged, that the prisoner had been in his employ for five weeks previous to August 23rd. On that day he drew some money and went off to get drink. The next day, when all hands were busily engaged in the hayfield, prisoner returned. Mr. Hughes told him not to smoke in the hayfield, and his refusal ' to give prisoner any more work enraged the latter, and he struck the farmer in the face. He followed this with other blows, and when spoken to by one of the farmhands swore he would kill the lot. In the mean- time Mr. Hughes retreated to the farm, but was over- taken in the lane, where the prisoner rained down blows upon his head with a heavy stick.—Mrs. Hughe then went into the box and told how the prisoner came to the farm in a violent rage, and broke thirty-six panes in the windows. —Prisoner was sent to goal for four months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
