      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 31, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 31, 1907</p>
        <p>Street Widening, Etc. Major J. Stewart, R.E., an Inspector of the Local Government Board will hold an inquiry at the Town Hall on Tuesday next at 10 a.m. into the applications of the Corporation for sanction to borrow £40,140 for street widenings and improvements, and £3,071, the sum required in connection with the proposed boiler plant extensions at the Electricity Works. FOR Wrras HIRE BACON try ASHLEY'S,Sankey-st.—Advt. Brass-Bands at Belle Vue. Twenty brass bands will compete at Belle Vue next Monday. For half-a-dozen of them there are rich prizes waiting —a cup, and various sums of money, amounting in all to £350. Brass band contests are held in many centres nowadays, but the Belle Vue gathering easily holds the first position. The fashion was set at Belle Vue half a century ago; to be exact, this is the 55th year of the series. Many noted bands will compete including Crosfield's (Warrington) and our readers will join with us in wishing the local bandsmen success. Alleged Theft at Warrington. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, Arthur William Talbot of po settled abode, was charged with stealing is. 9d. the:property of William Fitzgerald., The chief constable said the prisoner went to Fitzgerald's house in Thevvlis-street and asked for lodging. He (the chief constable) had no doubt that prisoner hdd made many false statements and he asked for a remand in order that the police may be able to make further inquiries. Prisoner slept with Fitzgerald who got up at 7 o'clock leaving prisoner in bed. Fitzgerald afterwards missed the money. Prisoner who had no objection was remanded for a week. _ _</p>
        <p>I--CHINA. TEA SETS.—Special show of latest art patterns. See windows, J. S. GARLAND; 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Lord Roberts' Thanks. Mr. R. Thompson, of the Alexandra Vaults, a mem- ber of the Warrington branch of the National Service League, has received the following letter: —" Dear sir, —I desire to express to you personally my sincere thanks for the trouble you have taken in increasing the membership of the league in response to my appeal. As you know, my great obje3t is to induce my colleagues to realize the risks we are running by our unpreparedness and the necessity for personal military, training as the only means of averting danger. Trust- ing you will continue to aid me in this work, I remain yours very truly, Roberts, F. 11., President." PUBLIC) BILNEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR Bs* BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt, Captain Withington's Jubilee. At Leigh on Monday Mr. C. Eckersley, on behalf oj the county magistrates of 'Leigh and district, presented Mr. T. E. Withington, of Culcheth Hall, with an oak and brass English chime clock and bracket, a silver oval two-handled flower bowl on ebonised plush, a silver oval chased centre dessert dish and two side dishes, and a silver plain reading lamp,in commemora- tion of his having completed fifty years' service as a county magistrate. Mr. Eckersley said he could bear testimony to the able maMner in which Mr. Withing- ton had carried out his duties and to the great courtesy and geniality he had always displayed. The county , magistrates in the district felt they could not allow , that opportunity to pass without showing in some tangible form the esteem they had for him. Mr. I Wiihington, in reply, said he would &amp;manila to do his ' best for the district. Unjust Scales. At the Borough Court on Monday before the Mayor I and other magistrates, William Butler, 165, Wellfield- I street, green grocer, was charged with having in his possession for trade purposes a pair of unjust scales. Mr. Elijah Place, chief inspector "of weights and measures, said on Saturday evening he was in a back street off Wellfield•street when he saw the defendant who had the scales on his cart. They were abso- lutely unfit for use. In April he was cautioned and he promised to destroy them. Mr. White (magistrates' clerk) : Are the scales for or against the purchaser ? Defendant: lam robbing myself. Mr. Place said they could be used both ways. The Mayor said it was a very serious case. Defendant had been given a chance. He would be fined 10s. 6d. and costs; the scales to be forfeited. I LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER • THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Order of Druids. At the ustwl meeting of the Druids Camp Lodge on Saturday the prize distribution in connection with the bowling main of the previous Saturday took place. There were present —District Trustee, Bro. William Bibby, -D C.S. Bro. E. Allen. D.T. Bro. R. Perdue, P.D.G.M.'s Bros. James Wilcock, Sam 6 Hankey, William Hankey, and John Bibby, and a very large attendance of members. At the conclusion of the ordinary Lodge business Bro. William Bibby took the chair. The D.C.S. Bro. Allen dealt at some length With the Actuary's report and its bearing on Friendly Societies in general. He believed that the Registrar was somewhat arbitrary in his demands for the build- ing up of huge funds, especially seeing that Friendly Societies were in such competition with Tontine Societies, and fully believed that Friendly Societies could be placed on a sound basis without drawing on the members any more than had already been done.— Bro. Bibby then presented the prizes to the winners as mentioned in our last issue, remarking that Friendly Societies or some of them did not attract members as they should do from the social standpoint. During the evening songs were given by Bros. Watson, Rose, Derbyshire, Armitage and Ball.—Votes of thanks were accorded the Chairman and D.C.S. for their presence and Bros. Packer, Hughes, and John Bibby for the manner in which they had carried out the howling arrangements, picnic, and social evening. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, &amp;C. Try J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-street, War- rington.—Advt. Exhibition of Pictures. Some remarkably fine pictures—the collection of Mr. Percy Davies—are at present on view in the show rooms of Mr. John Maginn, Rylands-street, and should be seen by all lovers Of art. The exhibition will con- tinue for a short time and eventually the pictures will be sold by auction. Of particular interest are three large works by Mr. George Sheffield, a well-known local artist, the chief being "On the downs "—a magnificent sea picture in charcoal, in which the fore- most objects are a number of great battleships with numerous smaller craft in view, the scene being made additionally impressive by the setting sun. Other notable specimens by the same artist are "The Baggage Waggon "—a road scene in winter—and " Wilmslow " which depicts a pleasant stream with a punt in the forefront, and the church in the distance. Mr. A. Pollitt, a painter of whom Warrington is justly proud, is represented by "On the Lugwy "—a beautiful painting of a dashing river amidst wild surroundings. One of the most valuable pictures in the collection is "The Domino Players," a fine creation, true to nature, by George Morland. It is priced at £l,OOO. Another striking work is the "View near Leominster" iit oils, by David Cox, which is valued at £900.. By the same painter there is "A windy day" in water colours. The exhibition also includes excellent specimens of the work of J. Sver, Mennesier, Sir Thomas Lawrence, P.R.A., Anne •Nasmyth, Samuel Scott, Bristowe, Arthur Meadow's, F. Danby, Hamilton Jay and Guiellett. There is a fine study in the nude by Sir dwin Landseer. P.S.A. Mr. T. L. Robins presided at the Wycliffe P S.A.. meeting on Sunday and spoke upon " Confidence:" He said that the only thing that could made business and all moral and spiritual work go (smoothly and well, was confidence. It was needful in politics, neeiful in the home, needful in all, institutions, and in all our dealings witfia our fellow men. Mr. Luckman, of Man- chester, gave the address upon" Whence? What? Whither ?" Darwin might be right or he might be wrong as to the origin of man. What concerned the speaker was "Am I growing nearer to God ?" He was more concerned as to what,he was. One should conscientiously do his duty, which was often attended with great difficulties, but one was always right in the end. gr. Bradley read the lesson, and Miss Armstrong gave two solos in a pleasing manner "God shall wipe away all tears," and "The Giver of Life." Mr. Payton presided at the organ. The Secretary expressed the hope of the meeting that the cloud of affliction resting upon the, speaker's home would soon be lifted. —There was a moderate attend- , ance at the weekly meeting of the Warrington Society when the Vice-President (Mr. A. Freeman) again occupied the chair and continued his instructive course of addresses on "Is there a God ?" He showed how conclusive evidence might be obtained from a study of the conscience, heart and soul of a man. Hiss L. Fairhurst was the soloist and sang in her most charming manner the two solos, "Nearer, my God, to Thee" and "Sun of my soul." Mr. H. Tonge was the accompanist. The speaker for the afternoon was Mr. R. Jones who gave an instructive-address on the words, "Who is on the Lord's side ? " The President and Messrs. Light and P. Dendy also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM</p>
        <p>CRACKERS.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY</p>
