      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MR. JOHN BURNS' RECORD.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN BURNS' RECORD.</p>
        <p>Fine Legislative Achievements.</p>
        <p>Without doubt the most successful Minister in the Cabinet is Mr. John Burns, the President of the Local Government Board, says the London Evening News." His record includes two Vaccination Bills, under which, among other things, the conscientious objector can now obtain exemption for his children by making a statutory objection. Then he has restored to women the right to sit upon public bodies. such as County and Borough Councils, giving the privilege of being elected Chairman or Mayor, as the case may be. This, however, is a very small matter compared with the Food and Public Health Bills, both of which con- cern the everyday life of the people. Mr. Burns brought in and carried a bill which gives the Local Government Board power .to make regula- tions giving the local sanitary authorities the duty of finding out how food is prepared for human consump- tion, to purify such places, and to insist upon cleanli- ness in the distribution and means of distribution.</p>
        <p>Another bill, originally presented as a private measure but reconstructed and adopted by Mr. Burns, is the , Public Health Bill of 100 clauses. This is the greatest safeguard we have had against disease through infection for the past twenty years. The Notification of Births Bill, presented by Lord Robert Cecil, and adopted by Mr. Burns, gives a better grip of the question of infantile mortality. This measure provides for the immediate notification of the birth of a child to the local medical officer.</p>
        <p>In the twenty months Mr. Burns has held office, he has been responsible for carrying through eleven public bills and 106 provisional orders, each of which may be described as a local Act of Parliament requiring indivi- dual care, attention, and judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>i THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>i THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING 24Th AUGUST, 1907. Total. Male. Female. Births Legitimate „ 47 26 21 Illegitimate 0 0 I Deaths 22 8 14 I Excess of births over deaths, 25 I The ages of mortality were :—Under 1, ; 1-5, 1; 5-15, 0; 15-25, 0 ; 25-65, 10 ; 65 and upwards, 4. Total, 22. The deaths included :-1 from whooping cough, 1 from diarrhcea, 1 from phthisis, 2 from cancer, 4 from lung diseases, 1 from premature birth, 2 from hear disease. 10 from all other causes. I The following cases of infoctions disease have been t reported during the week :—Scarlet fever, 13 cases, diphtheria, 2 cases, erpsipelas 1 case. Number of patients in hospital :—Scarlet fever, Borough 70,Newton 3 ; Rural 1; t0ta1,74. Enteric fever, Borough 0, Newton O. total 0. Diphtheria, Borough 0 Newton 0; total 0. Phthisis Borough, ; Newton, 0; total, 2. Totals, Borough, 72; Newton, 3; total, 76. Weather Return —Mean temperature 56Fah„and there- fore 3 deg. lower than the preceding week. Maximum temperature 68 Fah. on the 24th inst. Minimum tem- perature 46 on the 19th inst. Rainfall 1•06 inch. General direction of wind W.S.W.</p>
        <p>J. coorE HIBBERT, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>I WHITE CITY, MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>I WHITE CITY, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>I The White City Band Conceits, which are free to all, are one of the crowning glories of its many free attrac- tions. For next week, commencing Monday, September 2nd, in addition to the Northern Military Band, the Management. have secured at great expense, and take great pleasure in presenting for the first and only time in England, the 15th Light Horse Band of Calgary, Alberta, Canada. The Band consists of 45 musicians, amongst whont are many of the most eminent instru- mental soloists of the day. They have-received special permission of the Imperial Government. the Militia Council of Canada, and Lieut. Colonel James Walker, 15th Light Horse, to come to the Dublin Exhibition; and as they are leaving for home on Friday, September 6th, they will give a series of concerts afternoon and evening for four days only at the White City. The Band is one which will appeal to all lovers of detail. " Every lineament finely finished," the reeds in exquisite balance, the brass toned down to the very perfection of I mellow harmony, and the programme arranged to bring to .the fore the best points of organization. Captain Fred A. Bagley, as the Conductor of this superb body of musicians, is singularly free from conceit or self- consciousness. He is not only charmingly graceful when wielding the baton, but is a fine well-bred gentle- man, never resorting to any clap-trap in order to gain ' applause. His programmes are uniformly replete with 'good music, much that is light and popular, but none that can be descried as either vulgar or trashy, and b this jufficions arranging of programmes he has demon- strated his rioht to.membeiship ,111 the limited guild of capable, artistic conductors. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PADWITE.</head>
        <p>PADWITE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN C fIURC If —TI I 1: REV. J. MANNING' S FAREWELL.—Last Sunday morning the Rev. J. Manning who has left the Warrington, Wesleyan Circuit in accordance with the usual arrangements of that religious body.preached his farewell sermon. At the close he referred to the happy time he had spent in Warrington, particularly referring to the first twelve months he spent among the members of the Padgate Wesleyan Church, stating that there appeared to be in that church a moral atmosphere which enabled their ministers to give of their best. In closing he wished them happiness and prosperity in their family life, their business life and their church life. Much regret is felt by the members of the congregation and church, as well as in the Sunday school, at parting with the reverend gentle- man, whose labours had been much appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Services for Sunday.</head>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30 and evening 6.30, Rev. Thomas Miller (newly-appointed minister to the Warrington Circuit.) Golborne-street Baptist Church: Morning 10.30, evening 6.30, Mr. J. Harrison (Liverpool). Latchford Baptist Church: Morning and evening, Rev. J. Francis Matthews. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30 Rev. E. J. Smith, evening 6.30 Rev. Joseph Howard. Wycliffe Church Bewsey-street : Morning 10.30 and evening 6.30, Rev. J. Yonge. in the afternoon an open meting of the P.S.A.. Chairman Rev. J. Yenge; speaker Mr. W. G. Townend, J.P. (Mayor of Middleton, soloist Mr. W. H. Wellington. _</p>
        <p>At ijold-street Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday) there will be special services. The choir will give the anthems" Lord most holy " (Abt) and "The sun shall not go down." (Woodward) Mrs. Booth will contribute- solos. _</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Biptist Church: The Rev. John Francis Matthews will take the Wednesday evening service at Golborne-street Church every week until the end of the year. His subject for Wednesday next will be "What I think and believe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Hill Chile Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Hill Chile Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>1 The autumnal sermons will be preachedit Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel, on Sandal,' September 15th, at 2-30 , and 6.30 p.m. by the Rev. g. E. Lovell of Whitchnrch. In the morning there will be a Children's Service when ' special hymns will be sung by the children.</p>
        <p>---dirp,..-_,_.</p>
        <p>THE BRITISH INVESTOR. AND HIS SECURITIZS. In an interesting article from, tlit`3 pen of a well- known financial writer it was recently sought to demon- strate in a lucid manner the tendency of all the securi- ties of any one country to be affected by the same influences. For instance,. whereas all classes of British stocks have been moving steadily down during the past decade this has not been the case in many other countries where the price movements are controlled by entirely different causes. The writer in question, asserts, what he has already dealt with in "Investment an Exact Science," viz. that the capitalist who dis- tributes his investment; in high class securities over various quarters of the globe, suffers little from any depression which may beset the stocks of one particglar country, and the trade of the world as a whole, being continually on the increase, a fall in one of his holdings would be outweighed by a rise somewhere else. This is an interesting theory, and it provides food for thought to those investors who imagine they are insuring themselves against capital depreciation by spreading their investments over a large number of small holdings, without considering that they may all be subject to exactly the same trade influences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of Next Week's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening at 6 o'clock for the transaction of the following among other business.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH AUDIT.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Finance Committee is a copy of the report of the Borough Auditors, certifying briefly that the accounts, documents etc., are correct, and com- menting on the high state of efficiency in which the accounts are kept. The report is signed by Councillor Wilson (Mayor's Auditor) and Messrs. L. Bolton and E. England (elective auditors.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SCARLET FEVER.</head>
        <p>SCARLET FEVER.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee, Dr. Gaffikin submitted a report with reference to an outbreak of scarlet fever in the town. It was resolved that Sankey Sanatorium be used as a Convalescent Hospital for patients from Aikin-street Hospital. Resolved also that an honorarium of .C3O be granted to Dr. Gaffikin for extra duties discharged during the illness, and since the death of the late Dr. Gornall. Resolved, that an honorarium of 03 be granted to Mr, W. T. Flood, Chief Inspector of Nuisances, for the dis- charge of similar extra duties. SANKEY CANAL BRIDGE. SCHEME FOR A NEW STRUCTURE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, the Chairman reported that in company with Mr. Coun- cillor Wilkinson and the Borough Surveyor, he attended a Conference at Preston with reference to the widening of Sankey Canal Bridge. At the outset Mr. Alderman Tinnion informed the Conference that whilst the Corporation of Warrington wera in favour of the improvement they understood that all the County Authorities wished for was Warring- ton's moral support, and this they were bound to give. It appeared that the County Authorities had drawn up a plan and scheme for a new Bridge apportioning the cost thereof between various Authorities including Warrington, whose estimated share was £750. Mr. Alderman Tinnion, during the discussion which took place, informed the Conference that as this was the first intimation of the proposal to ask Warrington to con- tribute towards the improvement, he must at once state that he absolutely and definitely declined to take any responsibility, inasmuch as the Council bad not previously been consulted in the matter, but that if the plan and details were forwarded, the whole question would be considered by a Committee of the Council, and this course the Conference agreed to adopt. Resolved, that the action of the Chairman be approved, and that the best thanks of the Committee be given to the Deputation for their report. A BUST FOR THE MUSEUM. The Museum Committee has resolved that the special thanks of the Committee be given to Mr. J. Paul Rylands, Mr. W. H. Rylands, and Miss M. G. Rylands for their gift of a Marble Bust of the late Thomas Glazebrook Rylands, by J. Warrington Wood, and other portraits and specimens. NEW BATH AND BOWLING GREEN. _ _</p>
        <p>. . The Borough Surveyor has been instructed by the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee to prepare a scheme for the erection of a new bath and the laying out of a bowling green behind Garven House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MORE SALARIES INCREASED.</head>
        <p>MORE SALARIES INCREASED.</p>
        <p>The Water Committee has resolved that the salary of Mr. J. Gray, assistant water engineer, be increased from £l3O to £l5O per annum. The General Purposes Committee has resolved that the salary of Mr. T. H. Spinks, assistant town clerk, be raised from £l5O to £176 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MONKS, HALL'S RECREATION CLUB.</head>
        <p>MONKS, HALL'S RECREATION CLUB.</p>
        <p>The first annual flower show and sports promoted by the recreation club connected with Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., were held on Saturday, before a good crowd of employees and visitors. For- tunately the weather was most favourable for the occasion. The flower show was formally opened by Mr. F. W. Monks, the managing director of the Company, who complimented the workpeople on the initial success of the scheme. Great interest was centred in the flower show and it must be admitted that there was a fine display of flowers and plants. Special mention must be made of the collections of plants and flowers kindly sent by Mr. F. Monks, Mrs. F. W. Monks, Mr. H. Owen, Mr. G. Higgs. and Mr. C. Caird. A striking feature of the show was a decorated table taste- fully arranged by Mrs. G. Higgs and which was much ad mired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>_ Group of plants : 1, Birch ; 2, Walters. Foliage plant: 1, Darbvshire ; 2, Cooper. Flowering plant : 1. Talbot; 2, Broadhurst. Hanging plant: 1, Tate. Three greenhouse plants: 1, Walters; 2, Birch. Gladioli : 1, Millar ; 2, Fellows. Stocks: 1, Turner; 2, Bonehill. Pbloxes 1. Walters; 2, Bonehill. Asters: 1, Hickling; 2' Talbot. Carnations 2 1, H. Rathbone; 2, F. A. Rathbone. Pizoteeis- : 1, F. A. Rathbone. Eighteen violas : 1, Hickling ;2, Bonehill. Nine violas: 1, Cooper; 2, H, Rathbone. Pansies : 1, H. Ils.thbone. Six cactus dahlias: 1. Talbot; 2, Bonehill. Three cactus dahlias : 1, Bonehill; 2, ' Turner. Six roses, 3 TIT., 3 H.P. : 1, Darby, shire; 2, F. A. Rathbone. Six roses, H.P. : 1, F. Dumbell. Three roses, H.T. : 1, A. Darby- shire ; 1, F. A. Rathbone. Sweet peas : 1, A. Darbyshire ; 2, Dumbell. Three cut flowers: 1, Bonehill ; 2, Walters. Shallots: 1, Richards; 2, Fellows. Onions: 1, Dumbell ; 2,Turner. Peas : 1, Dumbell ; 2, James. Dwarf French Beans : 1, Clare. Broad beans : 1, Turner; 2, James. Red cabbage : 1, Hickling ; 2, James. Celery: 1, James; 2. Richards. Carrots : 1, Walters ; 2, Turner. Niarrovis : 1, James, Cauliflowers: 1, Hickling; 2, Turner. Parsnips : 1. Richards ; 2, Cooper. Potatoes: 1, Lee; 2, Hickling, Beetroot: 1, Richards ; 2, Walters. Tomatoes: 1. Hickling. Best plant grown on the works, Gleave ; Loaf (home made) : 1. Mrs. Shaw; 2 Mrs. Dixon. Small front garden: 1, Broadhurst; 2, dleave. Small back garden : 1, Darbyshire ; 2. Rathbone. Allot- ments (Hood-lane) : 1, Turner ; 2, Bonehill. RESULTS OF SPORTS. • Schoolboys, Members' Children (up to 14 years).- Ist heat: 1, T. Grimshaw, 11; 2, B. Deakin, 3. 2nd heat: 1, 0. Broadhurst, 2 ; 2, J. McCann. 2,Fival : 1, Grimshaw ; 2, Deakin; 3, Broadhurst. 80 Yards Handicap (open to all mambers).--Eleat 1, C. Jones, 8; heat 2, H. Lindley, 4 ; heat 3, H. Platt, 7 ; heat 4, W. Skidmore, 4; heat 5, W. Forster, 4. Final : 1, Lindley; 2, C. Jones; 3, H. Platt. 80 Yards Handicap for Youths up to 18.-lst heat: 1, A. Penn; 2, J. Marsh. 2nd heat: 1, A. Suminerfield; 2, T. Wall. 3rd heat: 1, J. Heywood; 2, E. Evans. Final: 1, Penn; 2, Heywood; 3, Evans. 80 Yards Handicap for Members over 50 Years.- Final: A. Roberts; 2, H. Osborne; 3, J. Narroway. Half-Mile Handicap (open to all members).-Ist heat: 1, W. Forster (30) ; 2, J. Hill (60); 3, H. Gerrard (scratch). 2nd beat: 1, A. Spencer (60) ; 2, W. Earle (50) ; 3.' W. Skidmore (15). Final: 1, Gerrard ; 2, Earle : 3, Spencer. 80 Yards Egg and Spoon Race for ladies employed by the company, and daughters of members.-Final: 1, G. Moran; 2, G. Hatfield ' • 3, C. Turner. Quarter Mile Obstacle (open to all members).-lst heat : 1, H. Whitfield (5) ; 2, J. Green (GO) ,• 3, W. Marsh (0); 2nd heat: 1. D. Brown (30) ; 2, G. Cox' (10) ; 3, W.Stewart (30) ; 3rd heat: 1, T.Wall (15) ; 2, G. Melia (10) ;3, J. 011 very (20). Final : 1 D. Brown; , 2, W. Marsh; 3, H. Green. At the close of the sports Mr, F. W. Monks presented the prizes to the respective winners. . Praise is due to Ar. A. Pe.nberton who ably dischai•ged the duties of hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SHOCKING CHILD NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>" Meant to Swing for Her."</p>
        <p>At the Borough Court on Tuesday before Mr. J. Rymer Young and other magistrates, Annie and Henry Williams, Webster's Yard, Dial-street, were charged with neglecting their three children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr. Browne,who prosacuted on behalf of the N.S.P. C.C. said Inspector Cox had taken money out of his own pocket to provide food for the children. Henry told the Inspector he had no proper alarm to waken him in a morning so the inspector bought him one for 3s. 6d. Three days afterwards Mr. Cox went to the defendant's house and found he had pawned the clock for is. 3d. The only food in the house was a bit of bread. When defendants were told about their neglect the male defendant said he wanted to be locked up for it and he did not intend to work again. Inspector Cox said he had had this case under his supervision for three years. The children were very badly neglected. He once went into the house and found the male defendant drunk on a - bed. They had a boy aged 14 but he had gone to live with his grandmother. P.C.'s. Burton and Andrews corroborated Mr. Cox's evidence. Henry Williams said when he came home from work he always found his wife under the influence of drink, but she was not drunk. She would go away for three days at a stretch and leave him to look after the children, which he was not able to do. On Monday night be heard a noise in the kitchen and on going to ascertain the cause he found his wife on the hearthstone, drunk. He was willing to look after the children to the best of his abilities. Mrs. Williams said her husband had been spending his money in drink and she had to go out working for food. Her husband told her that she might as well go for he was going to" swing "and it would be for her. The Chief Constable said both the defendants had been twice previously before the court. The bench sentenced the prisoners to four month's with hard labour each.</p>
        <p>A GOOD EYE TEST IS to read the small print in the newspaper 10 inches from the eye, not farther, each eye to be tested separately. If you cannot do this you should at once consult JOHNSTON WATSON. Eyesight and Spectacle Specialist, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LICENSING PROSECUTION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Runcorn Police Court, before Messrs. J. Littler and W. Timmins, William Mercer, licensee of the Holyhead Harbour Inn, High•street, was snmmoned for permitting drunkenness on his licensed premises. Mr. Griffiths, Chester, prosecuted, and the defendant was represented by Mr. Briggs, Stockport. The defend- ant pleaded not guilty. Mr. Griffiths stated that about 4.20 on August Bank Holiday, Inspector Dean, when passing the above house, heard a noise inside. A few minutes later he and police-constable Williamson entitred the house, and in the passage they found a man named Taylor leaning against the wall. Mrs. Mercer was also in the passage. The inspector callei Mrs. Mercer's attention to the man's condition, and she said to Taylor "I thought you had gone to bed." Taylor made no reply. Inspector Dean said it was time he went to bed lor outside, as he was drunk. The landlady replied that he had not been served there. It was ascertained that the man lodged there. He was then taken into custody, and the next morning was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk on liceased premises. Later the licensee and his son-in-law went to the police station. The former said Taylor had not been served in the house, and the latter said he had taken Taylor there to see a captain. The defence was that the man was a lodger, and that he had not been served in the house. He had been" shipping" with a captain.—The bench dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY. I The quarterly session of the South-West Lancashire District Lodge was held on Saturday last in the Baptist Church, Widnes, representatives from all parts of the district being present. Mr. J. Oliver, D.C.T., in his report, adopted a very hopeful tone, there being sins of progress and a great awakening of interest. The district secretary (Mr. Morris Jones) and the district superintendent of juvenile work (Mr. George Clarke) dealt with the statistical position, considerable dis, cussion ensuing. Reference was made to the recent visit of the naval members connected with the channel Fleet and the arrangements for their entertainments, also the visits and meetings addressed by Dr. Crother and Dr. Elssvorth, representatives of the United States Government to the international congress held at Stockholm, in addition to Miss Forsyth, from Boston, U.S.A., who represented the International Lodge at congress. Successful gatherings had been held in Liverpool, to welcome them on their arrival, and also to bid them farewell on the return journey, the latter meeting being presided over by Mr. A. Black. Mr. H. Hodgson, district councillor, having resigned owing to removal to Dewsbury, Mr. Templemure was elected to fill the vacancy. Finances were dealt with by the dis- trict treasurer (Mr. G. A. Walker), and arrangements made for special mission work. The agenda not being completed, the remainder of it will be considered at a special meeting. RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. FOOTBALLER FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs. John Littler (presiding) and Walter Timmins. Samuel Joyce, Poole-lane,a well-known local footballer, was fined ss. for having been drunk and incapable.— P.C. Brown said he had to carry him home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MUSICIAN IN TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>MUSICIAN IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>Thomas William Nlorris, a well-dressed man, • who makes a living by playing a violin in the street, was charged with using obscene language that day at half- past twelve o'clock. He strongly denied the charge.— Charles Ball, High-street, said that at half-past twelve that day the prisoner was playing a violin in High- street and his wife was with him playing the harp. He ordered some children away and he used bad language to them. A man passing warned him not to use such language and was sworn at for his trouble. Witness went to him and told him that the police in Runcorn were " down " on bad language and he had better be careful. He then swore at witness and wanted to light him.—Harriet Jones, Ellen Ball, and Frances Roberts all stated that the prisoner used filthy language to them.—Prisoner strongly denied the charge and said he had simply told the children to go away because he did not want them to obstruct the footpath. He and his wife were respectable people.—Sarah Jane Morris said she had not heard her husband use any bad language to the children though he ordered them away. They tried to be very civil. They had played all over England and had splendid references.—The Chairman : We think you have been a bit excited and forgotten yourself. You will be fined ss. Be careful in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>"BOOKMAKERS" FINED AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>"BOOKMAKERS" FINED AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, John Levy and William Hopkins of Liverpool, pleaded not guilty to a charge of unlawfully frequenting a field off Norman-road, Runcorn, for the purpose of betting on Saturday,—Albert Bratt, 2, Ashbridge-street, Runcorn, said he was a Volunteer. He saw the defendants at the sports on Saturday. They were betting. They had hooks and pencils, and there was a crowd: round. He saw plenty of money pass. He gave information to the poliee.—P.C• Brown said he was a comp3titor in the 120 yards race at thesports. and was consequently pre- sent in plain clothes.—The Magistrates' Clerk: Did we win or lose? (laughter.)—The Superintendent : We lost, sir. (Laughter.)—Witness said he saw Hopkins take two bets. When he left thd, field witness followed him. They went to Cawley's Bridge, where they were going to stand and pay out. They were searched at the station. and a book was found in the possession of Hopkins.— Levy asked why he was not cautioned or arrested on the ground. He was not arrested until after the sports were over. Superintendent Ennion produced a bundle of betting tickets, which, he said, were found on Levy. In the possession of Hopkins were five two-shilling pieces, three shillings, and two threepenny-pieces.— Hopkins denied betting. He had a fish and poulterer's business in Liverpool and had no need to bet. Ile heard Levy shouting " Even money Ditchfield," and he went to see what was the matter,when the policeman arrived. He was asked to walk to the station. (Laughter.) He frequented sports a bit, but not to bet. Each of the prisoners were fine-1 10s. and 10s. costs with the alterna- tive of 14 days' bald labour. WIDNES HORTICULTURAL SHOW.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, the eighth annual exhibition of plants, flowers, bread, vegetables, honey, eggs, &amp;c., promoted by the Widnes Horticultural Society,was held in a spacious tent on a field at Halton View. Rain fell slightly, although in previous years the weather has not been very favourable. It was opened in the afternoon by Councillor David Lewis, who paid a high compliment to the educative and elevating work the show performed. The exhibits were of excellent quality, though the season had been bad. There were only 400 entries—a slight decrease on last year. The tent presented a charm- ing appearance, the plants and flowers were splendidly arranged, and in the vegetable classes some excellent potatoes were shownparticularly the white kidney variety Peas were good, and some fine shalots were also shown. Good-quality lettuce and carrots were also on view. Large turnips, cauliflowers, and cabbages were only of indifferent quality. The flowers were very tine, the palm being carried off by cactus dhalias exhibited by Mr. H. Finney. Mr. A. J. Woolaston, Mr. A. Beesley, Mr. R. Clam, Mr. H. Rice, Mr. J. Herriman, and Mx. T. Herriman were amongst the most successful exhibitors. Asters and stocks were fair. Collections of plants were good, Mr. Clare's collection (first prize) being much admired. Baskets of flowers were also amongst the prettiest features of the show. St. Mary's Prize Band played during the afternoon, and the boys of the Farnworth Nautical School gave a gymnastic exhibition. Mr. Gossage's silver challenge bowl was won by Mr. A. Beesley (41 points), most points in vegetable classes (amateur). Mrs. Warren's silver challenge bowl, for flowers, was w.on by Mr. R. Clare, and the Brick and Tile Company's *vases, for gardens, by Mr. H. Finney. VOLUNTEER SPORTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>compete in the Widnes and District Alliance League. Next year it was proposed to form a cricket club. He hoped both clubs would be a credit to Runcorn. (Applause.) Mrs. Timmins then very graciously distributed the prizes, after which an exhibition of tilting the bucket was given.</p>
        <p>In-the-eVening the band played for dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>--- FREE DOCTORS FOR ST. HELENS MOTHERS. At the meeting of the St. Helens Health Committee, on Wednesday, it was reported that arrangements were being made by which midwives would be empowered, at the expense of the committee, to call in a doctor in maternity cases when they found it impossible to obtain the attendance of the poor law doctor. —Mr. H. H. Peet pointed out that the guardians had made provision for this, and that it would lead to overlapping.—The Chairman (Alderman Massey) said that ought to be avoided.—Mr. Peet said that a sub-committee of guardians was farther considering the matter.—Mr. Hamblett asked if it would lead to the pauperisation of the people who were attended by a doctor under these circumstances If it did, he would oppose it until that was altered.—Dr. Buchan said it would not.—The Town Clerk (Mr. Andrew) said that the clause in the Act permitted it being done without carrying such a stigma JUMPED IT THE ST HELENS CANAL. On Saturday afternoon a farmer named John Owen, when working about 250 yaris from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Havannah, Parr, saw a man jump into the water and sink. Owen went to the place, but could see nothing of the man. He immediately sent a messenger for the police, and Police-constable Carroll, of Haydock, recovered the body at 6.25‘p.m. The man appears to be between fifty and sixty years of age, sft. 71n. tall, dark brown hair, blue eyes, fresh com- plexion, but had no teeth in front. He wore corduroy trousers, brown jacket, black vest, brown muffler, with clogs on his feet. He possessed a peculiar, iron-hafted knife, and from his appearance it is thought that he had recently been an inmate of some public institution. The body lies at the mortuary to await identification. SILLY VOLUNTEERS. DISMISSED FROM THEIR CORPS. William Efulme and William Mercer, of Prescot, were charged at St. Helens on Tuesday for being in posses- sion of loaded firearms in a carriage, on the London and North-Western Railway between Widnes and St. Helens. Mr. W. Hutchen defended the young men who had been recruit members of the 2nd V.B. South 'lnca- shire Regiment. For the prosecution, it was stated that on July 20th the two youths, when returning from firing practice at Widnes, put ball cartridges into their rifles between Farnworth and Clock Pace, and shot out of the window. Mr. Elutchen said the young men had pleaded guilty, and they were very sorry for what they had done. They had only been members of the Volunteer Icorps for about three months, and the Volunteer colonel had taken a serious view of the matter, and, after a court-martial, had dismissed them from the regiment. They had been disgraced, and felt their position. They did not intend to frighten or hurt anybody. The magistrates said it was a very foolish and dangerous act. These young fellows ought to have had more sense. They would be each fined 40s. and lls. costs. ALLEGED JEWELLERY FRAUDS AT ST. HELENS. BARMAID AND THE CHEAP GOLD RING. At St. Helens, on Saturday, Abraham Lassman, Chesterton-road, London, was charged with obtaining two sums of money by falsely representing flash • jewellery to be-genuine gold. The Chief Constable prosecuted, and called a barmaid of the White Horse Hotel, College-street, who said that on June 210, she purchased a ring from prisoner for 3s. He said distinctly that he represented Messrs. Samuels, Man- chester, and that the ring was genuine, and was 18 carat. A man named Roberts,who was also in the house, bought a ring for 3. on the same representation. It was afterwards found that the rings were made of brass. —The Chief Constable said that Messrs. Samuels were frequently the victims of this kind of thing, and they had sent a representative to give evidence against the prisoner. —Mr. E. W. Swift, who defended, said that on the circular which defendant was exhibiting the name of Samuels and Sons was accompanied with the London address, and that there was nothing about Manchester on it. The witnesses also admitted that Messrs. Samuels and Sons were manufacturers of the finest • imitation gold and diamond jewellery. Mr. Swift urged that it was only on this circular that the sale was made, and denied that the prisoner .stated that he represented Samuels of Manchester.—The bench com- mitted the prisoner for trial at the quarter sessions, allowing bail in one surety of £lOO and two sureties of £5O each, providing the sureties were satisfactory to the police.</p>
        <p>• • TFUNERAL OF THE LATB,NIR. COTTON, J.P.. The fcneral of :11r. John Cotton, J.P., took place at St. Helens Cemetery-at noon on Saturday. Mr. Cotton was seventy-i3ight years of age, and had been a prominent figure in the town for over fifty years. The ser ;ice was conducted by the Rev. W. IL Jeffrey, and the mourners included Dr. John Cotton, Mr. Charles Cotton, Mr. James Cotton (sons), Miss Cotton (daughter), Dr. Latham, Messrs. R. Conk, James Cook, Neil, S. S. Pennington, G. Bramwell, D. M. F. Gaskin, F. Morton,. F. Hilton, aud J. Itowat. Amongst those present at the graveside were Messrs. J. B. Leach, J.P., B. B. Glover, J.P.. J. Boardman; J:P:(Rainford), Colonel J. W. NV-rear, J.P.. R. H. Darie, H. Rawlinson, H. Hill, John Cook, I'. lonrieff, W. Hutchings, and F., Rollwagen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>INSUR A.NCE OF CONSgNIPTIVF, PERSONS.</head>
        <p>INSUR A.NCE OF CONSgNIPTIVF, PERSONS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, the United Provident Assurance Company, of Manchester, were sued for £l5, by Mrs. Alice Riley, of 106, Parr-street.—Mr. Hutchen said Mrs. Riley was a widow. She insured her son with this company, and the premiums were paid by her up to twelve months ago. Plaintiff was in very poor circum- stances, owing to her sons being out of work, and at this time a neighbour named Mrs. Leonard undertook with the company's agent to continue the payment of the premiums. The son died, on July 22 this year. In, May. Mrs. Leonard thought it advisable, seeing that Riley was in a had state othealth, to obtain an assigu- ment of the policy. She informed the superintendent, and the agent accepted. sixpence in premiums. After-- wards, however, the company told Mrs. Leonard that they could not recognise the assignment.—Mr. H. L., Riley defended, and said the defence was that the so", prior to being insured, suffered from consumptior. The company's agent was never informed that the man suffered from chronic phthsis, or that he had been, treated at the workhouse infirmary for that disease. The young man eventunlly died from consumption.— The Bench decided in favour of the company, but expressed the opinion that the company should deal generously with plaintiff because ths agent accepted: the business, and it was his anxiety to get clients thav was practically responsible for the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EARLESrOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESrOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. GARDEN PARTY.</p>
        <p>Rainford Hall, the residence of Colonel and Mrs. R.. Pilkington will be the scene of a large gathering ot Y.M.C.A. people on September 7th, invitations being issued to the members of St. Helens, Earlestown, Ashton, and Prescot Associations to a garden party on that date.</p>
        <p>TRAP FOR MOTORISTS. On Thursday morning, at Newton-le-Willows Police Court, Thomas Palmer, chauffeur to Mr. Gordon Houghton. Tarporley, was fined £5 and costs for exceed- lug the speed limit. lie was driving back from Haydock Park races on the afternoon of the 10th instant, and between Newton-le-Willows and Hulme Bar,Warrington covered three miles in 6min. 16sec., which worked out at 26 miles 1,052 yards an hour. Mr. Hugh Edmund E. Peel, J.P.,of Bryn-y-pys-Overton,Flintshire (represented by Mr. A. G. Co!beck, solicitor, Wrexham), was mulcted in £2 and costs for driving over the same distance la smin. 59sec., which worked out at 30 miles 144 yards an hour. There were summonses against Mr. Charles T. Garland, a Warwickshire gentleman, for driving at a dangerous speed, ai\d also for reckless driving, but as defendant did not appear the cases were adjourned for a forthight, and the day's costs allowed against him. Mr. J. W. Knowles prosecuted for the police. RESULTS. 220 Yards Scholars' Handicap.—Heat 1: 1, A. Lyon FATHER AND DAUGHTER AT LAW. Runcorn); 2, J. G. Moores (Runcorn). Heat 2: 1, H. , DISPUTE ABOUT A PIANO. Hughes (Runcorn) ;2, P. Prica (Runcorn). Heat 3:1, , At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before W. E. Smith (Widnes) ; 2, T. Leach (Widnes). Heat 4: his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Richard Beer, 2.3, 1, F. Mort (Runcorn) ; 2,J. D. Holt (Runcorn). He,at Tamworth-street, Earlestown, sued his daughter 5 (Weston) ; 2, G. Harrison (Widnes). Margaret Singleton Forshaw and her husband, James Fi:nal L : 3. Forshaw,Haydock, for the specific return of a piano 200 Yards Detachment Handicap.—Heat 1 : 1, value 22.8 and £2 for detention.—Mr. H. J. Longton Private Arthur Richardson; 2, private T. Battersby. appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. H. Lindon Riley, Heat 2 : 1, Private J. Sefton; 2, Oorporal G. ComleY. barrister, defended. Neither the plaintiff nor the IF, Hughes n ; 2, Smith ; 3, Mort. Heat 3 : 1, Private E. Poole ; 2, private W. Dutton. defendant appeared, and it ultimately transpired that Final : 1, Sefton (eight yards) ; 2, Richardson (15 both were ill.—The point in dispute was the ownership yards) ;, 3, Comley (15 yards). of the piano which, it was stated, Mrs. Forshaw took away 120 Yaids Flat Handicap (open).—Winners of heats: with her on her marriage. Plaintiff alleged that it S. G. Deakin (Runcorn), j. Didsbury (St. Helens), H. belonged to him, as he had been paying for it under'. Hartley (Warrington), T. Postings (West Cheshire H.), hiring agreement, whereas his daughter submitt--i. ~.......0 C. W. Hawkins (( looter), J. Derwent (Pembroke HO, it was her property.—Richard Beer, son of the plaintiff, Final: 1, Postings (6i yards) ; 2, Deakin (7} yards) ; said Mrs. Forshaw was not earning anything up to the time of her marriage.. She kept house while the other'a went out to work. He had seen his father send nostal orders to pay for the piano. By Mr. Riley : He did 3se, cDoinddshpulrayce(.lo4,myaerdls2).2.Wssoecn. easily. Fine fight for not hear that his father we- present when the piano was yFalradts )H;a Handicap a? i(;:o.p e Jepson,n).— 1 S WarringtonK . taken away, and that pointing to it with some pride he , opposed the application, stating that when Mr. Longton ; the ground of the illness of the plaintiff.—Mr. Riley 2-ssec. said: "What do you think of this for a wedding present ?" His father was taken ill suddenly that Tug-of•War.—tiand team beat Sergt. Jones's team. morning.—Mr. Longton applied for an adjournment on Chesteryoauiirtd.asl)f‘-V;mo(84in:81/43by. twope unis yards; S a lbf aodßi third.(3 8373 Tr di ms )e. , Fair 2n in .11 field I\, VAa smhbbuulrann' Ambulance; 0 ; 2, ambulanceßand. One Mile Flat Handicap.-1, J. Ditchfield, Widnes ; was told that an adjournment would be asked for on the (100 yards) ; 2, H. K. Jepson, warrington (s 7 yards) ; ground of the illness of Mrs. Forshaw he gave the unique team beat Band. c, S. K. Hayward, (Chester), (120 yards). ' answer that he never a