      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>THE LATE REV. E. J. WILLCOCKS•I 1 gentleman. He was a gentleman to the backbone and Ihe carried that spirit into the whole of his poblie and professional work. Nothing that had been said over- estimated his devotion to duty, and up to the end his mind was occupied with the work of the school for , which he lived. He (Alderman Bennett) was sure that An impresaive memorial service to the late Rev.B. J. if Mr. Willcocks was able to look upon the old town Willeocka, IW. A., for twenty-eight Years headmaster of , and to see the outcome of his work in future years, it the Tinteler Grammar School. was held at the Warring- ' must be a source of satisfaction to him in the higher ton Pariah Church on Sunday morning. There was a life, for a work like his could not end now, but moat. large congregation. including local educationists and influence generations yet unborn. Their expression of scholars and members of the staff of the Grammar sYmPatilY was not a mere perfunctory one; it came School. The hymns "Days and moments goickiv from their hearts. (Hear. hear.) flying." "The sower went forth sowing," and "On the ' The Rev. J. YONGE said he had had the pleasure of and 1 conversations with him on various matters both in that Resurrection morning " were sung. The preacher was knowing Mr. Willcocks pretty well, and had had various . the Rev. Canon Gibson Smith, vicar of Atherton. formerly second master at the Grammar School. who room and in private, and he had the highest respect for h took for his text the words :—" Therefore, my beloved im both as a man and as an educationist. He appeared 1 brethren, be ye steadfast, unmoveable, always abound_ ito throw his whole heart and mind into the work he int, in the work of the Lord, forasmuch aa bad to do. He (Mr. Yonge) did not know what Mr. in vain in the Lord." ye know that your la Willcocks was RS a disciplinarian, but he apprehended is not. .. h —1 Cor. xv. 58. After an eloquent sermon on death e was firm and discerning, and he was sure he was an : and immortality. he made a touching allusion to the ! enthusiastic teacherand one who inspired in his boys the late Rev. FL J. Wilcock. T have lately, he said. had ' highest ideals:From what Grammar Schoolboys had told so far as any him he knew he had won their esteem and affection in ; an opportunity of attempting to estimate, non* words of mine can estimate, the pre-eminent value ! nearly every case. As a town they had lost a valuable citizen, a man whose influence would tell on the right side in every direction, and whoever was called to fill of his official work. This morning T would rather remind you of his • noble Christian life—a shining , examnle of steadfast obedience to the call of duty even his place would, be hoped, follow worthily in the foot- i rs to the very end. To do our duty in that state of steps of such a good man. (Hear, hear.) life to which it. shall please God to call us The resolution was carried in silence, the members is is the practical ideal which the Catechism sets standing. before us in early days. How steadfastly, with what minute and conscientious care he lived TM to that ideal is known to all who were nrivileged to work with him, or to he taught by him. It mattered not, how monotonous his toil mighthe. how wearying to the patience,bow full of petty detail : still it was carried on by him with the same earnest accuracy. Yet he never became* the living machine that many men became gm the result of devotinif them- selves to work that involved great detail. No one conl.l wish for a more warm-hearted and affectionate friend. renerons and unselfish. He VMS very charitable in his indgments. and very tolerant towards those who differed from him. One of his crowning virtues, in my -fires, WAS the tanderneas with which he always spoke of anyone who had fallen into disgrace. I remember well how time and time again he said to me of such cases, "You know. as T grow older, T always feel it easier to make allowances for people." He was not merely a man of broad mind, bnt of broad sympathies, which is far better. He had an pager way of brushing sway all side 'series, and grasp- ing the nrincinie that underlay any practical onestion that. came before him. Bnt he WAS not. one of those who allowed all their attention to he absorbed in the present. We always had an eve on the future, and ever bore in mind that as we enrsalvea are inheritors of the mighty past, so We are to leave behind us an inheritance -still more enriched hr AST work for those who come after.' Do -von know those line.* which the late Bishop Creightnn, with unconacirms prophecy, gave as a motto for the last. year of his life on earth?</p>
        <p>Oh. earlier Rhsill the rose hnds blow, In after Team those 'hapniar years, And children weer when we lie low, Far fewer tears, far softer tears.</p>
        <p>Oh. true shall boyish laughter ring, Like tinkling (+imps in kinder times, And merrier shall the maidens sing And I not there, and I not there.</p>
        <p>Well. the sontiment so gracefrilly exp.esse&amp; and with Igo much feeling. quite represents the attitude of mind of our departed friend. And now he is gone from us we may say of him that be " lnred to brighter worlds. and led the way." The strenuous life is ended at on early ago. The kind heart that was always so readily -impelled towards helning others has ceased to heat. But he has left behind him a memory which we shall treasure so long as we remain on this earth—a memory of a brave and manly life, standing firm in simple faith by the grace of God. AM the lesson of it is surely. this :—" Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye stead- fast, unmoYeahle. always abounding in the work of the Lord. forasmuch as ye know that your labour is .not in vain in the Lord" At the close of the service the organist (Wr. P. IVT. Darby, Mut*. Rao.) played Chopin's Funeral March, the congregation standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EDITCATION COMMITTEE'S TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>EDITCATION COMMITTEE'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>Before the commencement of the business at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening, the CHAIRMAN (Councillor Fulton- Smith) said he had a very sad duty to perform, and that was to notify the death of the Rev. E. J. Wilcocks, who, un to a recent date. was a member of that body. They all knew what an indefatigable man he was, and what a good educationist. and how much he bad left his mark upon 'a generation of Warrington young men, who spoke with pleasure of the many happy days they bad spent under his tuition; also how they had been able to carry on their work in life through his successful and nainstaking efforts to educate them. His amiability and happy frame of mind with which he received everyone with whom he came into contact. they would all miss, and yet they would be glad they had known him as one who bad proved himself a friend. He (Conncillor Fulton-Smith) would like to read a-letter he had received that morning from the Rev. Canon Stevenson :--" Hazelbank, Grange- over-Sands, August 24th, 1907. Dear Mr. Fulton- Smith.—l do not expect to be present at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday, and as T have not attended the meetings of the School Management Committee it would be better that someone more conversant with the business should move the confirmation of the minutes. Von will doubtless refer to the death of the Rev. E. J. Willcocks, and I atn sure that you will express the feelings of respect and esteem which all who knew him entertained for him. I only mention this to ask you to allow me to associate myself with any expression of sympathy and 'tribute of respect which the committee may see fit to formulate. As I look back over the 26 years in which I have known . Mr. Willcocks, what strikes, me most forcibly is his almost unparalleled devotinn to duty. He lived for the Grammar School, and almost renounced all else for its sake, and this devotion, to; we all know, he carried on to the very end. notwitirstendine the altering and weakness which he bad to endure. He stood by the school, and never' lost heart in days when, from no fault of his, it was not so flourishing. Though naturally conservative, he set to work bravely to adant the school to the changes of modern education, and it is pathetic that just as the school has begun to make decided progress, and to enter upon an even more successful career, he has been called to lay down his work and rest from his labours." He (Councillor Fulton-Smith) moved: "That this com- mittee do place on record an expression of their regret at the logs sustained by the town by the death of the Rev. E. J. Willcocks, M.A.. and of their appreciation of his long and valued services in the cause of educa- tion in the borough, and do convey to his widow and family their deep sympathy with them in their bereave- ment. Mr. ROBERTS seconded, and said be should like to associate himself with everything the Chairman bad said, and also with what was contained in Canon Stevenson's letter. Perhaps he might, he allowed, as one of the governors of the Grammar School. to say how absolutely true it was that Mr. Willcocks identified himself with the highest code of honour in regard to parsonal finty,perbaps more than many men had hitherto done. He knew that the changes which had recently occurred in the curriculum and the buildings connected with the school were a source of great anxiety to Mr. Willcocks who at. the time was not in the best of health. The tenacity with which he attached himself to every- thing connected with the changes was more than remarkable. and if ever a man died in harness it might be said of him that he did. He (Mr. Roberts) could testify from an increasing intimacy with him during -the last seven or eight years to his remarkable character in that particular respect, to his abounding sense of duty, to his entire devotion to the cause of the school, and to the high code of honour he lived up to and which be endeavoured to impress on his pupils. (Hear, hear.) They wonld have greet difficulty in filling 'big place. The finest testimony to his memnry would be seen in the after lives of those who had had the greatest privilege of being under his care for a very important period of their lives. Mr. PLINSTON said he should ever remember the kind welcome be received from Mr. Willcocks when he first became a member of that authority. Mr. Willcocks was a man absolutely free from cant and snobbery and be (Mr. Plinston) had a high appreciation of him. 113 was always genial and sincere. Alderman Bitr.,TETT said it was one of the regrets of his life, Wareingtonian as he was, that he could not claim to have had the honour of being a student of the Grammar School. The loss was his. From all he-knew of Grammar School boys, the benefit they had derived from the school, and the influences for good exercised upon them by Mr. Willcocks, to attend a school under his regime must indeed have been a privilege, the results of which it was almost imnossible to over estimate. He hai, however, the privilege of knowing Mr. Willcocks intimately and he did not think he had -ever met anyone who was a finer type of an English</p>
      </div>
