      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Memorial Service at the Parish Church. I</head>
        <p>Memorial Service at the Parish Church. I</p>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Life in Camp.</p>
        <p>[By A VISITOR]. In the genial company of the Vicar of Stockton Heath (the Rev. James Collins) we journeyed to Rhyl, on Friday the 9th of August and spent a very enjoyable day in initiating ourselves into the mysteries of camp life.</p>
        <p>Reaching Rhyl at 11-47 we speedily arrived at the camping ground at Voryd Farm. Passing through the gate, which was closely guarded by youthful sentries armed with rifles, we were confronted by a vast array of canvas amongst which the finding of the Stockton Heath Company appeared likely to prove a matter of some difficulty, but with the directions with which we had been supplied and by asking a few questions we soon arrived at line 8 and in the cavernous depths of the second tent into which we inquisitively peered we discovered two members of the Stockton Heath Company, who informed us that the inspection—the event of the week—was just over. The day being fine and bright, the curtains of the tents were raised and the bedding—neatly folded together in small heaps, was laid out to air. We were particularly pleased with the soldier-like bearing of the lads and with the three large bugle bands which provided martial strains. A brass and reed band played capital selections during I unch.On enquiry we were informed that it was the band of the Manchester Regi- ment C.L.B. Having some experience of bands we were much interested in the excellent playing of the boys and, when lunch was over, took the oproortunity of making a closer inspection of them. The band con- sisted of 1 piccolo, 6 clarionets, 17 brass instruments and drums, and was one of the best boys' bands we have heard. We next decided on a tour of inspection round the camp, and first having a look at the Stockton Heath boys, some of whom were preparing for an afternoon nap—we made our way to the hospital tent, again occasionally tripping over tent pegs. We learnt that there had been very little illness among the whole of the boys, and only two cases of any seriousness, the ailments in both cases having been contracted before coming to camp. Every possible care was taken of the sufferers, who were promptly sent home. All other cases of illness were of a more or less trivial character. We found four patients in hospital and judging from what we saw, we are sure that the arrangements for their well-being could not have .been improved. We were pleased to learn that one of the Stockton Hestia lads—Corporal Sankey, who holds his first year's ambn- lance certificate, has had an opportunity of putting his knowledge to a practical use, and has been assisting in hospital. And now, having stated our own impressions from what we saw we will proceed to give a few particulars of the daily routine of comp life, and the conditions under which it was carried out. Let us then begin byl stating that the camp was situated on a large flat pleateau, girt by the sand hills which fringe the shore, on the one side, and by the railway on the other; that it ICOR was the North-Western Camp, was the largest C.L.B. camp ever held, comprising over 400 tents and accommodating 2452 officers, staff-sergeants and boys, coming from all parts, from Whiteheaven to Worcester and from Lincoln on the East. The camp consisted of three battalions under the general command of Colonel Moore, V.D. of the sth Manchester Regiment. Tba Stockton Heath Contingent formed part of the Bth Company of the first battalion, the battalion being under the command of Lieutentant-Colonel. E. Fret-cher i (Captain of the Ist V.B. Manchester Regiment) and the Bth Company being under that of Captain Higham of St. Paul's Sale Company. Captain R. H. Lapage was, most of the week, with the Volunteers at Llan- beris, hut Lieutenants J. S. Shotton and A. H. Austin were in camp at Voryd with the Stockton Heath Com- p-inv. The first battalion (845 strong) was drawn from Manchester, Chester, Carlisle, Worcester, etc. 'fhe second (811 strong) from Wigan, Southwell, Lichfield and Wolverhampton ; and tho third (796 strong) from Manchester. Leicester and Males. The Rector of Liverpool. Canon Kempthorne, assisted by Rev. Edgar Rodgers (Staff Chaplain) and a large number of other clergy attended to the spiritual welfare of the boys. The chief medical officer was Lieutenant Colonel W.Prosser White. Every morning;divine service was held on the drill ground. The boyi mustered in camp, and headed by their bugle bands, marched to the drill ground where the reed and brass band played them in, and accom- panied the service. On the Sunday, two celebrations of the Holy Communion were held at 5.30 and 6.45 in the morning. On the Wednesday morning the Stockton</p>
        <p>Heath Company attended Holy 'Communion at 6.30 in the Church tent,tbere being on that occasion,from 800 to 1,000 nresent. Sergeant-Major Stewart (Ist Man- chester Regiment) took the officers in hand, and as early as 6.30 in the morning, took them out skirmishing and later engaged them in Company drill, imparting to them much useful instruction. There were also classes of instruction for the lads. Recreation was also provided, competitions being arranged in football, cricket, wrestling and boxing. Concerts were held every even- ing at 7.30 except on the Friday night which was devoted to the distribution of prizes won in the com- petitions, and also those awarded for the cleanest tent. The tents were inspected every day and we are pleased to learn that both tents occupied by the boys of the Stockton Heath Company won a prize for neatness and cleanliness, on the Sunday, No. 2 tent receiving a first prize and No. 1 tent being commended as being very good. On the following day the prize was awarded to No. 1 tent. _</p>
        <p>The daily routine was as follows :—Reveille at 5.15 a.m., when the buglers played round the camp. Then biscuits were served out and the battalions paraded at 6.25 for an hour's drill, breakfast at eight, battalion parade at 10.30 for an hour and a half or two hours' drill, dinner at one. On the Tuesday and Thursday afternoons there was another battalion parade at 2-30, and on Wednesday at the same time a bathing parade in which about 1,000 lads took part. On the afternoons, and also in tne evenings, the boyagyere allowed to spend their time as they liked. The majority went to Rhyl, where the various amusements proved attractive. Tea was served at 5, and "Tattoo " which sounded at 10 p.m.' was the signal for everyone to be in camp, and for the boys to retire to rest. - - - - -</p>
        <p>Friday was the red-letter day of the week, for that was the day fixed for the inspection by General Sir C. J. Burnett, K.C.8., Commander-in-chief of the Western command, who arrived on the parade ground about 11 o'clock.At the conclusion of the inspection, the lads rormed into a squareand were addressed by the General, who said that the inspection had given him the greatest Possible pleasure. He was pleased with their fine march- ing and accurate drill, and thought the boys would turn out to be a credit, not only to themselces, but to their country. He hoped the boys would go away from Camp strengthened in health, and determined to uphold what they had been so carefully taught. They would then act as leaven amongst the youths with whom they would be thrown. He attached the greatest possible importancetto the Church Lad's Brigade and that was why he harpersonallv come to inspect them. The boys. headed by their bands. then marched back to camp. In the afternoon the General made a detailed inspection of the camp and was deeply impressed with the excellent arrangements. On the following day the camp was astir at 4 a.m. and preparations were briskly made for striking the tents, this occurring simul- taneously at the sound of the bugle. The Stockton Heath Company, together with others travelling in the same direction, left Voryd by aspecial triin at 11.10. The week's enjoyment was somewhat marred by heavy rain At times, which stopped two parades, and also by thelitrong wind which prevailed early in the week, several tents being blown down and others flooded by the rain. The labonrs of Captain Crawley (the Quartermaster) will be understood and appreciated when we state that no fewer than 400 bell tents and 25 marquees were required, with 2.500 beds and ground sheets, and 5,900 blunkets. the total weight of baggage having been 70 tons. The daily issue of rations amounted to 2,3001b5. of bread, 1,4501bs of meat, the same quantity of potatoes, 6001bs. af butter, and 450 pots of jam. During the week, 8 tons of wood and 12 tons of coal were con- sumed ; whilst £l4 worth of water, transmitted from the town supply through specially laid pipes, has been used. Captain Crawley has taken part in 55 camps and what he does not know about camping is not worth knowing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LADY MOTORIST KILLED.</head>
        <p>LADY MOTORIST KILLED.</p>
        <p>A New York correspondent telegraphs that Mrs. Leslie Helsey, a well-known contributor to automobile newspapers, and herself a keen motorist.hqs been killed while taking part in a speed test on the Morrir Park track. The unfortunate lady was found pinned under- neath the car, which was turned completely over. Skidding is believed to have caused the accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 31, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 31, 1907.</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>THE DIFFERENT METHODS OF CHRISTIANITY AND SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Replying to John Workman's article I do not want to labour the point of the parable of the labourers and the vineyard, but must repeat that John Workman has not proved his point.. The labourers did not receive according to their needs because the needs of no two individuals are equal and in this case each received equally. But John Workman persistently ignores what to me seems the central thought of the parable. The householder's right is distinctly stated in his own words : "Is it not lawful for me to do what I will wit!, mine own?"—A right which socialists stoutly deny. John Workman tells us that" A true Christian is a man who should own nothing." If Christ's intention had been to teach division accord- ing to needs, as John Workman seems to think, the householder's reply to the grumbler would surely have referred to the question of the labourers' needs. Instead of this there is the clear declaration of indi- vidual rights. "Is it not lawful for me to do what I will with mine own ?" But as I have said before I depricate placing great importance on isolated texts or parables. What we want to find are the great principles of the teaching of the founder of the Christian faith and the methods of their operation. claim that the whole of Christ's teaching aims at regeneration from within as opposed to the Socialist's method of regeneration from without. Socialism seeks to compel men to do right by the intervention of the State, Christ seeks to make men rejoice to do the right by regenerating the heart and life. Herein lies the distinct line of cleavage between the two systems. This essential difference of method renders co-operation impossible. Christianity proclaims the futility of outward compulsion, and says that force is no permanent remedy. Socialism on the other hand says in effect "Christian teaching has failed to bring about the adoption of its principles by working from within, we will bring these about by a compulsory State system. We will organize a plan by which the voluntary individual system will be superceded by a compulsary gollectiv e one. We will take from each according to his capacity aad will give to each according to his need and so fhlfil the law bf Christ. Christianity can make no part or lot in these methods because they seek to supplant the methods of its founder. One came to Christ with the request "Master speak to my brother that he divide the inheritance with me." The Saviour said to him "Man WI o made me a judge or divider over you." The appeal to Jesus was that he should assume the office of Judge and by an out- ward authority compel a division. He emphatically declines to assume such a position. But while so doing He in no way approves the injustice or avarice of the one who retains the brother's share of the family inheritance. But the Saviour is much more soncerned by the wrong which the selfish brother does to himself than he is about the wroeg done to the defrauded brother. And after all is the former not the more important? He proceeds to teach by parable the futility of riches and shows how he wiih sets his heart upon them loses the beat in this life as well as in that which is to come. "He that sinneth against his brother wrongeth his own soul." The wrong which the selfish man did to himself was far more serious and gave Christ more concern that the secondary wrong to the defrauded brother. Why then did He not adopt the Socialist's plan and use His authority by ordering a division and thus right both wrongs at one stroke? Simply because to effect any permanent reformation in character the initiative must be evoked from within and not be the result of compulsion from without. We see in this the fundamental difference between Christ's xnethod and the method of Socialism. A difference so vital as to make any co-operation impossible and to some extent to throw the two systems into antagonism. Again Socialism concerns itself mainly with material comfort, a well fed body, physical sufficiency, while Christianity is mainly concerned with the inward life, it protests against the starved siiiir; Christianity places the soul first, the body second. Socialism would reverse this order. Most Socialists have recognized that Christianity and Socialism can form no alliance, indeed are funda- mentally antagonistic. One of the thorough-going Socialists has lately said that the reign of happiness could not be inaugurated until the theory of the =minted policeman above was eradicated, and every trace of Christianity must be extinguished before Socialism could prevail. I desire to recall the fact that we are here dealing not with the economic asnects of the question, but with the contention of Socialists of the order of John Workman, who seems to contend that Socialism is the embodiment of Christianity. I think the Editor of the "Voice of Labour" will on calm, unprejudiced thought, recognize the essential difference between the two systems which he tries to harmonize. It is n matter of very little importance what offices I personally hold, or what class of thought I represent. The main question is have I mis-stated the essential differences of Christianity and Socialism, and if so wherein? If I have occasion again to write on this subject I will deal with the somewhat foolish and short-lived attempt of the early Church to establish Socialism. CHARLES E. PARKER. Denbigh, August 27th, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>MUNICIPAL COAL SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL COAL SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>, Your correspondent "Politician "in last week's "Voice of Labour" refers to the question of a municipal coal supply raised by Councillor Stott at the last meeting of the Town Council, and calls his proposal a "hare-brained scheme." Your corres- pondent also says " that this is the first time that he ever heard of a Town Council which could sell anything cheaper than private enterprise can." It is surprising to find any individual, claiming to be a politician, so ignorant of the success of municipal management as your correspondent appears to be. Has he never heard of municipalities who have been able to supply gas, water, and electricity much cheaper than private companies have done? There are also scores of instances where tramway charges have been reduced, and wages of the employees incr sased, after the tramways have been taken over by local Corporations. Regarding what was said by Councillor Stott I am in entire agreement with it. If one of the Corporation departments can purchase coal at four shillings per ' ton less than private householders are able to do, why could not the ciorporation buy for the whole of the domestic consumers and save them the difference in the cost? There appears to be an impression, which perhaps Councillor Stott is not aware of, that he was mistaken in his figures, but I have it on reliable authority, that the figures were qnite correct, and that the tender referred to was for best house coal delivered in Warrington. I am also given to understand that this week-end there is to be an increase of is. Bd. per ton which will bring the price of coal to something like siic shillings per ton more than is being paid by the Corporation department referred to. Councillor Stott might have gone further and pointed out that by purchasina in large quantities—such as Nvould be required if the Corporation bought for the whole of the domestic consumers —coal could be bought at an even lower price per ton than that referred to at the Council meeting. I understand that the reason why the Gas Committee object to publish the prices, paid for coal for the Gas Department is because the figure is so comparatively low owing to them buy- ing in such large quantities. Perhaps Councillor Stott —who will know what the prices are which 'the Gas Committee are plying—can tell us whether they are not very much lower than those paid per ton .by the Water Committee.</p>
        <p>I would further suggest that by several corporations combining together to purchase coal mines, very much more could be saved than by purchasing from private coalowners. It has been said that coal mines at the present time are more lucrative than gold mines. It is a fact that we have been paying winter prices for coal during the summer months. Those of your readers who read the report of the July meeting of the Town Council would find that the Price for coal required for the Gas Department is 2s. 6d. per ton more this year than last. Surely no intelligent person will be prepared to say that the coal ' miner has received an increase of wages equal to 2s. 6d. per ton during the past twelve months, rather it is much nearer 20. I remember Sir James Joicey, in au article he I published in the" Times" some years ago,stateri that the profits from the coal mines in tbe year 1889 were £63372,ooo,whereas in 1900 the profits were£403275,000</p>
        <p>Why should we permit private individuals to take such a large profit from the coal mines? Were they responsible for placing this vast mineral wealth in the bowels of the earth, there might be some justifica- tion for the owners of the mines receiving some profits on the output, but seeing that they have done nothing whatever towards creating the minerals of this, or any other country, we are in my opinion entitled to claim that the whole of the people should receive the benefit to be derived therefrom.</p>
        <p>It is impossible to fully discuss such a large ques- tion in the limited space allotted to the " Voice of Labour" but I claim that it is the duty of the muni- cipality, in the interests of the citizens, to seriously consider the question of taking steps to provide the whole of the inhabitants with the coal they require believing that by this means thousands of pounds might be saved to the community. If it is beneficial for a Corporation to supply gas and electricity would it not be quite as ad vantageous to provide coal? I trust that the Socialist members of the Council —now that the question has been raised —will continue to agitate for a municipal coal supply. I am one of those who believe in the principle of what the people socially need they should socially own. THE SCOUT. TRADES UNIONISM AND SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>When I wrote my first contribution some weeks ago my object was to show the results which had been obtained in other constituencies by the co-operation of the Socialist and Trades Union forces. I tried to show how shortsighted the Warrington trades unionists were in refusing to co operate with the Independent Labour Party, and expressed the opinion that the local trades unionists had more to gain than to lose by such co-operation. I also asserted that it was the work of the I.L.P. which had made a Labour party possible. In a subsequent article I stated that Sir Thomas Whitaker was only repeating what members of the I.L.P. have been saying for many years, when he told his constituents in the Spen Valley Division that "the Socialist and Labour parties aimed at the destruc- tion of the Liberal party," although I was careful to add that we aimed at the destruction of the Conserva- tive party also. None of my critics have disproved my assertions, rather have they evaded them and introduced other matters. \ Your correspondent " Politician " still contends that three out of every four trades unionists are Liberals and Tories. This I do not admit, but for the sake of argument I am willing to grant his contention.</p>
        <p>If 25 per cent. of the trades unionists•are Socialiets does this not prove that the Socialist movement has made very great progress during the past five years ? Take for instance, the increased Labour and Socialist vote in the recent bye-elections in the Jarrow and Colne Valley Divisions, compared to the vote registered at previous elections in the same constituencies. A few minutes' consideration of those figures will con- vince any intelligent and unbiased mind that my state- ment that the I.L.P. aims at the destruction of the Liberal and Tory parties is becoming a possibility. How can we build up a powerful Socialist and Labour party in any other way except by weakening the other two great parties ? I am as willing as your correspondent is to give honour where honour is due, and don't remember, during this discussion, having condemned the good work of the Liberal Labour members. My contention is that there is a much better method of advancing the interests of the workers by sending Socialist and Independent Labour men to the House of Commons than men who have to depend on the Liberal party for their successful return, and who are to a great extent subject to the Liberal whips. Another correspondent who signs himself as " Commonsense " is also guilty of ignoring the prin- cipal points in my previous contributions add raises questions which are more suitable for discussion at an I.L.P. branch meeting, or at the annual conference of that organisation. My reply to " Politician " also answers "Commonsense" in that portion of his letter where he says that I unjustifiably exaggerate the political influence of the I.L.P. I agree with your correspondent where he states that he believes that a complete reorganisation of industry and commerce will alone solve all the social evils which he details. He also tells us that these reforms cannot be brought about until they receive the intelligent and enthusias- tic support of (at least) a large majority of the electorate of this country. Why do we require the support of at least a large majority of the electorate to bring this about? I should think at least a majority could accomplish this. On what figures does your correspondent base his statement that the Liberal and Tory parties have jointly received nine tenths of the recorded votes at recent contested elections? Surely he cannot be referring to Jarrow and Colne Valley, neither is the statement true regarding. these • constituences which Labour candidates contested at the last general election. He also objects to an alliance of the Socialists and Trades Unionists. Does he mean that the trades unionists would better advance their interests by continuing to support the Liberal and Tory parties instead of co operating with the Socialists? If he does deny this, then I must confess that he is the first conyincsd Socialist that I have heard of who holds that opinion.</p>
        <p>-His statement that he "has been over ten years a convinced Socialist" reminds me of the late Sir William Harcourt when he said that " we are all Socialists now." I don't know whether your corres- pondent is one of those who are pleased to call themselves " un-attached " Socialists, and who evidently expect to bring about Socialistic reform by holding aloof from the Socialist parties, or whether he is one of those members of the I.L.P. who are never satisfied except when trying to create dissension in the ranks, and to show many of us apply the term of party "wreckers." I have been a convinced Socialist and also a trades unionist more than ten years, but I should not think of trying to advance the interests of the party to which I belong by attempting to discuss contentious points of administration and policy, which are questions for the I.L.P. branch meetings, in the "Voice of Labour."</p>
        <p>Your correspondent says he is not ashamed of his faith, neither am I ashamed to sign myself a</p>
        <p>SOCIALIST TRADES UNIONIST</p>
      </div>
