      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SUGGESTIONS FOR NEW STANDING ORDERS FOR THE TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS FOR NEW STANDING ORDERS FOR THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A correspondent has sent us the following suggested standing orders :</p>
        <p>1. Under no circumstances except when electing the next Mayor shall the Council meetings be prolonged after midnight. 2. The Mayor and Clerk shall not be called upon to explain the same Standing Order more than six times at any one meeting. 3. No Councillor shall be permitted to speak more than one hour at a time on any one subject and not more than ten times in all at any one meeting. 4. Not more than four Councillors shall be allowed to speak on the same silbject at the same time, provided that where all desiring to speak are representatives of the same ward not more than three of them shall he allowed to speak at once, provided also that if such representatives of bhe same ward are about to advocate some special privilege for their own ward, only one shall be allowed to speak, and that one shall be the Councillor who earliest in point of time shall have to seek re-election. 5. When a Councillor is proceeding with a speech which is obviously a repetition of what has already been stated, any other Councillor may cry out " Che'stnuts," and thereupon the Council- lor who was speaking shall resume his seat. 6. If any Conncillor more than four times at the same meeting shall fume and fret, violently comb his hair with his hands, and cry out "Mr. Mayor, where are we now ?" he shall (that is to to say on the fifth offence) be suspended for the remainder of the meeting. 7. In order to avoid unpleasant and unwarrantable attacks upon personal honour all Councillors shall make a declaration before every meeting as to whether they are or are not financially interested in any of the business of the Council about to be transacted, and if they are so interested they shall neither vote nor speak on the particular matter, nor shall they be counted in ascertaining whether there is a quorum present. If as a consequence no quorum can be obtained this is hardly likely to happen in Warrington). 8. In order to avoid unseemly references to the travelling expenses of Councillors attending the various conferences in different parts of the world there shall not henceforth upon the same bill of expenses and upon the same side of it be more than two charges for the same thing.</p>
        <p>" I cannot calculate the speed ; it went past md like a flash of lightning," was the evidence of a cabman in a case at Colchester, where a motorist was fined for driving his car to the public danger.</p>
      </div>
