        <p>CALIFORNIAN INDIANS. The Department of Anthropology of the University of California has been enriched by the acquisition of the first skeletons of Porno Indians possessed by any museum or 151 liG institution. An expedition sent by the do. Majority for Crosilelds, 35 partment to Mendocino County, California, returned with five complete skeletons, The matches for Saturday are : several parts of skeletons, many beads and P. and K. v. Whitecross No. 1. other objects buried with the dead. These Longford Wire v. Sankey Wire Mills. will be of great value in determining the Crosfields No. 2 v. Walker's Brewery. Referee, G. Holbrook. qualities and characteristics of the Pomos The prizes for the recent main will be pre- and their relationships with other tribes of sented at the Bowling Green Inn, Bank Quay, on California Indians. , Monday evening. The Pomos practised cremation, which ex- plains the almost complete lack of remains of them. They were of middle height, with round, heavy skulls. Many living Pomos are to be measured and photographed for pur- poses of comparison with the skeletons. It is expected that knowledge of the origin, cornections, and wanderings of the Indian tribes will be considerably increased by this determination of their characteristics, and that much information that is not supplied by a study of their language and customs will be obtained.</p>
        <p>MAIN AT ARPLEY.—A four-landed main for the prize given by the President (Alderman Forshaw) in connec- tion with the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club was heid on Saturday at Arpley. There were 32 entrants. In the final Messrs. T. Cooper and G. F. Freeman beat Messrs. R. T. Fairclough and H. Mawdsley by 13-11.</p>
        <p>THE" LIVERPOOL EXPRESS" CHALLENGE CLIP A WARRINGTON WINNER.—The bowling competition for the " Express " Challenge Cup has been won by Mr. John Wareham, an old Warringtonian, who was at one time a member of the Penketh Bowling Club. He was successful out of over 1,000 entries.</p>
