      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>CHESHIRE AGRICULTURAL SHOW.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AGRICULTURAL SHOW.</p>
        <p>Local Winners.</p>
        <p>The show of the Cheshire Agricultural 50c;01,,,- was held under pleaaant oonditions on Wednesday on the Roodee at Chester. With a day of sunshine and a gentle breeze, the show was well calculated to bring out a host of visitors, and it was indeed not wanting. The attendance was larger even than it was last year, when the show was also held on the Roodee and accompanied by equally favourable weather. Formerly the county show was held every year in a different town, not always with the best results. A few years ago,however, it was resolved to try the experiment of holding the exhibition at Chester over a series of years. So far it has proved eminently satisfactory. The entries of Wednesday totalled 2,235 as against 2,395 last year while the attendance numbered 10,000.</p>
        <p>Among the more distinguished of the winners was Mr. James Gould,'of Lymm, whose grey mare Darling (nine years) was the chief prize-taker in one class and was declared the best exhibit of her kind among the horses on the show-ground. Honourable positions were also held by Mr. Raingill's three-year-old filly Lockinge Forest Blossom, from Altrincham, which has already been a conspicuous winner at other shows this year. The light horses consisted, of course largely of hunters in a county where the hounds are course, with some devotion. It is customary to look for high quality in stock of this kind. This year, however, the standard seemed higher honour. Much the same may be said of the hackney olasses,in which the judges were Sir Gilbert Greenall and Mr. F. Baguley. , Sir Gilbert Greensll occupied the chair at the I luncheon, and in responding to the toast of "the Cheshire Agricultural Society," said he felt sure that shows of that character did, and had done,a tremendous work for the agriculturists of this country. (Hear 'hear.) One of the most comforting things was that wherever stock was bred—in whatever part of the world —they had to come back to the old country to replenish their stock. (Hear, hear.) They must always remember that the interests of the landlord and tenant were identical, and it was only when things were on a sound business footing and when friendly relationship existed between landlord and tenant that they could hope agriculture would flourish. (Hear, hear.) The local prize winners included the following : CATTLE.</p>
        <p>Bußs.—Above one and under two years old: 3, S. W. Gould, Foxley Hall, Lvmm. Under two years old : 3, C. Eel!, Norley Hall. finder one year old: 2, E. Clarke, Moss Brow, Warburton; 3, S. W. Gould. Dairy Heifers.—Under three years "old, in milk or in calf: 3, R. Clarice, Warburton Park. Under one year old: 1, C. Bell, Norley Hall ;3, S. W. Gorton.</p>
