      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Police Sports.</head>
        <p>The Police Sports.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Allow me to echo your congratulations of praise to our local custodians of the peace, for the noble efforts they make each year on behalf of our charitable institutions, and the popular support they get from the public I am sure amply repays them for the trouble they go to. But don't you think the time has arrived when the hum and buz of "evens bar one" and so forth were checked, and not allowed to run rampant under the very eyes of those in authority by imported gentlemen from surrounding districts. Further, while the opportunity affords is it not possible that some effort should be made to protect tne poor dupes, who are constantly being handled by the prowling wolves who parade Haydock-street, Owen- street, Portland-street, Dallam-lane, Liverpool-road, Knutsford-road, Howley, &amp;c.,. &amp;c. Surely if those in power cannot capture the" bookie" on account of the scouts and spies, then punish' these for aiding, &amp;c. Many hard-working men's homes are suffering severely to-day through the betting evil. Hoping to see a move made on this "class " for the betterment of our town, I am yours &amp;c., THE MAN IN THE STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>Warrington District No. 4.</p>
        <p>The quarterly Council meeting of the above district was held in the Co-operative Assembly Rooms,Golbor no on Saturday last. Bro. Joseph Hill D.C.R. presided, being supported by thejwhole of the district officers and representatives from the 19 branches. Bro. T. Winstanley D.S. pre- sented the Executive's report which showed only an increase of five members during the past quarter this making a total membership of 1,297. The half-yearly balance sheet also the balance account of the district property at Wigan, Golborne, and Earlestown and valuations returns were submitted to the meeting. The sick fund showed a deficiency of is. sd. in the 2 and the funeral fund 20s. 11d, in the 2. A long discussion arose on the valuation returns and the excessive sick pay in several of the tents conse- quent on the Compensation Act. It was moved and carried that application be made to the Board of Directors for permission to transfer • the sum of £306 to the sick fund amount, this amount being the surplus of funeral fund valuation. Bro. B. Warburton, D.S.J.T., presented his report of the last quarterly meetin, of the Juvenile District - held at Stockton Heath. Ile said the Juvenile District continued to grew both numerically and financially and good work was being done in the juvenile branches in training the children to be sober and intelligent men and women. The report of representatives to the H. INI.O held at the Town Hall, Newcastle under the auspices of the Northumberland District the first week in August was given by Bro. E. Walkden District Trustee and Bro. B. • • ' Walker, P.D.C.R. Bro. Walden gave a brief bitt splendid summary of the legislation carried through in the Rechabite Parlia- ment. Bro. Walker foltowed, and both spoke of the pleasure it afforded them to know that while most of the principal friendly societies in the country were barely making any headway the Order of Rechabites was going up by leaps and bounds, and making records . not only of opening new branches but by increased membership. The magnificent increase of 27,517 juvenile members, after giving 12,098 in transfers to • the adult section, and an increase of 19,324 adult mem- : bers and an increase of funds amounting to £215,361 with 650 new branches opened during the past two s -years was indeed marvellous and something for every Rechibite to be proud of. The report of the H.M.C. representatives were . accepted with thanks. The District Executive with four additional members •isitom the representatives were appointed to form a .l'eommittee to re-model the District and Tent Rules. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>The Chief Ruler called upon Bro. T. Massey (District Treasurer), to make a presentation of a D.P.R.C. framed emblem to Bro. B. Walker, of Providence Tent, Latch- ford, for passing through the chairs of the District. Bro. Massey spoke very highly of the faithful dis- charge of the duties, and the good work accomplished ; by Bro. Walker during his term of office, and he had great pleasure in handing him that beautiful framed . emblem of the Order on behalf of the District. .Ero. B. Walker responded, and said he accepted with pleasure the P.C. Ruler's framed emblem presented to 111,n, by the District. Too much had been said of the ; little serv.,-., he had been able to render to the District 443cring his term of office, but he had been fully repaid for any _ sacrifice be had made by the pleasant recollections of the kincinegs he had received from the members when visiting the vartuu3 bi.uches ,the •District. Bro. Walker was much affected, and touched the feelings of all present when he stated that he owed all he had to the influence of the members of •the.t,rder. If it had not been for the Rechabite ,Order he would in all probability instead of being engaged in the work of trying to ,lift up and brighten 'the lives and better the position and circumstances of -others he would have been found like many other working men spending both his time and money in the publouse. It was decided to accept the invitation given by Bro. A. Ward of Excelsior tent to hold the next district meeticg at Vadgate. A splendid tea was provided by the members of 'Ebenezer tent,Golhorne. A Tote of thanks was proposed to the friends at 'Golborne by:Bro. Walter Elulme and seconded by Bro. W. Wright for their hospitality, the meeting being Iroug6, tea close about 6.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>SORMAND WRITERS' ASSOCIATION. First Annual Meeting. -- Thelirst ancual general meeting of the Warrington wad District. Shorthand Writers' Associatiov was held on Wedaesday.evening last at the Winmarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street. The chair was taken at-7.45 p.m. by the President of the Association, Mr. G. C. Brown, F.1.P.5., and there was a good attendance of members, both ladles and gentlemen. The S'4CRETAiI,I: read the first annual report of the Committee. This proved ,to be a very intereating document, and spoke well for the enthusiasm which had been displayed in the town in the inception of an association of shoral.and writers. The number a mem- bers reached the high number of 54, a figure which will compare with any other Association. During the past session thirty meetir-gs have been held, and as many -papers read on_shorthand, literary, and commercial:cub- jects. The thanks of the Associationevere tendered to those who had personally attended and read papers. A special appreciation was accorded of the meeting which was conducted by the ladies on the 17th October last. The proceedings were carried through with despatch, and the discussion which followed the reading of the paper written by Miss Kate Pickard, B.A.,entitledi "Ought a woman to earn her own liviug ? " was most instructive. In the Natioak Federation Speed Examine-, tion held last December Miss Norbury obtained a certificate at SO words per minute, , and Mr. Davies at 100 words per mieute, with distinction. Two social gatherings have seen held at the Winrnarleigh Cafe, and a cycle run to Budworth in gune proved enjoyable. The Committee hope that the coming session may be attended with equal success to the last. The Treasurer read the financial statement which .showed that there was a balance in hand, which. ;though .stuell, was very encouraging for the first year's *ork. he alteration in the rules of,the Association was sub- mitted by the Committee which will require that full members of the Association shall he competent to write phonography at a minimum rate of-fit; words per minute. All °tier members wilt be deemed associates, and will have at, a lower subscription all•the advantages of the literal.' portion of the association's work. The altera- tion war carried. The PRESIDENT addressed the meeting, and in a short brig4t spoech expressed his great pleasureat the remark- able success of the Association. It was sound numeri- cally and :dnancially,land that was a lot to say for an Associatiop of one year only. The existence of the Association was largely due he supposed to himself. On an occasion et the Technical irnstitution in the town. he let.slip that he thought such a Society as this should be formed in a place like Warrington. The idea was taken up, and he was very pleased to gee with what success. He thought that a very consideraele leakage in the ranks of shorthand writers, and a vc,7 considerable waste of money also, had taken place through there being no gathering such as this in the past, where students of shorthand could meet and render ous another help, and besides develop that es.-it tie corps which was so much urged by the shorthand jousnals and societies. The etection ro&amp; officers took place, and the followine were elected :—Presideat, Mr. G. Z. Brown, ; secretary, Mr. J. Marsh, M.1.P.5.; treasurer, Mr. A. N. Legge. M.I.P.S. ; .cornmittee, Miss B. Dain‘ith, Miss Connolly, Miss Carr, Miss Norman, Mr. T. Calm. F.C.1.5., Mr. H.A. Webb, 'Hr. Craddock, Mr. Walsh. The first meeting of the Association was fixed for September 25th, at the Winmarleigh Cafe, when speed practice will be resumed, and a paper will be rAad.</p>
        <p>CAUgHT AN 18L13. TROUT. An enormous trout has 'nut been ,caught by Mr. Brigg, with rod sad line, in the New River at Harringay. The fish, which is of the great gray or lake variety, has ffequently been seen in the water hetwoLen Wood Green and Harringay for the past two years, and had been the subject of keen competition by ail local angtsrs. It was landed after half.an-hour's ‘truggle and found to weigh ISlb., and measured 2ft. fiin, wvlght is a remd for pny topOn water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>'AN UNDESIRABLE CITIZEN.'</head>
        <p>'AN UNDESIRABLE CITIZEN.'</p>
        <p>[BY MR. H. Wninum.]</p>
        <p>In my various communications to the " Examiner " it has rarely been my good fortune to have anything of a nice and pleasant nature to say touching the land of my adoption. As individuals the Americans are, I suppose, just as nice, and just as un-nice as any others of • the English speaking race, but when you get beyond the close and personal relationship into the wider business arena of mun icipal,State, or federal life it is then that one, not to the manner born, runs against the snags that hurt when the bump comes. America would be all that cue could wish were it not for the quakes, fires, tornadoes, blizzards, cyclones and floods. These are ume of the bad features that Dame Nature must be held accountable for. Even these, bad as they are, are not the worst of life's fitful scenes. Having no redress we accept them with as good grace as possible and inwardly wish that man could, or would, try to make what remained to us of life worth living for. The English radical has a lot of pat schemes he intends the club at Westminster to endorse as soon as the old man of the sea descends from his perch, of which I think, payment of members, universal suffrage, and direct taxation are at least three. Not one of these would I support were I in England, if I thought for one moment that the Englishman was not only a better educated man but that he really knew what the word ' liberty ' stood for and, as a general thing, knew when licence was sneaking around much better that his kin beyond the sea. Long years ago a few English radicals, of noble as well as ignoble birth, thought out and developed the idea that a man, of whatever station in life should be held innocent, until proved guilty, in all criminal charges. This led to the jury system, petty and grand. In France they manage these things differently. The police tell the arrested one that he is guilty and if he cannot prove his innocence why it goes hard with poor Frenchy,and his peers are simply nowhere.ln America what do we do? The answer to this would simply mean a book and a big one. We have the jury system and it is an institution of fearful and wonderful make- up. As a rule it takes weeks to get it through the badgering, and cross examining of prosecution and defence attorneys. It is not unusual for the lawyer for the defence to treat the jury as it was an entity of the deepest criminal dye. If these things are done in the open we may be sure of much worse being done behind the closed doors. The treatment the prisoner receives when in the hands of a specially qualified policeman whose purpose is to force confession makes one think of the inquisition of the middle age. Some of my readers may think I am writing nonsense, not so, just a bit of plain truth, some of it to be referred to at another time.</p>
        <p>And now, what of the "undesirable citizen." One of the most indiscreet Presidents the tnited States has ever had, and one who, if he is not more careful, may be the last representative of the present class system, in a recent speech made pointed reference to three prominent labour leaders, as "undesirable citizens." These men, Messrs. Moyer, Haywood, and Pettibone were in prison at the time and had been since about February 16th, 1906. Before going further, let me say that Mr. T. P. O'Connor in the issue of his paper " P.T.O. " for May 18th, 1901, gives considerable space to the doings of these three men, and to the labour organization of which they were the chief officers, and to the life the miners of the Rockies lead. T.P. says: "It is a grim fight of ill-tempered and necessarily ill-conditioned men with all that is brutal, cruel, and animal in both the one side and the other, developed to gigantic pro- portions by the unhealthy and abnormal atmosphere of the place. Study the psychology of the two parties to the fight, the grim, arrogant, and pushful man, who has come here to win millions out of these men, and who is haunted amid all the lonely, black, and com- fortless atmosphere of his existing life, with visions of luxuriois restaurants in New York, palatial mansions in Washington, perhaps a seat in the magnificent Capitol, whose vast proportions and gleaming white marble are a very fine embodiment in stone of the inner visions that disturb the minds of so many of America's vaulting ambitions. Such a master has little time to consider the feelings of others, or their interests, or their inner lives, and thus it comes to pass,Capital becomes cold, heartless, cruel, merciless, tyrannical."</p>
        <p>I want to say right here that the above description of the capitalist of the Rockies and pretty near of the whole of the employing classes of the West is sketched to the life. They are, with few exceptions, a foul- mouthed mass of greediness, and were it not for the labour organizations the lot of the wutlxca. woulci 13G not one whit better than is that of the Russians to-day. On Friday, February 16th, 1906, three deputy sheriffs arrived in Denver, Colorado from Boise City, Idaho,with extradition papers for Messrs. Moyer, Haywood, Pettibone. On Saturday at midnight, and with the permission of Governor McDonald, the men were seized and placed in the county gaol. They were denied permission to notify friends or counsel of their arrest. While the friends and relatives of these men slept, the Pinkerton detectives had them board a special train and they were soon speeding towards Idaho. When these kidnapped men arrived in Idaho they were at once taken to the Penitentiary and not permitted to see anyone. The labour world was fairly staggered at the arrest of these men, and a mighty cry went up from all parts of the country protesting against the kidnapping of these men, for the only reason that they were leaders of a labour movement. That these men were kidnapped there is no doubt, no process of law was gone through, simply an under- standing between the two governors of the two states was sufficient to keep them in durance Vile for over sixteen months. The manager of the Pinkerton detectives, one McParland, said "they would hang." That was futough. His assertion that he had informa- tion and evidence sufficieat to hang these three men for atrocious murders, was mere bluff, or, as the result has proved, a downright lie. The leading features in the charge are, that Governor Steunenberg was assassinated on December 30th,1905. Six weeks after the three men were charged with complicity in this outrage. To-day, Moyer and Haywood are at liberty. Moyer is out on bail and Ilaywood was acquited, the jury saying that they found no connection between the murderer Harry Orchard and the labour leaders whom he charged in his confession with having paid him various sums to murder friend or foe, whoever they might he, who were opposed to the Western Federation of Miners. This Orchard proved himself a monumental liar as well as a wholesale murdensr. Most murderers get converted after their horrid work is done. So it was with Orchard. He says he never remembers having a conscience. This feature remained with him, and enabled him to impose upon his gaolers and others around him who insist pon it that his confession was the truth, and nothing but the truth. The jury decided otherwise, and this in brief is a sketch of what T. P. O'Connor calls " 4_merica's latter horror." There is a feature in this case of Haywo-)d's that must not be lost sight of. Throughout the whole of the labour ranks a strong impression runs that the employers from east to west are determined to break up unions. The employers first make it almost impossible for the workman to join a 4-,nion. If he succeeds in doing this they dis- charge him. Should the men manage to form their union and demand that their leaders retain their situations, and that their union be recognized, there is a conflict, and soldiers are hurried to the scene. Lurid reports of outrages begin to appear in the Press, the readers not knowing at the time that pretty well the whole of the Press of the United States is owned by the capitalists of Wall-street, who are also the owners of the mines, the said mines being gold mines. Before things have got to the point of conflict, the Pinkerton manager has put in an appearance, and his peculiar specimen of humanity, the labour spy, has got a job in the mine. Engaged as he is for his special qualities of being able to worm himself into the confidence of his fellow workmen he soon becomes an otrcial of the Union, and a daily record is sent to the employers of the doings and sayings of the men while at work and in the Lodge room. now the charge is male, and made with sufficient reason, that the employers were the instigators, through tb_c Pinkerton Detective Agency, of the whole of Oreliard',a murderous work, and only failed in their effort to hang Haywood by being unable to convince the jury that he was Orchard's paymaster. Horrid work this, is it not ? It is, and when we remember) that not in all mines do the men receive their wages, in coin, but, that the " tornmy shop" is in full swing it is still more horrid, and all to make millionaires. I send along with this communication a book, "The Pinkerton Spy" and dur friend John Workman" may say a word on the question. I may say that the Western Miners Federation is through the efforts of Haywood, a Socialist body. Of course the word " Anarchy " is in common use now and is always handy to throw like a bomb at the head of anyone who dares to lift his voice against Russianizing • A merica</p>
        <p>The gght is on, tud it is going to be a 6ght to Ow</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday August 31, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday August 31, 1907.</p>
        <p>death as the following shows:</p>
        <p>MINEOWNERS RENEW FIGHT ON UNION. MEMBERS OF WESTERN FEDERATION ARE BEING DISCHARGED AT CRIPPLE CREEK.</p>
        <p>CRIPPLE CREEK, August 6.—The acquittal of William D. Haywood, Secretary of the Western Federation of Miners, has been followed by what appears to be an effort on the part of the mineowners and operators of Cripple Creek to renew the war upon all members of the Western Federation. Last night the union men working on the Thompson and Humphrey leases were peremptorily discharged. It is stated that the Granite will let out a number of Federation men to-morrow, and that the other mines in the district will fall in line and drop such of their employees as are affiliated with the Federation.</p>
        <p>At largely attended meeting of the Cripple Creek Mine Owners' Association held in Colorado Springs, it was decided not to abolish the card system at Cripple Creek, but to enforce the Anti-Federation rule to the utmost. It is understood that this policy is not unanimously approved by all the members of the association, but those who favour an " open shop" are only a small minority, and were easily out-voted. Those who favoured the abolition of the obnoxious card system, or Anti-Federation policy, claim they cannot secure enough competent miners unless they employ union mtn.</p>
        <p>OVER THE WALL. Evading the Factory Inspector at Warrington. At the Borough Police Court, yesterday, the Warring- ton Wire Rope Works, Ltd., Bewsey-road, were sum- moned for committing breaches of the Factory Act by employing six young persons beyond the regulation hours.—Mr. Browne represented the defendants. Mr. Robert Eatock, Factory Inspector, said this was one of the worst cases of systematic disregard of the Act he had yet discovered, and it was not improved by the extraordinary precautionary methods adopted by the firm to avoid detection. The entrance gates of the factory were kept constantly locked owing to a secret process carried on there, and admittance was gained by the visitor ringing a bell and declaring his business. He visited the Factory on complaint on July Mr. Browne objected to this date being Introduced as it was not mentioned in the summons, and the Magistrates' Clerk upheld the objectiorf. Mr. Eatock continuing said that on July 25th, after taking the necessary measures to circumvent the peculiar tactics of the firm,— Mr. Browne: I object to that. We are bound to keep the doors locked by order of the Admiralty. Mr. Eatock: Those are not the peculiar tactics. The peculiar tactics are the smuggling out of the boys when the Inspector rings the bell, and that I can prove. I doti't object to the locking of the door. Continuing he said on July 25th he was admitted to the works after a short interval and found two young persons at work in the rope walk at eight o'clock at night: Four other boys who were working in another part of the factory got notice of his presence and escaped over the wall. Their names and addresses were obtained and the follow- ing day they made declarations. They were on the date in question being employed 24 hours beyond the legal time. They were all under 13 years of age. On looking through the time book he found illegal over- time had been carried on for some weeks, on as many as four nights in one week and even up to 10.30. Mr. Browne objected to this being mentioned and the Clerk also held that it should not be gone into. :ND% Eatock concluded by asking for a heavy penalty to be inflicted, in view of the exceptional circum- stances.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne for the defence said the Company manu- factured torpedo netting for the Admiralty and were under stringent regulations,the doors having to be kept locked to prevent enemies from entering and discover- ing the process. They had been exceedingly busy of late-11nd the boys were quite willing to work overtime for extra pay, especially as the work was of a light character. They were all nearly 18 years of age, one being 17 years and 10 months. The firm were at present in negotiation with the chief factory inspector of the Manchester District to see if a scheme could be devised to enable the boys to work extra time on certain evenings, as was allowed in continuous wire drawing. The offence was purely technical. Defendants were fined 10s. and costs in each case (£6 4s. in all).</p>
        <p>SOCIALISM AND TOWN LIFE. Address by Councillor Ball. The Warrington branch of the Independent Labour Party is conducting an active propaganda and meetings are to be held regularly at Bridge Foot. On Sunday evening a large crowd assembled to hear an- address by Mr.F.W.Potter,of Oldham,on "Carnegie and his millions. ' Mr. Reeves presided. On Monday evening another ; meeting was held, Councillors Stott and Ball being !! the speakers. I Councillor STOTT alluded to the rapid increase in membership of the Independent Labour Party, pointing out that the branches numbered 600 as against 250 three years ago. During the same period the number of direct representatives of their party on local authorities had increased from 300 to 850. Three years ago they had one representative in Parliament—Mr. Keir Hardie—but to-day they had seven, while the members of their organization in the House totalled about 22. They denied that Socialism meant robbery, slavery, or atheism or that they wanted to take the children away from their parents and have them reared in some large barracks at the expense of the State. Socialism would bring about the salva- tion of the workers and it was the only remedy for the present evil conditions. It had been suggested by some Liberal leaders—Dr. Macnamara in particular —that if the Liberal party was going to cope with the work of the Socialist organizations, the young Liberals would have to go out to the street corners and advocate the principles of Liberalism. He (Councillor Stott) only wished that Dr. Macnamara Or any other Liberal chief could influence the members of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association or Progres- sive Labour League to come out into the open air and tell the people what the principles of Liberalism were. The members of the LL.P. invited them to do as the Liberal leaders had suggested, for if Liberal principles were not worth advocating in that way they were not worth supporting. Councillor BALL delivered an address on "Socialist policy and town life." Socialists, he said, considered the present basis of society was entirely wrong. A system which was based upon competion—mere struggling one with another for the necessaries of life, like dirty dogs struggling over a bone—was not one which a society of human beings, educated and civilised, should tolerate for above one generation. The results of co.operation in many walks of life had shown that mutual helpfulness and mutual regard for each other's rights and privileges had proved in the long run far better ways and means of satisfying the common need than the competitive system. As regarded the ordinary Liberal or Tory who entered the Council, they would find that very often his politics did not enter into his methods of dealing with the town's affairs. That was because Liberals and Tories believed in the' present individualistic state of Society. They believed that by a certain amount of competition, each man seeking the best for himself, the general result would be the good of the greatest number. Socialists said, however, that the competitive system had resulted not in happiness, peace, quietness and comfort, but that on the contrary millions of their fellow beings were living under condi- tions that were entirely unfair. Socialists had a nobler programme. Councillor Ball then expounded Socialistic beliefs and asked why, if it had been found useful for the community to supply its own g,s, water Stc., the principle of common owner- ship should not be extended to many necessaries of life? They found in Warrington many houses that were unfit for human habitation, which had been condemned again and again by •the medical officer, but Owing to the , interests of certain property owners the houses still remained and were inhabited. 0113 of the great problems , of the day was how to get hold of the land necessary to help them to build proper human dwellings. Socialists s proposed to obtain control of the land—and in this they I were supported by many members of other political , parties—by the taxation of all unoccupied land so that I land should be brought into use,because they found very often that people who owned land in a town held it , until they could compel the community to pay a very ' heavy price. Councillor Ball instance.' a piece of land in Warrington which formerly brought in £BO a year 1 when used for agricultural purposes, and now when it I was occupied by works the rent received by the land- owner was about £BOO. Yet he was never taxEd on the increased value. The value of all land was generally about one third the total value of the community. The value of Warring- ton to-day was about £250,000 a year. Therefore ' £BO,OOO worth of land was going without proper taxa- tion according to its value. Socialists did not want to I run municipal undertakings primarily as profit making concerns. They should be established first for the convenience and good of the community. Socialists did not %vent the profits to go to the relief of the rites, becarse they could have a new source of income in the taxation of land values. Then again, instead of borrowing money from the bankers and paying them interest the Council should accumulate the profits and use them to buy up open spaces, provide bowling greens, &amp;c. Councillor Ball also emphasised the fact that when undertakings belonged to the community, good wages and conditions of employment could be, insisted upon. Socialists advocated that education should be free to everyone and also that the State should see that the children were properly fed and nourished, so as to be fit to receive the education. The outcry about the declining birth rate was all fudge. What was wanted was that the country should develop to the host of its power and capacity the children it already possessed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MONEY-LENDING TRANSACTIONS.</head>
        <p>MONEY-LENDING TRANSACTIONS.</p>
        <p>Peculiar Case at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., the Latchford Advance Office, 44, Old-road, Latchford, sued Frank James WhittlmMarquis of Granby Inn, Church-street, and William Woods, shoemaker, 55, Church-streat, for £5O balance of principal and interest due on a joint promissory note. Mr. Steel appeared for the plaintiffs and Mr. Browne for the defendant Woods.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said the defendants had signed a promissory note for £62 and a summons was issued under the Bills of Exchange Act, 1855. The defendant William Woods, however, obtained leave to defend from the Registrar on Monday on the ground that he did not know at the time that he was signing a promissory note. Samuel Burdett was then called and proved that the defendants signed the note in Mr. Whittle's public- house.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne: The Latchford Advance Office con- sisted of himself, his wife, son (tad daughter. He denied that when the promissory note was presented to Woods for signature it was folded up, leaving nothing visible but four blank lines. Ile did not know that Woods was "not worth fifty pence." He was satisfied with what Whittle said as to Woods's position. Woods read over a portion of the promissory note before he signed it. Whittle did not tell Woods in witness's presence that it was only,a " dummy "signature he wanted. The word" dummy was never used. What was your object in getting a man to sign a pro- missory note for £62 when he was only earnirrn.„ .C 1 a week ?—Mr. Whittle assured me he had a good house in Heywood and I took his word for it. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Witness maintained that the name and the address "23, Queen-street, Heywood," were written by Woods, although Mr. Browne suggested they were in different styles of handwriting. He admitted that from the time the note was signed (October 12th, 1906), until the summons was issued he had never made any demand verbally or in writing upon Woods. He was advised to take proceedings because there was a bilis, of sale gazetted against Whittle. The same night that lie served the summons he received a gold watch of the value of £l5 to £2O from Whittle.</p>
        <p>' Didn't 51r. Whittle tell you he had got the summonses on the.two Woods's who brought them to him imme- diately they were served ?—No. Didn't he get them because he had admitted to them that he bad got them to sign something and they did not know what they were signing ?—No, certainly not. . .</p>
        <p>And that he tho4ht he wo-uld -be able to pay the two promissory notes off before they became due ?—No. There was no mentiom of that.</p>
        <p>Didn't he say he only got their signatures because he thought he could pay the amounts without his family knowing ?—Nothing was mentioned about the signa- tftres at all.</p>
        <p>Assuming his Honour finds a verdict in your favour, what hope have you of getting a penny from these two poor old cobblers? His Honour: That is not material. _ •</p>
        <p>Further questioned, witness admitted that he paid to Whittle the sum of £5O, that he had taken a promissory note for £62, and that he had received £27 from Whittle in cash, leaving a balance of the principal of £35. And you charge the E xho rb i tan t interest of £l5 on £35 in five months P—Witness pointed opt that there' were fines for being behind with the payments. So that you only gave him £5O; you have drawn back £27 and now four or five months afterwards you want £5O again.—That is one condition of the promissory note. Mr. Browne: It is four or five hundred per cent. 137 Mr, Steel He had known Whittle for 25 years and had had various trarissotions with hint. The Woods's were only served with their summonses ten minutes before the other defendant, so that they had not the opportnnity of taking them to Whittle before witness saw him.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne addressed his Honour and pointed out that Woods was a working cobbler whom Mr. Burdett had known a great many years. Did his Honour believe for a moment that such a man would siga a promissory note for £O2? He saw no money pass and saw nothing on the paper but three blank lines. He k Ur. Browne) submitted that no money-lender in the world would have taken such a man for surety unless it was, as was suggested, to oblige Whittle with a "dummy " signature. Woods knew Mr. Burdett as a coin agent for Messrs. Richardson but not as a money lender. About twelve months ago he signed a paper under similar circum- stances, being under the impression that it was only required to attest payment of a coal account. Mr. Browne submitted that it was never the intention of the plaintiffs to .look to Woods for a single penny until ' the bill of sale was gazetted. If his Honour held against Woods he would ask leave to defend under the Money Lenders' Act of 1900, as this was undoubtedly a" harsh and unconscionable bargain," working out at 400 or 500 per cent. If his Honour thought the signa- ture was obtained by fraud or withholding on the part not only of Whittle but with the tacit consent of one of the plaintiffs, the action ought not to succeed. William Woods was then called and said he lodged at 6, General-ztreet, and was a cobbler employed by his brother Thomas at 55, Cnurch-street, next door to the Marquis of Granby. He had known Mr. Burdett for many years as an agent in the employ of Messrs. Richardson but had no idea he was a money-lender and had never heard of the Latchford Advance Office until the summons was served upon him. Ha remembered being called in from his work to the Marquis of Granby where he found Mr. Burdett and Mr. Whittle in the bar pirlour. Mr. Whittle, pointing to a piece of paper folded up, asked him to put his signature on one of tha blank lines, adding that it was only a dummy signature that was required. Witness complied with the request, thinking it had something to do with a coal bill. He heard nothing further of the matter until he was served with the summons. The Heywood address on the pro- missory note was not in witness's handwriting. His Honour: Certainly the address seems to be in a different handwriting. By Mr. Steel : Mr. Whittle tilled in the address. Witness did not see the word' " securities " near the lines set apart for signatures. He had not the chance to read anything ; it was done so suddenly. He con- tended that his signature was obtained under false pretences. If he had known it was a promissory note, he would not have signed. His Honour then heard a second case of a similar kind in which Thomas Woods, 55, Church-street, master shoemaker, and Frank James Whittle were sued for £5O on a promissory note for .244. Samuel Burdett proved the signing of the note by the defendants, and dented that it was folded. .By Mr. Browne : Witness asked Woods for his signature and told him he had better read the paper first. Woods looked at the heading and said to the other defendant : " You will see that it is right." Whittle said : " I will attend to it. He will not trouble you." Witness advanced Whittle £3l 4s. and took a promissory note for £44. He now sued for £5O, the sums of £9 16s. and £6 being charged for interest and fines in three mos. It was in accordance with the terms of the promisFory note. Thomas Woods was wen called and gave similar evidence to his brother, although he referred to a separate occasion. He knew what promissory notes were, as he had used them in his business. His Honour said the case was extremely simple. He I had given as much time as was reasonable to the con- sideration of any defence that might he suggested, but he did not complain of the time that had been occupied. Mr. Browne, acting on his instructions, had gone into tho matter very deeply, but the facts that remained, which were not disputed, were enough for him (his Honour) to decide the case' Both William and Thomas Woods admitted that the signatures to the notes were genuine. There was a question as to the address written upon I William Woods's paper but that was not of the smallest I consequence. The signatures were genuine and were not disputed. Both the Woods's set up the defence that I the promissory notes which they signed were so folded that they could not tell they were promissory notes. It was a very singular thine.' that Thomas Woods, who was a man of business and had used promissory notes, could not tell that the document was a promissory note. " Ant I made of wood" continued his Honour "or of flesh and blood ? Can I believe two men independently would go in and be trapped in that way? I cannot believe it. They knew they were signing something and they knew that that sometßing must be of use to somebody. If they thought at all, they must have known it was some transaction in which they might incur some liability somehow. I don't believe their story at all, Whit I believe is that these men, each of tima oe the geparate occasions, signed ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Our Football Annual and Sports Record is nearly sold out. Have you got a copy yet? By the way, there is a small correction we are asked to make. In our list of Secretaries' addresses the address of Mr. H. L. Clayton, secretary of Fairfield A.F.C., is given as 80, Orford- lane, whereas it should be 80, Padgate-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>There is not much information to impart with regard to the prospects of the Warrington men. Most of last season's players are fit and well and eager for the fray. Since our last issue the crowd at the practice games has had an opportunity of welcoming Berry and Kenyon back to the field of play. Both are useful men, and can render the Club good service if they are enthusiastic enough.</p>
        <p>Regret has been expressed at an injury already to this season's captain, T. Hockenhall, who damaged his leg the other day. I am informed that the injury is not very serious, and will only keep the popular half-back out of the field for a few weeks.</p>
        <p>Fish is said to be undergoing quiet training in a secluded retreat. Don't ask where ! But, "it's early to bed and early to rise," and if it makes Jack healthy and wise it will be the best training he has ever had.</p>
        <p>There will be another practice match at 6 p.m to-day.</p>
        <p>The Club " smoker" in Parr Hall to celebrate the cup victory of last April is to be held on Tuesday evening next, September 3rd. Members will have. their comforts attended to free of cost, and may also bring friends. An attractive programme has been arranged.</p>
        <p>The New Zealand team set sail last Saturday on board the Orton% and are due in England early in October. It is expected that the names of the players will be given to the public in a few days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>St. Helens, on Saturday afternoon, met a very power- ful Rest of League team on the Denton's Green ground. There was tremendous excitement through out the game, and when St. Helens won just on the stroke of time the enthusiasm was unbounded.</p>
        <p>At the interval the League Cup was presented to the captain of the St. Helens team by Colonel R. Pilking- ton, C.R., the president both of the League and of St. Helens Club. He congratulated St. Helens on winning the cup, and said the League had proved a most useful institution inasmuch that the clubs comprising it were composed almost entirely of amateurs, only one pro- sessional being allowed to each team. Professionalism in itself was all right, but was far more satisfactory for spectators to come and see a match between the best of their own people. St. Helens had done exceedinOy well, having secured 21 points out of a possible 24.-Ir. S. G. Urmson, the St. Helens captain, who was received with cheers, accepted the cup, and said they had only been defeated once, and that was by their neighbours—St. Helens Recreation— but that seemed to do them more good than harm. He thanked the team for the splendid way they had worked together. and concluded by calling for three cheera for Colonel Pilkington.</p>
        <p>In the game the Rest of League batted first and made 150. The presentation took up a good deal of time,and when Urmson, Turner, and Holden, the three best bats- men, were out at 18, it looked as if St. Helens were sure to be defeated easily. The younger members, however, came to the rescue. T. B. Rigby and W. L. Mercer gave the Saints a chance by beautiful cricket, which brought in 66 runs, and then Marsden scored freely. This player made the winning kit off the last ball but one of the match, and, amid much cheering, St. Helens retired winners, thus completing a glorious season's work.</p>
        <p>Warrington had a rest from cricket on Saturday, but they sent several of their representatives to take part in the match,Rest of the League v. St. Helens, and they gave a good account of themselves, T. Wright knocking up 27, W. EL Podmore 23, and J. Bibby 17. Cresswell, the professional, also took part, but he was stumped for 2.</p>
        <p>The great local event was the final for the Works Competition, in which Walker's Brewery and Fletcher Russells were opposed. Walkers are to be congratu- lated upon achieving such an easy victory. In the 50 minutes allotted to them they knocked up 96 for nine wickets and afterwards dismissed their opponents for 34. H. Cook was the most successful batsman, his innning realising 31. Dargan was in grand form with the ball, taking five wickets for six runs.</p>
        <p>At the close of the match Alderman Forshaw (presi- dent of the Warrington Club),in presenting gold medals to the winners and silver ones to the runners up, said that owing to the bad season they had not had such good gates, nut no would do all in his power to encourage that interesting game. Gold medals were presented to W. Bennett for the best bowling perform- ance—(seven wickets for a single run) presented by Mr. E. Pierpoint, and to W. Howard for the highest individual score in the works competition (68) presented by Mr. J. W. Bibby.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League, Cheshire Lines Engineers again placed a victory to their credit, the vanquished being Orford Barracks. Wilson was the only soldier to obtain double figures (17). Duckworth, Jordan, Brown and Elill batted well for the Engineers, for whom G. Chamberlain took five wickets for five runs.</p>
        <p>This win entitles the railway .men to medals. If the soldiers win their match against St. James' at the Barracks to-day it will mean that the Saints and the Engineers will be level with 20 points each. If so, a match will have to be played between the teams, to decide the championship of the League . St. James' however, will strain every nerve to prevent a reverse to-day. Saturday's match was the ninth successive win for the Engineers in League matches.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans went down before Mackie's on Saturday. For the Printers, C. Woodbead (18) was top scorer.</p>
        <p>Moore and Daresbury entertained Penketh and won easily. NV. Antrobus batted in grand form, making 33 not out. A Dutton bowled exceedingly well, taking 8 wickets for 7 runs.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall suffered a reverse at Barnton. They were quite unable to make headway against the deliveries of Dutton who captured 8 wickets for 7 runs.</p>
        <p>Oaklands met St. Elphin's and had no difficulty in defeating them. There was nothing remarkable about the scoring Stathers (11), being the only player who reached double figures.</p>
        <p>Warrington Thursday had no difficulty in disposing of the Warrington Cycle Club on Thursday. B. Lowe batted exceedingly well, making 67 not out. ‘Vells came next with a well-earned 26. Taylor did well with the ball, taking seven wickets for 63. Fo- the Cyclists T. Wright (17), U. Watkins (13) and J, Podmore (12) were the only ones to reach double figures.</p>
        <p>the club visited Burtonwood and Winwick. A smoking concert was held at the Swan Inn and proved a great success. An excellent programme of songs, recitations duets, ecc., was gone through.</p>
        <p>To-day's run will be to Bollington instead of Brereton as was announced on the fixture card.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>A considerable amount of interest was taken in the final for the Warrington District Association Champion- ship which was dicided at Heatley on Saturday. The well-known bowling champions, Bull's Head, met Rylands Brothers and after some exciting contests the former won by the narrow margin of W. Bull's Head were victorious in eight out of the twelve games and are to be , congratulated on their achievement. Praise is also due to Rylands Bros., for the splendid play which carried them into the final.</p>
        <p>The works competition is progressing rapidly and Saturday saw the close of the third round Crosfields No. 2, gained a victory at the expense of Penketh tannery and Sankey Wire Mills proved too strong for Wire ltopery. Crosflelds No. 1 succeeded in defeating Monks Hall No. 2 by the large majority of 35.</p>
        <p>The matches for to-day are as follors :—P. and K. v. Whitecross No. 1; Longford Wire v. Sankey Wire Mills; Crosfields No. 2 v. Walker's Brewery in which match Mr. G. Holbrook will referee.</p>
        <p>The prizes for the recent main will be presented at the Bank Quay Bowling Green on Monday evening. Congratulations to Mr. John Wareham, an old War- ringtonian on his success in winning the" Express" challenge cup, against 1,000 competitors, Mr. Wareham who was at one time a member of the Penketh Park Bowling club, was presented with a gold medal, his victory being a very popular one. The champion, who was seated on the shoulders of the friends, caused great merriment when he informed the people that he had not only laid himself out to win the cup, but that" if he lived they would not be able to take it from him."</p>
        <p>Cycling. Warrington Cycling Club journeyed to Little Leigh on Saturday, and the outing was very much enjoyed, Mr. Fieldhouse, the captain, being in command. On their arrival they indulged in a fishing match at Acton Grange, permission having been given by the War- rington Anglers' Association. The first prize went to Mr. J. B. Poole, who caught some fine roach. The second prize was awarded to Mr. A. G. Robertson, while the third and fourth prizes went to Messrs. F. Garnett and W. Ashton respectively.</p>
        <p>The final week day run took place on Tuesday when</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Warrington Athletic Club.</head>
        <p>Warrington Athletic Club.</p>
        <p>Tho members of the above club have been ex- ceptionally successful during the past three weeks as the following particulars will show Bennett, 2 firsts and 1 second; H. Mounttield, 2 firsts and 1 third; H. Jepson, 1 first, 2 seconds and 1 third; J. Bardsley, 1 first and 1 second; G. Watmough,l first; J.Houghton, 1 first and 1 second; J. Stewart, 2 firsts and 1 second ; B. Powell, 1 first and 1 third; J. Twiss, 1 first; S. Andrews, 1 first; J. Bibby, 1 second; W. Price, 1 second ; W. Kidd, 1 second ; J. Bramhall, 1 second ; T. Wilcock, 1 second; J. Moran, 4 thirds ; Total 13 firsts 11 seconds and 7 thirds. It is worthy of note that three members of this club hold individual championships, viz., 3. Watmough, Liverpool and Dis- trict Junior Cross-Country Championship; C. Berry One Mile N.C.U. Championship (Manchester Centre) ; also the Quarter Mile N.C.U. Championship (Liverpool Centre), which was won on Saturday last at Prescot by that dashing young rider B. Powell who has only during this season graduated from the novice class. The Club are also holders of the Liverpool and District Junior Championship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Love That Women Desire."</p>
        <p>This modern romance by G. Carlton Wallace tells the old story in a particularly fascinating way. Love at first sight is succeeded by the machinations of villainy to prevent a happy consummation, and matters are brought to such a crisis that the hero betakes himself to Brazil, survives the perils of the primeval forest,escapes assassination at the hands of the villain's emissaries by a hair's breadth, returns to the old country with untold wealth just in time to prevent his lady love from being tricked into matrimony with the villain and, of course, does what everyone expects by marrying her himself. The curtain descends as the bridal party enters the church amid joyous acclaims and to the strains of festal music. As may be gathered, the play abounds in incident and excitement. It is adequately presented and there is not a weak spot in the cast. Mr. John Davidson makes an ideal lover and hero and whether as the generous-hearted yeomaa farmer,alvvays willing to help the distressed, tha ardent lover declar- ing his passion, or the doggedly persevering explorer in a far-off land, he is equally happy. He has a pleasing voice and an easy stage presence and his portrayal has met with general acceptance. Miss Lalla Stanhope in the charming role of Sybil Thorburn achieves a notable success. She acts naturally, being dramatic when occasion demands, while the emotionalism is not over- done. Mr. J. 0 Stewart, as the unrelenting squire,Mr. Oliver Rogers as his unscrupulous son,and Miss Aimee- G-rattan-Clyndes,as the jealous and scheming daughter, have unpopular parts,but they rise to the occasion and infuse sufficient vigour into their representations to make them thoroughly realistic. The piece is by no means devoid of humour —in fact there is quite a wealth of it. Mr. J. W. Wilkinson, who plays the constable rejoicing in the euphonious name of' Sower- burry, is a host in himself and convulses the audience with his escapades in the " execution of his duty." Mr. John Hignett, as Dennis O'Rourke, makes a typical, happy-go-lucky Irishman ever ready for a joke, and Miss Winifred Barton is highiy amusing as the pert Pattie. The play is exceptionally well mounted, the forest scene presenting a unique and beautiful stage picture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>"THE WALLS OF JERICHO."</p>
        <p>An engagement of exceptional interest will be inaugurated at the Royal Court Theatre next week when Warrington playgoers will be afforded an oppor- tunity of seeing Mr. Alfred Sutro's marvellously successful society play "The Walls of Jericho " which has now been played to crowded houses in all parts of the English-speaking world for nearly three years. "The Walls of Jericho "was originally produced by Mr. Arthur Bourchier at the Garrick Theatre,London,•in October 1904,and ran there until the following October, when it was transferred to the Shaftesbury Theatre to make room for Mr. Bourchier's season a month ago. During this period" The Walls of Jericho" has been played by as many as four companies at a time in the English provinces, and it has also been an unqualified success in Australia, America, and South Africa, and eigni,eeu moucha ago Messrs. Dauucey and Leveaus took an English company to Holland which played the piece in En4lish in all the leading Royal and State Theatres of the Netherlands. This is the only case on record of a serious English play being produced in the original language in these theatres. The Company which is to play" The Walls of Jericho " in Warrington next week is one of unusual strength and ability, and will include Mr. Wilfred E. Payne, who has played the leading parts in "Lady Windermere's Fan," "A Woman of no Import- ance,"" Diplomacy," "The Freedom of Suzanne" etc., and also "Jack Frobisher" (the leading part in "The Walls of Jericho ") in all the leading theatres of South Africa previous to starting on the present tour. Miss Dora Hole, who plays the leading lady's part, has played important parts in London with Mr. Beerbohm Tree and Mr. Cyril Maude, has understudied Mrs. Forbes Robertson, and played promi- nent parts with Mr. Martin Harvey and Mr. F. R. Benson. Other members of this excellent company are Mi. E. Spencer Geach, who has played leading parts with Miss Kate Rorke, Mr. Edmund Tearle, and at the Court Theatre, London ; Mr. W. Kingsford Pearce who played Mr Weedon Grossmith's part in "The Duller" and was in Mr. Beerbohm Tree's company in "Oliver Twist " ; Mr. Charles Fancourt who has just finished playing Mr. Henry Ainley's part in "The Shulamite ;" Mr. Alexander Cassy who was recently at the Duke of York's Theatre, London, in "A Royal Family" ; Miss Margaret 'Murray who lately played at the Court and Duke of York's Theatres in London ; Miss Margaret Nybloc who played the leading parts in" Little Mary" "Merely Mary Ann "and" Quality Street " ; and Miss Elsie H. Ross who comes here direct from understudying leading parts at the Haymarket Theatre, London. From such an unusually talented and experienced company of London actors and actresses local playgoers may confi- dently count on a perfect representation of Mr. Sutro's great play, which will be produced with special scenery and effects which are duplicates of those used at the .Garrick Theatre. Dealing, as "The Walls of Jericho " does, with" The Smart Set" of modern society, the ladies' dresses are a striking feature of this production, and represent the last word of Dame Fashion on the modes of the moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PLOT OF THE PLAY.</head>
        <p>THE PLOT OF THE PLAY.</p>
        <p>Jack Frobisher,a Queensland squatter who has con- trived to amass a huge fortune by sheep-farming— living the free life of primitive man, learning his lessons and winning his experience from nature—comes to London and mixes at once with "The Smart Set." and among the first things he does is to marry Lady Alethea, the daughter of the impecunious and self- seeking Marquis Steventon. Lady Alethea is of frivolous temperament .and neglects her child and household duties, and Jack Frobisher, with charming docility, follows his wife from house to house and yawns through wearisome functions. Out in Queens- land he was held to be a strong man, and had gained for himself the name of " Fighting Jack," but in London he becomes merely Lady Alethea's husband—the accommodating purse-holder of the Marquis of Steventon's handsome daughter. Dashing young Harry Dallas is moved to offer equivocal homage to Lady Alethea, but in her heart of hearts there dwells an honest love for her husband. In his stupid blundering way Jack comes near to giving this its death-blow. His wife's young brother Max has taken a fancy to the young lady holding the position of " companion " in Lord Steventon's household—with disastrous results—and Jack insists that the lad shall marry the girl. Against such a proceeding the family pride is at once up in arras, and Jack's persistence only makes Alethea the more angry. Hankey Bannister, one of Jack's earliest and closest friends, now appears on the scene. He too has become a millionaire, and he falls in love with Lady Lucy, Alethea's young sister who—though her affections urge her to accept her cousin Wilfred—cynically announces her intention to spread a net for the capture of her unsophisticated suitor. So once more it falls to Jack's lot to arouse his wife's wrath by opening his friend's eyes to the truth.</p>
        <p>_ At last comes the catastrophe that leads to the final rupture between the two. Carried away by a sudden access of passion, Harry Dallis reveals himself in his true colours, and Lady Alethea, to her horror, finds her- self constrained in his frenzied embrace. Entering unexpectedly, Jask is witness of more than enough to justify his suspicions. In his anger he admits of no compromise. They must go back to Queensland at once. Alethea refuses to see the necessity for this and refuses to accompany him, but a reconciliation is effected through the love they both have for their little son, and together they " go back to Nature,to mix with real men and women."</p>
        <p>"The Walls of Jericho" has been an immense success in all parts of the world. It is as if a massive mirror were erected on the stage in which one may see the members of "The Smart Set "reproduced with mar- vellous fidelity—flirting, playing at inevitable bridge, and tearing the reputations of their absent friends to shreds and tatters. In fact it is 4` Vanity Fair "over again.</p>
      </div>
