        <p>these notes and knew perfectly well what they were." Continuing he said a man could tell a promissory note at a glance. Defendants said they were told that what was wanted was a dummy signature. That was another part of their evidence which he did not believe, because he could not, try as he might. What could be the meaning of their giving a dummy signature? A signature must mean something. How then could it be a dummy signature and what good would a dummy signature be to anybody? Even if . defendants thought it was a coal transaction they must have known it involved liability. This was the clearest possible caselt was said that the Woods's were cobblers and earned only a small amount. The money-lender, however, said he put certain questions to the publican and believed his representations about the men. Mr. Browne held it was manifest that the money-lender never expected to get a penny from the men. If so, why did he want their signatures? The whole case was manifestly undefended. If the men were such fools as to sign documents of that kind without due considera- tion, they were responsible for it. There was not a rag of defence in the cases and there must be judgment for the plaintiffs with costs. • Mr. Browne asked for leave to plead the Money Lenders' Act. His Honour : I see no reason whatever to interfere in this matter. Mr. Browne: Four or five hundred per cent! Will you find it is not a harsh and unconscionable transaction? His Honour: I simply say I decline to give any remedy or redress under the Money Lenders' Act in this case. At the request of Mr. Steel his Honour entered formal judgment against Frank J. Whittle as well as against the other defendants. He made no order as to how the money was to be paid.</p>
